    "global index": "ArcMMLU_62",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose the following three 8-bit bytes: 01010101, 01110000, 01001100. What’s the 1s complement of the sum of these 8-bit bytes? ( )\n\nOptions: \nA. 00010001\nB. 11101101\nC. 00010010\nD. 10001000\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "First, add the three bytes as integers:  \n01010101₂ = 85,  \n01110000₂ = 112,  \n01001100₂ = 76.  \nSum = 85 + 112 + 76 = 273.  \n\nIn 8-bit binary, 273 mod 256 = 17 = 00010001₂.  \nThe 1s complement of 00010001 is 11101110, but that’s not an option — so they likely mean “1s complement of the 8-bit sum” of the bytes computed in binary addition, ignoring final carry, then take 1s complement.  \n\nLet’s do binary addition directly:  \n01010101 + 01110000 = 11000101,  \n11000101 + 01001100 = 00010001 (carry 1 discarded in 8-bit).  \n1s complement of 00010001 is 11101110 — still not in options.  \n\nBut if they meant “Internet checksum style”: add all, add carry to sum, then 1s complement”:  \nSum = 273 → 256 + 17 → in 8-bit: 00010001, add carry 1 → 00010010,  \n1s complement of 00010010 = 11101101, which is option B.  \n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{B}",
