      "generated_answer": "Here’s an executable Python function that solves the problem:  \n\n```python\ndef can_form_2x2_square(grid):\n    # All possible top-left corners of a 2x2 square in a 3x3 grid\n    positions = [(0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0), (1, 1)]\n    \n    for r, c in positions:\n        # Extract the 2x2 square values\n        cells = [grid[r][c], grid[r][c+1], grid[r+1][c], grid[r+1][c+1]]\n        \n        # Count colors in this 2x2 block\n        white_count = cells.count('W')\n        black_count = cells.count('B')\n        \n        # If all same color\n        if white_count == 4 or black_count == 4:\n            return True\n        \n        # If three of one color and one of the other, we can change one cell\n        if (white_count == 3 and black_count == 1) or (black_count == 3 and white_count == 1):\n            return True\n    \n    return False\n```",
