    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given `N` giants, each with a shoulder height `A_i` and head height `B_i`. We want to stack them one on top of another, so that each giant (after the first) stands on the shoulders of the previous one.\n\nOur goal is to **maximize the final head height of the topmost giant**, by choosing the best permutation of giants.\n\n---\n\n### Key Insight:\nLet’s say we stack giants in order $ P_1, P_2, \\dots, P_N $. Then:\n\n- The head height of the topmost giant (giant $P_N$) is:\n  $$\n  A_{P_1} + A_{P_2} + \\cdots + A_{P_{N-1}} + B_{P_N}\n  $$\n  Why?\n  - The base giant contributes its full body to the stack.\n  - Each giant below the top contributes their **shoulder-to-shoulder height**, which is $A_i$, because the next giant stands on their shoulders.\n  - The top giant contributes their **full height from ground**, but note: their shoulder is placed at the top of the previous giant's shoulders, so their **head** appears at:\n    $$\n    (\\text{sum of previous } A_i) + B_{P_N}\n    $$\n\nSo the total head height of the top giant is:\n$$\n\\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{N-1} A_{P_i} \\right) + B_{P_N}\n$$\n\nHence, the **order of the first N-1 giants only matters through the sum of their A_i**, and the **last giant contributes B_i instead of A_i**.\n\nSo we can think of it as:\n- Choose one giant to be on top (call it `j`), which gives us extra: $ B_j - A_j $\n- All others contribute $ A_i $\n- Then total height = $ \\sum_{i=1}^N A_i + (B_j - A_j) $\n\nTherefore:\n$$\n\\text{Maximum height} = \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^N A_i \\right) + \\max_i (B_i - A_i)\n$$\n\nBut wait — **is that correct**?\n\nLet’s test with Sample 1:\n```\nGiant 1: A=4, B=10 → B-A=6\nGiant 2: A=5, B=8 → B-A=3\nGiant 3: A=2, B=9 → B-A=7\n\nSum of all A_i = 4+5+2 = 11\nMax(B_i - A_i) = 7\nTotal = 11 + 7 = 18 → matches\n```\n\nSample 2:\n```\nAll A_i = 1, B_i = 1 → B_i - A_i = 0\nSum A_i = 5\nMax diff = 0\nTotal = 5 → matches\n```\n\nSample 3:\nWe need to compute sum of A_i and max(B_i - A_i)\n\nLet’s compute one:\n- Giant 1: 690830957, 868532399 → diff = 177701442\n- Giant 2: 741145463, 930111470 → diff = 188966007\n- ...\nWe can just compute in code.\n\nSo the algorithm:\n\n1. Read N\n2. Read N pairs (A_i, B_i)\n3. Compute total_A = sum(A_i for all i)\n4. Compute max_extra = max(B_i - A_i for all i)\n5. Answer = total_A + max_extra\n\n---\n\n### Why not put someone with higher B_i on top?\nBecause all others contribute only A_i. So if we choose giant `i` on top, total height is:\n$$\n\\sum_{j \\ne i} A_j + B_i = \\left( \\sum_{j=1}^N A_j \\right) - A_i + B_i = \\sum A_j + (B_i - A_i)\n$$\nSo yes, we just need to maximize $ B_i - A_i $\n\n---\n\n### Code:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    n = int(data[0])\n    giants = []\n    index = 1\n    total_A = 0\n    max_diff = 0\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        a = int(data[index])\n        b = int(data[index + 1])\n        index += 2\n        giants.append((a, b))\n        total_A += a\n        max_diff = max(max_diff, b - a)\n    \n    result = total_A + max_diff\n    print(result)\n\nsolve()\n```",
