      "generated_answer": "We are given an undirected graph with two connected components:\n\n- The first component consists of vertices $1$ to $N_1$, and it's **guaranteed to be connected**.\n- The second component consists of vertices $N_1+1$ to $N_1+N_2$, and it's also **guaranteed to be connected**.\n- Vertex $1$ and vertex $N_1+N_2$ are **disconnected** initially (because the two components are separate).\n- We must **add exactly one edge** between a vertex $u \\in [1, N_1]$ and a vertex $v \\in [N_1+1, N_1+N_2]$.\n- After adding this edge, the entire graph becomes connected (since the two components are now joined).\n- Let $d$ be the **shortest path distance** from vertex $1$ to vertex $N_1+N_2$ in the new graph.\n- Our goal: **maximize** this shortest distance $d$ over all possible edges we can add.\n\n---\n\n### Key Idea:\n\nAfter we add an edge $(u, v)$, a path from $1$ to $N_1+N_2$ can go:\n\n1. $1 \\to u$ (within first component),\n2. $u \\to v$ (the new edge),\n3. $v \\to N_1+N_2$ (within second component).\n\nSo the total distance is:\n$$\n\\text{dist}_1(1, u) + 1 + \\text{dist}_2(v, N_1+N_2)\n$$\n\nWe want to **maximize** this total distance, by choosing appropriate $u \\in [1, N_1]$, $v \\in [N_1+1, N_1+N_2]$.\n\nLet:\n- $d1[u] = $ shortest distance from vertex $1$ to vertex $u$ (for $u \\in [1, N_1]$)\n- $d2[v] = $ shortest distance from vertex $v$ to vertex $N_1+N_2$ (for $v \\in [N_1+1, N_1+N_2]$)\n\nThen for any $u \\in [1, N_1]$, $v \\in [N_1+1, N_1+N_2]$, the total distance is:\n$$\n\\text{distance} = d1[u] + 1 + d2[v]\n$$\n\nWe want the maximum possible such distance.\n\nSo the answer is:\n$$\n\\max_{u \\in [1,N_1], v \\in [N_1+1,N_1+N_2]} (d1[u] + d2[v] + 1)\n= \\left(\\max_{u \\in [1,N_1]} d1[u]\\right) + \\left(\\max_{v \\in [N_1+1,N_1+N_2]} d2[v]\\right) + 1\n$$\n\nBut **wait**: is that correct?\n\nActually, **no restriction** prevents us from choosing any $u$ and $v$, so **we can independently** choose the $u$ that maximizes $d1[u]$ and the $v$ that maximizes $d2[v]$.\n\nThus, the answer is:\n$$\n\\max(d1) + \\max(d2) + 1\n$$\n\nBut we must compute:\n\n- $d1$: shortest distances from vertex $1$ to all vertices in component 1 (vertices $1$ to $N_1$)\n- $d2$: shortest distances from vertex $N_1+N_2$ to all vertices in component 2 (vertices $N_1+1$ to $N_1+N_2$)\n\nThen take the max in each group, sum, add 1.\n\nWe can use **BFS** since all edges are unweighted.\n\n---\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. Read input: $N_1, N_2, M$\n2. Total vertices: $N = N_1 + N_2$\n3. Build graph from $M$ edges\n4. Extract subgraphs (but we don't need to explicitly separate; just run BFS on relevant components)\n5. Run BFS from vertex $1$, but only among vertices $1$ to $N_1$ (though the graph may have edges to other vertices, we are only interested in distances within the first component — but note: the first component is fully connected among $1..N_1$, but may have edges going out? The input constraints say that **all pairs in $1..N_1$ are connected**, and similarly for the second. But edges may exist between components? **Yes**, the input doesn't forbid that, but the condition \"vertex 1 and vertex $N_1+N_2$ are disconnected\" suggests that there's no path — so **no edge or path between the two components currently**.\n\nSo the graph is split into two connected components: $[1, N_1]$ and $[N_1+1, N_1+N_2]$, no edge between them.\n\nBut note: input says $a_i \\leq b_i$, and values up to $N_1+N_2$, but we are guaranteed connectivity within each block and that $1$ and $N_1+N_2$ are disconnected.\n\nSo we can:\n\n- Run BFS from vertex 1, but only over the first component (ignore vertices > N1 after the fact)\n- Run BFS from vertex $N_1+N_2$, over the second component\n\nBut the graph might have edges that connect first and second component? The initial condition says vertex 1 and $N_1+N_2$ are disconnected, so no path — so even if there are edges between components, they must not connect the blocks? But the connectivity condition says all vertices in $1..N_1$ are connected — so if there were an edge from first to second, the two components would merge unless all such edges are missing.\n\nSo in the input, there are **no edges** between $[1, N_1]$ and $[N_1+1, N_1+N_2]$ — otherwise the two big components would be connected.\n\nHence, we can safely run BFS on the full graph, but we only care about the distances in each connected component.\n\n---\n\n### Implementation:\n\n1. Parse input.\n2. Build adjacency list.\n3. Use BFS from node `1` (index 0 if 1-indexed), but only consider nodes <= N1? Not exactly — we want distances in the first component. Since there's no connection to second component, BFS from 1 will only reach nodes in first component.\n\nBut wait: what if there is an edge from first to second? Then the components would merge. But we're told $1$ and $N_1+N_2$ are disconnected — so such edges **must not exist**.\n\nSo: the graph is two separate connected components.\n\nWe can:\n\n- Run BFS from 1 → get all distances in first component (vertices 1 to N1)\n- Run BFS from $N_1+N_2$ → get all distances in second component\n\nBut we only care about vertices in each component.\n\nSo:\n\n- Let `dist1[i]` = shortest distance from 1 to i, for i in 1..N1\n- Let `dist2[i]` = shortest distance from $N_1+N_2$ to i, for i in N1+1..N1+N2\n\nThen:\n- `max1 = max(dist1[1..N1])`\n- `max2 = max(dist2[N1+1..N1+N2])`\n\nAnswer: `max1 + max2 + 1`\n\n---\n\n### Example Check:\n\n**Sample Input 1:**\n```\n3 4 6\n1 2\n2 3\n4 5\n4 6\n1 3\n6 7\n```\n\nTotal vertices: 7 (N1=3, N2=4)\n\nComponent 1: vertices 1,2,3\n- Edges: (1-2), (2-3), (1-3) → triangle\n\nBFS from 1:\n- 1: 0\n- 2: 1\n- 3: 1\n→ max1 = 1\n\nComponent 2: vertices 4,5,6,7\n- Edges: (4-5), (4-6), (6-7)\n\nSo graph:\n- 4--5\n  \\\n   6--7\n\nBFS from 7 (since N1+N2=7):\n\n- 7:0\n- 6:1\n- 4:2 (via 6)\n- 5:3 (4→5)\n\nSo:\n- dist2[4]=2, dist2[5]=3, dist2[6]=1, dist2[7]=0 → max2 = 3\n\nAnswer = 1 + 3 + 1 = 5 → matches.\n\nBut wait: why is 5 at distance 3 from 7?\n\n7 → 6 → 4 → 5: yes, 3 edges.\n\nSo `dist2[5] = 3`\n\nIf we connect u=2 (dist1=1) and v=5 (dist2=3), then path:\n\n1 → 2 → 5 → 4 → 6 → 7: wait, that's 1-2 (1), 2-5 (1), 5-4 (1), 4-6 (1), 6-7 (1) → total 5 edges.\n\nBut shortest path might be shorter?\n\nBut in the new graph:\n\n1 → 2 → 5 → 4 → 6 → 7: 5 edges.\n\nIs there a shorter path?\n\n1 → 3 → 2 → 5 → 6 → 7: same.\n\nOr 1 → 2 → 5 → 4 → 6 → 7.\n\nBut from 5, must go to 4, then to 6, then to 7. So yes, that's shortest.\n\nBut what about:\n\n1 → 2 → 5 → 4 → 6 → 7: 5 edges.\n\nIs it possible to get longer than 5?\n\nSuppose we pick u=3 (dist1=1), v=4 (dist2=2): total = 1+1+2 = 4 < 5.\n\nu=1 (dist1=0), v=5 (dist2=3): 0+1+3=4.\n\nSo maximum is 5.\n\nSo algorithm is correct.\n\n---\n\nNow, let's code accordingly.\n\nWe assume:\n\n- Vertices are 1-indexed\n- We build graph with bidirectional edges\n- Run BFS from node 1 to get distances to all nodes in first component (nodes 1 to N1, but BFS will only stay in first component since no connection to second)\n- Run BFS from node $N = N1+N2$ to get distances in second component\n- Extract max in each, add 1\n\nBut wait: what if during BFS from 1, we accidentally reach second component? We are guaranteed that 1 and $N1+N2$ are disconnected, so BFS from 1 will not reach second component.\n\nSo no problem.\n\nLet’s write the code accordingly.\n\nNote: We need to do BFS on large graphs (up to 300,000 edges, 300,000 nodes)\n\nWe'll use collections.deque.\n\n---\n\n### Code:\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    # Parse first three values: N1, N2, M\n    n1 = int(data[0])\n    n2 = int(data[1])\n    m = int(data[2])\n    \n    n = n1 + n2\n    graph = [[] for _ in range(n + 1)]  # 1-indexed, index 0 unused\n    \n    index = 3\n    for _ in range(m):\n        a = int(data[index])\n        b = int(data[index + 1])\n        index += 2\n        graph[a].append(b)\n        graph[b].append(a)\n    \n    # BFS from vertex 1 to get distances in first component\n    dist1 = [-1] * (n + 1)\n    q = deque()\n    q.append(1)\n    dist1[1] = 0\n    \n    while q:\n        u = q.popleft()\n        for v in graph[u]:\n            if dist1[v] == -1:\n                dist1[v] = dist1[u] + 1\n                q.append(v)\n    \n    # BFS from vertex n (N1+N2) to get distances in second component\n    dist2 = [-1] * (n + 1)\n    q = deque()\n    goal = n\n    q.append(goal)\n    dist2[goal] = 0\n    \n    while q:\n        u = q.popleft()\n        for v in graph[u]:\n            if dist2[v] == -1:\n                dist2[v] = dist2[u] + 1\n                q.append(v)\n    \n    # Find max distance in first component (among vertices 1 to n1)\n    max1 = 0\n    for i in range(1, n1 + 1):\n        if dist1[i] > max1:\n            max1 = dist1[i]\n    \n    # Find max distance in second component (among vertices n1+1 to n1+n2)\n    max2 = 0\n    for i in range(n1 + 1, n + 1):\n        if dist2[i] > max2:\n            max2 = dist2[i]\n    \n    answer = max1 + max2 + 1\n    print(answer)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nLet’s test with sample 1:\n\n- First BFS from 1: reaches 1(0), 2(1), 3(1) → max1 = 1\n- Second BFS from 7: reaches 7(0), 6(1), 4(2), 5(3) → max2 = 3\n- Answer = 1 + 3 + 1 = 5 → correct.\n\nSample Input 2:\n\n```\n7 5 20\n10 11\n4 5\n10 12\n1 2\n1 5\n5 6\n2 4\n3 5\n9 10\n2 5\n1 4\n11 12\n9 12\n8 9\n5 7\n3 7\n3 6\n3 4\n8 12\n9 11\n```\n\nn1 = 7, n2 = 5 → total vertices = 12\n\nComponent 1: vertices 1 to 7  \nComponent 2: vertices 8 to 12\n\nWe need to run BFS from 1 → get distances for 1..7  \nThen BFS from 12 → get distances for 8..12\n\nThen max1 = max distance from 1 to any node in 1..7  \nmax2 = max distance from 12 to any node in 8..12\n\nThen answer = max1 + max2 + 1\n\nLet’s roughly simulate:\n\nFirst, build graph from edges.\n\nImportant edges in first component (1..7):\n\n1-2,1-5,1-4,2-4,2-5,3-5,5-6,5-7,3-7,3-6,3-4,4-5\n\nThis is a dense graph — likely diameter is small.\n\nLet’s do BFS from 1:\n\n- 1:0\n- 2,4,5:1\n- from 2: already have\n- from 4: →3? (if 4-3 exists) → yes, edge 3-4\n- from 5: →3,6,7\nSo 3,6,7: distance 2\n\nSo all nodes reached in at most 2 steps → max1 = 2\n\nSecond component: 8,9,10,11,12\n\nEdges:\n- 10-11\n- 10-12\n- 9-10\n- 11-12\n- 9-12\n- 8-9\n- 8-12\n- 9-11\n\nLet’s do BFS from 12:\n\n- 12:0\n- 10 (12-10), 11 (12-11), 9 (12-9), 8 (12-8): all distance 1\n- From 10: 9 (already), 11 (already)\n- From 9: 8 (already), 10,11 — all covered\n- From 8: 9 (already)\n\nSo all nodes are at distance 1 from 12 → max2 = 1\n\nThen answer = 2 + 1 + 1 = 4 → matches sample.\n\nBut wait, is there any node at distance 2 in second component?\n\nNo: 12 is connected directly to 8,9,10,11 → so maximum distance from 12 is 1.\n\nSo max2 = 1.\n\nHence, answer = 4.\n\nSo code is correct.\n\nLet me run the code on the sample to be sure, but logic holds.\n\nFinal code:\n\nWe just need to make sure we consider **only vertices in the respective components** when taking max — but since the BFS from 1 won't reach second component (and vice versa), the distances in dist1 for nodes > n1 will be -1 (but we don’t use them). Similarly for dist2.\n\nBut we only iterate from 1 to n1 and from n1+1 to n, so safe.\n\nHere is the final version:\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n1 = int(data[0])\n    n2 = int(data[1])\n    m = int(data[2])\n    n = n1 + n2\n    graph = [[] for _ in range(n+1)]\n    \n    idx = 3\n    for _ in range(m):\n        a = int(data[idx])\n        b = int(data[idx+1])\n        idx += 2\n        graph[a].append(b)\n        graph[b].append(a)\n    \n    # BFS from vertex 1\n    dist1 = [-1] * (n+1)\n    qq = deque([1])\n    dist1[1] = 0\n    while qq:\n        u = qq.popleft()\n        for v in graph[u]:\n            if dist1[v] == -1:\n                dist1[v] = dist1[u] + 1\n                qq.append(v)\n    \n    # BFS from vertex n (N1+N2)\n    dist2 = [-1] * (n+1)\n    qq = deque([n])\n    dist2[n] = 0\n    while qq:\n        u = qq.popleft()\n        for v in graph[u]:\n            if dist2[v] == -1:\n                dist2[v] = dist2[u] + 1\n                qq.append(v)\n    \n    max1 = max(dist1[i] for i in range(1, n1+1))\n    max2 = max(dist2[i] for i in range(n1+1, n+1))\n    \n    print(max1 + max2 + 1)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n```",
