# But we can choose which characters to remove.\n        # Optimal strategy: \n        # - If we have too many odd-frequency letters, we'd prefer to remove one from an odd-frequency letter\n        #   to turn it even (reducing odd_count by 1)\n        \n        # However, we don't need to simulate all possibilities.\n        # We can try to compute the minimum number of odd-frequency letters we can achieve after k removals.\n        \n        # With k removals, we can reduce the odd_count by at most k (if we always remove from odd-frequency chars),\n        # but also note that removing from even-frequency chars will increase odd_count.\n        # So optimal is to always remove from odd-frequency chars when possible.\n        \n        # But here's a key insight:\n        # We want to achieve odd_count <= 1 in the final string.\n        # Let x be the final number of characters with odd frequency.\n        # We want x <= 1.\n        \n        # The number of odd-frequency letters changes by ±1 with each removal.\n        # So the parity of odd_count flips with each removal? Actually, no:\n        # - Removing from an odd-frequency char: odd_count decreases by 1, but the count becomes even\n        #   so net change: -1\n        # - Removing from an even-frequency char: odd_count increases by 1, because even->odd\n        #   so net change: +1\n        \n        # But we can choose which one to remove.\n        # So we can decrease odd_count if there is any odd-frequency char available to remove from,\n        # otherwise we must increase it.\n        \n        # However, we don't need to simulate step-by-step.\n        # After k removals, the minimum possible odd_count is:\n        # - We can reduce odd_count by up to k, but we can't go below 0\n        # - BUT: when odd_count is 0, we can only increase it by removing from any char (since all are even)\n        # - So we need to calculate reachable odd_count after k operations.\n        \n        # Alternate approa