      "generated_answer": "We are given a tournament with `N` players, initially each in their own team. There are `N-1` matches. In each match, two teams are merged, and the winner is decided probabilistically based on the **sizes** of the teams.\n\nFor each player `i`, we are to compute the **expected number of matches won by the team that contains player `i`**, over the entire tournament. The result must be printed **modulo 998244353**.\n\n---\n\n### Key Observations\n\nLet’s analyze what happens:\n\n- Each match combines two teams.\n- The probability that a team wins a match is proportional to its size.\n- Each match contributes 1 win to the winner’s team members.\n- The key idea: each player’s expected number of wins is the **sum over all matches** of the probability that the **team containing that player wins that match**.\n\nBut note: once teams merge, a player stays in the current team. So, we can think **backwards**: we simulate the entire tournament, tracking team merges and computing the contribution of each match to each player’s expectation.\n\nHowever, directly simulating the probabilities for each player in each match is infeasible due to the large `N`.\n\nInstead, we can use **union-find with tracking of team size and expected contributions**, and **linearity of expectation**.\n\n---\n\n### Insight:\n\nLet’s define:\n- For each match `i`, suppose the two teams have sizes `a` and `b`.\n- Then the probability that the first (size `a`) team wins is `a / (a + b)`.\n- The probability that the second (size `b`) team wins is `b / (a + b)`.\n\nNow, **every player in the first team** gets a **+ `a/(a+b)`** to their expected win count from this match.\nSimilarly, **every player in the second team** gets a **+ `b/(a+b)`**.\n\nSo, **for each match**, we can add this contribution to **all players** in the respective teams.\n\nBut we have up to 200,000 players and 200,000 matches — we can’t update every player in a team each time (that could be O(N²)).\n\nInstead, we can **use union-find with lazy propagation or delayed updates**, or alternatively use **DSU with \"contributions\" stored per component**, but we need to assign contributions per player.\n\nAlternative idea:\n\nUse **DSU to simulate the tournament**, and for **each match**, compute the contribution and **add it to all players currently in the team**.\n\nBut updating every player in the team is too slow.\n\nBetter idea: use **inversion via DFS or tree-like propagation**, but here’s a better approach:\n\nWe can **process each match**, and **add the expected contribution from that match to every player in the respective teams**.\n\nBut we can't iterate over every player in each team every time.\n\nWait — **each player starts alone**, and **teams only grow**. Each player belongs to exactly one team at a time.\n\nWe can use a DSU that tracks:\n- `size[i]`: the size of the component (team) of node `i`\n- `contribution[i]`: accumulated expected win value for player `i`\n\nBut the catch: each match contributes to all members of the two teams.\n\nSo when a match happens between two teams of size `a` and `b`:\n- Each member of the first team gains `a / (a + b)`\n- Each member of the second team gains `b / (a + b)`\n\nWe can precompute modular inverses and keep an array `E[i]` for player `i`.\n\nBut the issue: updating `a` players and `b` players takes `O(a + b)` per operation → total `O(N²)` worst-case.\n\nBut note: `N ≤ 2e5`, and total number of elements merged is `O(N)`, so **if we use union-find with small-to-large merging**, we can update each player once per merge they are involved in, and the total complexity becomes `O(N log N)`.\n\nThat is acceptable.\n\n---\n\n### Plan:\n\nWe simulate the tournament in order.\n\nWe maintain:\n- `parent[i]`: for union-find (not strictly needed, we can use a list of components)\n- `team[i]`: the set of players in the team of player `i`? → too expensive\n\nInstead, we maintain:\n- `component[i]`: pointer to a component (if we use DSU with tracking), or\n- use dictionaries: map root → list of players? That’s expensive.\n\nBetter: we use **DSU with small-to-large merging**, and for each component, we maintain:\n- `size[r]`: number of players in component with root `r`\n- `members[r]`: list of players (or simply, we are going to update them when match happens)\n\nBut storing full list may be `O(N)` per merge, and total cost is `O(N log N)` due to small-to-large.\n\nWe also keep an array `ans[i]` for expected value of player `i`.\n\nFor each match `(p, q)`:\n- Find the current root/component of `p` and `q`\n- Let `a = size[root_p]`, `b = size[root_q]`\n- Then:\n  - For each player in component of `p`, add `a / (a + b)`\n  - For each player in component of `q`, add `b / (a + b)`\n- Then merge: combine the two components, update size and members\n\nBut the division is modular: we need `a * inv(a + b) mod 998244353`, etc.\n\nWe need modular arithmetic.\n\nDefine:\n```python\nMOD = 998244353\n```\n\nWe can use `pow(x, MOD-2, MOD)` for modular inverse.\n\n---\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. Initialize:\n   - `ans = [0] * (N+1)`  (1-indexed)\n   - `parent = list(range(N+1))`\n   - `size = [1] * (N+1)`\n   - `members[i] = [i]` for each i\n\n2. For each match `(p, q)`:\n   - Find root of `p`: `rp`\n   - Find root of `q`: `rq`\n   - Get `a = size[rp]`, `b = size[rq]`\n   - Compute `prob_p = a * inv(a + b) mod MOD`\n   - Compute `prob_q = b * inv(a + b) mod MOD`\n   - Add `prob_p` to every player in `members[rp]`\n   - Add `prob_q` to every player in `members[rq]`\n   - Merge: pick new root (say, attach smaller to larger), update size and members\n\n3. Use union by size and small-to-large merging: merge smaller component into larger one.\n\nBut note: the matches are given in order. We must process in that order.\n\nWe cannot use path compression because we are updating parent pointers and merging arbitrarily? But we can use union-find with union by size and lazy parent updates.\n\nHowever, since we only do `N-1` unions