:\n$$\n\\max(|x_i - x_j|, |y_i - y_j|) = \\max( |u_i - u_j|/2, |v_i - v_j|/2 ) \\quad \\text{?} \\quad \\text{No.}\n$$\n\nWait:\nActually, a known identity:\n$$\n\\max(|a|,|b|) = \\frac{1}{2} ( |a+b| + |a-b| )\n$$\n\nBut we have:\n$$\n\\max(|x_i - x_j|, |y_i - y_j|) = ?\n$$\n\nBut consider:\n$$\n|x_i - x_j| = \\left| \\frac{(u_i - u_j) + (v_i - v_j)}{2} \\right|\n$$\n$$\n|y_i - y_j| = \\left| \\frac{(u_i - u_j) - (v_i - v_j)}{2} \\right|\n$$\n\nThen:\n$$\n\\max(|x_i-x_j|, |y_i-y_j|) = \\frac{1}{2} \\max( |(u_i - u_j) + (v_i - v_j)|, |(u_i - u_j) - (v_i - v_j)| )\n$$\n\nBut:\n$$\n\\max(|A+B|, |A-B|) = |A| + |B|\n$$\n\nBecause:\n- If A and B same sign: |A+B| = |A|+|B|, |A-B| = ||A|-|B|| ≤ |A|+|B|\n- So max is |A| + |B|\n\nSimilarly for opposite signs.\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\max(|A+B|, |A-B|) = |A| + |B|\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n$$\n\\max(|x_i - x_j|, |y_i - y_j|) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( |u_i - u_j| + |v_i - v_j| \\right)\n$$\n\nWait:\nLet A = u_i - u_j, B = v_i - v_j\n\nThen:\n$$\n\\max(|x_i-x_j|,|y_i-y_j|) = \\frac{1}{2} \\max(|A+B|, |A-B|) = \\frac{1}{2} (|A| + |B|) = \\frac{1}{2} ( |u_i - u_j| + |v_i - v_j| )\n$$\n\nYes!\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\text{dist}(P_i, P_j) = \\begin{cases}\n\\frac{1}{2} \\left( |u_i - u_j| + |v_i - v_j| \\right) & \\text{if } (x_i+y_i) \\equiv (x_j+y_j) \\pmod{2} \\\\\n0 & \\text{otherwise}\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nAnd since within a group, all points have the same parity of $x+y$, and $u = x+y$, so $u_i \\equiv u_j \\pmod{2}$ → $u_i - u_j$ even → so $|u_i - u_j|$ is even → divisible by 2 → so the whole thing is integer.\n\nSimilarly $v = x - y$: $v_i - v_j$ even? $x_i - y_i - (x_j - y_j) = (x_i - x_j) - (y_i - y_j)$, and since $x_i+y_i$ and $x_j+y_j$ same parity, then $x_i-x_j$ and $y_i-y_j$ same parity → so difference even → yes.\n\nSo |u_i - u_j| and |v_i - v_j| are even → sum divisible by 2 → result is integer.\n\nSo now:\n\nFor each group (same $ (x+y) \\mod 2 $), define:\n- $ u_i = x_i + y_i $\n- $ v_i = x_i - y_i $\n\nThen:\n$$\n\\sum_{i<j} \\text{dist}(P_i, P_j) = \\sum_{i<j} \\frac{1}{2} (|u_i - u_j| + |v_i - v_j|) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\sum_{i<j} |u_i - u_j| + \\sum_{i<j} |v_i - v_j| \\right)\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute sum of absolute differences of a list in O(n log n): sort the list, and use prefix sums.\n\nIt is well known that:\n$$\n\\sum_{i < j} |a_i - a_j| = \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} a_i \\cdot (2i - n + 1)\n$$\nafter sorting.\n\nOr more clearly:\n\nIf we sort array $a$, then:\n$$\n\\sum_{i < j} |a_i - a_j| = \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} a_i \\cdot (i - (n-1-i)) = \\sum_{i} a_i (2i - n + 1)\n$$\n\nLet me verify:\n\nFor sorted $a_0 ≤ a_1 ≤ \\ldots ≤ a_{n-1}$:\n\nFor element $a_i$, it appears in:\n- As larger: in pairs (j, i) for j < i → i times, contributes $a_i - a_j$ → total contribution as larger: i * a_i - sum_{j<i} a_j\n- As smaller: in pairs (i, j) for j > i → (n-1-i) times, contributes $a_j - a_i$ → total: sum_{j>i} a_j - (n-1-i) a_i\n\nSo total contribution of $a_i$:\n- $i a_i - \\sum_{j<i} a_j - (n-1-i) a_i + \\sum_{j>i} a_j$? No, we want sum of |a_i - a_j|, so it's linear.\n\nBetter: total sum:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i<j} (a_j - a_i) = \\sum_j a_j \\cdot (\\text{number of } i < j) - \\sum_i a_i \\cdot (\\text{number of } j > i)\n= \\sum_j a_j \\cdot j - \\sum_i a_i \\cdot (n-1-i) \\quad \\text{?}\n$$\n\nSince i < j, for fixed j, i from 0 to j-1 → j terms.\n\nSo:\n$$\n= \\sum_{j=0}^{n-1} a_j \\cdot j - \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} a_i \\cdot (n-1 - i) \\quad \\text{wait no}\n$$\n\nActually:\n$$\n\\sum_{i<j} (a_j - a_i) = \\sum_{j=0}^{n-1} a_j \\cdot j - \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} a_i \\cdot (n-1-i)\n$$\n\nLet k be index:\n\n= \\sum_k a_k [ k - (n-1-k) ] = \\sum_k a_k (2k - n + 1)\n\nYes.\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\sum_{i<j} |a_i - a_j| = \\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} a_k (2k - n + 1)\n$$\nif a is sorted in increasing order.\n\nTherefore, algorithm:\n\n1. Read N and points.\n2. Split points into two groups:\n   - group_even: (x_i + y_i) % 2 == 0\n   - group_odd: (x_i + y_i) % 2 == 1\n3. For each group:\n   - If size < 2, skip.\n   - Else:\n     - Extract u = x + y for all points in group\n     - Extract v = x - y for all points in group\n     - Sort u and v\n     - Compute sum_u = sum_{k} u_k * (2*k - len + 1) over sorted u\n     - Compute sum_v = similarly for v\n     - total_group = (sum_u + sum_v) // 2   [since we have 1/2 factor]\n4. Add group_contribution to answer.\n\nBut note: the 1/2 is outside: (sum_u + sum_v)/2\n\nBut sum_u and sum_v are sums of absolute differences over the u's and v's.\n\nYes.\n\nLet’s test with sample 1:\n\nPoints:\n- (0,0): u=0, v=0, sum=0 even → group_even\n- (1,3): u=4, v=-2, sum=4 even → group_even\n- (5,6): u=11, v=-1, sum=11 odd → group_odd\n\nOnly group_even has 2 points: (0,0) and (1,3)\n\nu = [0,4] → sorted: [0,4]\nsum_u = 0*(2*0 - 2 + 1) + 4*(2*1 - 2 + 1) = 0*(-1) + 4*(2-2+1)=4*1=4\n\nv = [0,-2] → sorted: [-2,0]\nsum_v = (-2)*(2*0-2+1) + 0*(2*1-2+1) = (-2)*(-1) + 0 = 2\n\ntotal = (4 + 2) // 2 = 6 // 2 = 3\n\nYes! Matches.\n\nSample 2:\n\n5 points:\n- (0,5): u=5, v=-5 → sum=5 odd\n- (1,7): u=8, v=-6 → sum=8 even? wait: 1+7=8 even → even\n- (2,9): 2+9=11 → odd\n- (3,8): 11 → odd\n- (4,6): 10 → even\n\nSo:\n\nGroup even:\n- (1,7): u=8, v=-6\n- (4,6): u=10, v=-2\n\nGroup odd:\n- (0,5): u=5, v=-5\n- (2,9): u=11, v=-7\n- (3,8): u=11, v=-5\n\nGroup even: 2 points\n- u: [8,10] → sorted [8,10]\n  sum_u = 8*(2*0-2+1) + 10*(2*1-2+1) = 8*(-1) + 10*(1) = -8+10=2\n- v: [-6,-2] → sorted [-6,-2]\n  sum_v = (-6)*(-1) + (-2)*(1) = 6 - 2 = 4\n- group_even_contribution = (2 + 4) // 2 = 3\n\nGroup odd: 3 points\n- u: [5,11,11] → sorted [5,11,11]\n- v: [-5,-7,-5] → sorted [-7,-5,-5]\n\nCompute sum_u:\n- k=0: 5*(2*0 - 3 + 1)=5*(-2) = -10\n- k=1:11*(2*1-3+1)=11*(0)=0\n- k=2:11*(2*2-3+1)=11*(2)=22\n- sum_u = -10 + 0 + 22 = 12\n\nsum_v:\n- v sorted: [-7,-5,-5]\n- k=0: -7*(2*0-3+1) = -7*(-2)=14\n- k=1: -5*(0)=0\n- k=2: -5*(2)= -10\n- sum_v = 14 + 0 -10 = 4\n\ngroup_odd_contribution = (12 + 4) // 2 = 16//2 = 8\n\nTotal = 3 + 8 = 11 → matches.\n\nPerfect.\n\n### Code Plan:\n\n- Read N\n- Read N lines of (x,y)\n- Group by (x+y) % 2\n- For each group:\n   - If size < 2: skip\n   - Else:\n      - u = [x+y for all in group]\n      - v = [x-y for all in group]\n      - Sort u and v\n      - Compute sum_abs = sum( a_i * (2*i - n + 1) ) for list a of size n\n      - group_sum = (sum_u + sum_v) // 2\n      - add to total\n- Print total\n\nNote: since u and v differences are even, the total sum of |u_i-u_j| is even? But no: sum of many even numbers? Not necessarily.\n\nBut we have (sum_u + sum_v) must be even? In example: 4+2=6 even, 12+4=16 even.\n\nWhy?\n\nBecause each |u_i - u_j| is even? Yes: u_i and u_j both even or both odd? In a group: (x+y) mod 2 same → so u_i ≡ u_j mod 2 → u_i - u_j even → |u_i - u_j| even.\n\nSimilarly for v: x_i - y_i: parity? But x_i+y_i ≡ x_j+y_j mod 2 → and x_i-x_j ≡ y_i-y_j mod 2 → so v_i - v_j = (x_i-x_j) - (y_i-y_j) ≡ 0 mod 2 → even.\n\nSo each |u_i - u_j| and |v_i - v_j| is even → sum over pairs is even → so sum_u and sum_v are even → sum_u + sum_v even → divisible by 2.\n\nSo integer.\n\nWe can safely use integer arithmetic.\n\nNow code:\n\n```python\ndef main():\n    import sys\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    points = []\n    for i in range(1, n+1):\n        x, y = map(int, data[i].split())\n        points.append((x, y))\n    \n    group_even = []\n    group_odd = []\n    \n    for x, y in points:\n        if (x + y) % 2 == 0:\n            group_even.append((x, y))\n        else:\n            group_odd.append((x, y))\n    \n    total = 0\n\n    for group in [group_even, group_odd]:\n        size = len(group)\n        if size < 2:\n            continue\n        u_list = [x + y for x, y in group]\n        v_list = [x - y for x, y in group]\n        u_list.sort()\n        v_list.sort()\n        \n        def sum_abs_diff(arr):\n            n_arr = len(arr)\n            total_sum = 0\n            for idx, val in enumerate(arr):\n                total_sum += val * (2 * idx - n_arr + 1)\n            return total_sum\n        \n        sum_u = sum_abs_diff(u_list)\n        sum_v = sum_abs_diff(v_list)\n        group_total = (sum_u + sum_v) // 2\n        total += group_total\n\n    print(total)\n\nmain()\n```\n\nLet’s test with samples.\n\nSample 1: expect 3 → passes.\n\nSample 2: expect 11 → passes.\n\nEdge: many points.\n\nTime: O(n log n) due to sorting.\n\nn <= 200000 → acceptable.\n\nWe'll run it.\n\nBut note: the formula `sum_abs_diff` uses `idx` from 0 to n-1.\n\nFor sorted array, it computes sum_{i<j} (a_j - a_i) = sum_k a_k (2k - n + 1)\n\nYes.\n\nSo this should be correct.\n\nLet’s write the code accordingly.\n\nOne more check: two points in group: (0,0) and (1,1)\n\n- (0,0): u=0, v=0\n- (1,1): u=2, v=0\n- group even\n\nu_list = [0,2] → sum_u = 0*(0-2+1? 2*0-2+1=-1) -> 0*(-1)=0; 2*(2*1-2+1)=2*1=2 → sum_u=2\nv_list = [0,0] → sum_v=0*(2*0-2+1)+0*(...)=0\ngroup_total = (2+0)//2=1\ndist should be max(|1-0|,|1-0|)=max(1,1)=1 → yes.\n\nGood.\n\nNow output code.\n\n\n```python\ndef main():\n    import sys\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    points = []\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        x, y = map(int, data[i].split())\n        points.append((x, y))\n    \n    group_even = []\n    group_odd = []\n    \n    for x, y in points:\n        if (x + y) % 2 == 0:\n            group_even.append((x, y))\n        else:\n            group_odd.append((x, y))\n    \n    total = 0\n\n    for group in (group_even, group_odd):\n        size = len(group)\n        if size < 2:\n            continue\n        u_list = [x + y for x, y in group]\n        v_list = [x - y for x, y in group]\n        u_list.sort()\n        v_list.sort()\n        \n        def sum_abs_diff(arr):\n            n_arr = len(arr)\n            s = 0\n            for i, val in enumerate(arr):\n                s += val * (2 * i - n_arr + 1)\n            return s\n        \n        sum_u = sum_abs_diff(u_list)\n        sum_v = sum_abs_diff(v_list)\n        total += (sum_u + sum_v) // 2\n\n    print(total