      "generated_answer": "We need to check if we can place three given polyomino shapes into a 4×4 grid without overlapping, using only translation and rotation (no reflection), covering all cells exactly.  \n\nLet's break this down step by step before writing the code.\n\n---\n\n## **Step 1 — Understanding the problem**\n\nWe have **three polyominoes**, each given as a 4×4 grid of `#` (occupied) and `.` (empty).  \nThese are *templates* showing how the polyomino looks when placed somewhere in a 4×4 grid (not necessarily aligned to the top-left corner in the template).\n\nWe can:\n- **Translate** them anywhere in the 4×4 grid.\n- **Rotate** 90°, 180°, 270°.\n- **Not flip** (mirror) them — so rotation only, no reflection.\n\nWe need to check if there exists a placement of all three (in some rotation each) so that:\n1. They fit entirely inside the 4×4 grid.\n2. They don’t overlap.\n3. Every cell of the 4×4 is covered by exactly one polyomino.\n\n---\n\n## **Step 2 — Approach**\n\nA brute-force search is feasible because the grid is small (4×4 = 16 cells) and we have only 3 pieces.\n\nWe can:\n1. For each polyomino, generate all its **distinct rotations** (0°, 90°, 180°, 270°) from its given template.\n2. For each rotation, extract the **relative coordinates** of its `#` cells, normalized to top-left.\n3. Try to place each polyomino (in some rotation) somewhere in the 4×4 grid.\n4. Check all possible permutations of polyomino order and placements.\n\nBut careful:  \nWe must ensure that in the given template, the polyomino is not necessarily aligned to the top-left of the 4×4 box. So when extracting shape, we find all `#` cells, compute min row and min col, subtract to get relative coordinates.\n\nAlso, we must not generate reflected shapes.\n\n---\n\n## **Step 3 — Generating rotations without reflection**\n\nFor a set of coordinates `(r, c)` relative to top-left of its bounding box, a 90° rotation (clockwise) around origin `(0,0)` is `(c, -r)`. But we need to re-normalize after rotation so that min row and min col are 0 again.\n\nWe store rotations as **tuples of (dr, dc)** sorted, so we can compare and deduplicate.\n\n---\n\n## **Step 4 — Search method**\n\nWe can model the 4×4 grid as a 16-bit integer (bitmask), where bit `(r*4 + c)` is 1 if occupied.\n\nPlacement of a shape at `(top, left)` means adding bits for each `(top+dr, left+dc)` if within bounds.\n\nWe can do DFS:\n- Start with empty grid.\n- For each polyomino (in some order), try all its rotations, try all positions where it fits without overlapping current occupied cells.\n- Place it, mark cells occupied, continue with next polyomino.\n- After placing all 3, check if all 16 cells are covered.\n\nBut wait — we have exactly 3 polyominoes, and they must cover all 16 cells. So the sum of their `#` counts must be 16.  \nWe can check that first as a quick filter.\n\n---\n\n## **Step 5 — Implementation plan**\n\n1. Read input: 12 lines = 3 polyominoes × 4 rows each.\n2. For each polyomino:\n   - Find its cells in its 4×4 template.\n   - Normalize to top-left (0,0) to get shape.\n   - Generate 4 rotations, deduplicate.\n3. DFS/backtracking over polyominoes:\n   - For current polyomino, try each rotation.\n   - Try all positions in 4×4 grid where it fits without going outside.\n   - Place if no overlap, recurse.\n4. If all placed and all 16 cells filled → \"Yes\", else \"No\".\n\n---\n\n## **Step 6 — Writing the code**\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef read_polyominoes(data):\n    \"\"\"Read 3 polyominoes from 12 lines.\"\"\"\n    polyominoes = []\n    for i in range(3):\n        grid = [list(data[i*4 + j]) for j in range(4)]\n        polyominoes.append(grid)\n    return polyominoes\n\ndef get_shape(grid):\n    \"\"\"Extract normalized shape from a 4x4 grid.\"\"\"\n    cells = []\n    for r in range(4):\n        for c in range(4):\n            if grid[r][c] == '#':\n                cells.append((r, c))\n    # Normalize to top-left\n    min_r = min(r for r, _ in cells)\n    min_c = min(c for _, c in cells)\n    shape = sorted((r - min_r, c - min_c) for r, c in cells)\n    return tuple(shape)\n\ndef rotate_shape(shape):\n    \"\"\"Rotate shape 90 degrees clockwise around (0,0) and normalize.\"\"\"\n    new_shape = []\n    for r, c in shape:\n        # Rotate: (r, c) -> (c, -r)\n        new_r, new_c = c, -r\n        new_shape.append((new_r, new_c))\n    # Normalize\n    min_r = min(r for r, _ in new_shape)\n    min_c = min(c for _, c in new_shape)\n    new_shape = sorted((r - min_r, c - min_c) for r, c in new_shape)\n    return tuple(new_shape)\n\ndef generate_unique_rotations(shape):\n    \"\"\"Generate all unique rotations (no reflection).\"\"\"\n    rotations = []\n    current = shape\n    for _ in range(4):\n        rotations.append(current)\n        current = rotate_shape(current)\n    # Deduplicate\n    unique = []\n    seen = set()\n    for rot in rotations:\n        if rot not in seen:\n            seen.add(rot)\n            unique.append(rot)\n    return unique\n\ndef solve():\n    data = [line.rstrip() for line in sys.stdin]\n    if len(data) < 12:\n        return \"No\"\n    \n    polys_grids = read_polyominoes(data)\n    \n    # Step 1: Check total cells == 16\n    total_cells = 0\n    shapes_rots = []\n    for grid in polys_grids:\n        shape = get_shape(grid)\n        rots = generate_unique_rotations(shape)\n        shapes_rots.append(rots)\n        total_cells += len(shape)\n    \n    if total_cells != 16:\n        return \"No\"\n    \n    # Step 2: Backtracking\n    def dfs(idx, occupied_mask):\n        if idx == 3:\n            # Check if all 16 cells occupied\n            return occupied_mask == (1 << 16) - 1\n        \n        for rot in shapes_rots[idx]:\n            # Try all positions for this rotation\n            for top in range(4):\n                for left in range(4):\n                    new_mask = occupied_mask\n                    ok = True\n                    for dr, dc in rot:\n                        r = top + dr\n                        c = left + dc\n                        if not (0 <= r < 4 and 0 <= c < 4):\n                            ok = False\n                            break\n                        bit = 1 << (r * 4 + c)\n                        if new_mask & bit:\n                            ok = False\n                            break\n                        new_mask |= bit\n                    if ok:\n                        if dfs(idx + 1, new_mask):\n                            return True\n        return False\n    \n    result = dfs(0, 0)\n    return \"Yes\" if result else \"No\"\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    print(solve())\n```",
