    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We can solve this by mapping the vertices of the regular pentagon and checking whether the distances between points are the same.  \n\nIn a regular pentagon, vertices can be labeled sequentially: A, B, C, D, E.  \nThe distances between vertices come in two types:  \n- **Adjacent vertices** (distance 1 step along the perimeter)  \n- **Non-adjacent vertices** (distance 2 steps along the perimeter)  \n\nIf we label vertices in order `A=0, B=1, C=2, D=3, E=4`, then:  \n- Distance 1: |i - j| = 1 or 4 (since it wraps around)  \n- Distance 2: |i - j| = 2 or 3  \n\nSo we just need to check if both pairs are the same type of distance.\n\nHere’s the Python code:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip().split()\n    if len(data) < 2:\n        return\n    s1s2 = data[0]\n    t1t2 = data[1]\n\n    # Map letters to numbers for easier distance calculation\n    m = {'A': 0, 'B': 1, 'C': 2, 'D': 3, 'E': 4}\n\n    s1, s2 = m[s1s2[0]], m[s1s2[1]]\n    t1, t2 = m[t1t2[0]], m[t1t2[1]]\n\n    # Function to compute \"pentagon distance type\"\n    def dist_type(a, b):\n        diff = abs(a - b)\n        if diff == 1 or diff == 4:\n            return 1  # adjacent type\n        elif diff == 2 or diff == 3:\n            return 2  # non-adjacent type\n        else:\n            return 0  # same point (should not happen given constraints)\n\n    if dist_type(s1, s2) == dist_type(t1, t2):\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```",
