ar 2\n4. 8-20 \n -> 3\n7. 8-7\n -> 4\n\n no fifth edge.\n\n So deg(8) = 4\n\n not 5.\n\n Earlier I said 8-2, but there is no edge between 8 and 2.\n\n So deg(8) = 4\n\n similarly, \n\n vertex 2: edges: 3,9,11,12,13,17,18 -> 7 edges -> deg 7\n\n vertex 7: 19-7, 8-7 -> deg 2\n\n vertex 8: 3,18,20,7 -> deg 4\n\n vertex 14: 12,10,6 -> deg 3\n\n vertex 2: deg 7\n\n So degrees >=3:\n- 2:7\n- 8:4\n- 14:3\n-> a = 3\n\n leaf_count = number of deg 1 vertices.\n\n deg 1: vertices with degree 1:\n\n1,3,4,6,11,13,16,17,18,20 -> \n11: 2-11\n13: 2-13\n16: 2-16\n wait, 18: edge 2 of 8-18 -> only one edge -> deg 1\n similarly, 20: 8-20 -> deg 1\n3: 8-3 -> deg 1\n as above.\n\n So deg 1: 1,3,4,6,11,13,16,17,18,20 -> 10\n\n M = (20 - 10 + 2) / 3 = 12 / 3 = 4\n\n a = 3 ( vertices 2,8,14 with deg 7,4,3)\n\n then need M - a = 1 center with degree 2\n\n so the levels are:\n- deg 7,4,3, and one 2 -> [7,4,3,2] -> sorted: 2 3 4 7\n\n MATCHES expected output.\n\n I had a mistake: I thought 8-2, but there is no such edge. So deg(8)=4, not 5.\n\n Sorry.\n\n So algorithm is correct.\n\n### Final Algorithm\n\n1. Read N\n2. Read N-1 edges\n3. Compute degree for each vertex\n4. Count number of leaves = number of vertices with degree 1\n5. M = (N - leaf_count + 2) // 3\n6. Collect degrees of all vertices with degree >= 3\n7. Let a = count of such vertices\n8. Add (M - a) copies of 2 to the list (because those are level-2 stars)\n9. Sort the list\n10. Print\n\n Note: We can prove that M - a will be non-negative, and the solution unique.\n\n Let's code accordingly.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    edges = data[1:]\n    degree = [0] * (n + 1)\n    \n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        u = int(edges[2*i])\n        v = int(edges[2*i+1])\n        degree[u] += 1\n        degree[v] += 1\n    \n    leaf_count = sum(1 for i in range(1, n+1) if degree[i] == 1)\n    M = (n - leaf_count + 2) // 3\n    \n    levels = []\n    count_ge3 = 0\n   