      "generated_answer": "I'll solve this step-by-step.\n\n## Problem Understanding\n\n1. We need to find the longest substring of `word` that doesn't contain any string from `forbidden` as a substring\n2. A valid substring cannot have any of the forbidden strings as its substring\n3. We need to return the length of the longest valid substring\n\n## Approach Analysis\n\nSince the constraints are large (word length up to 10^5), we need an efficient solution. Let me think about possible approaches:\n\n1. **Brute force**: Check all substrings - O(n²) substrings, each check O(n) - too slow\n2. **Sliding window**: Use two pointers to maintain a valid window\n3. **Two pointers with optimization**: Since forbidden strings have max length 10, we can optimize the checking\n\nThe key insight is that forbidden strings are at most length 10, so when we extend our window, we only need to check the last few characters.\n\n## Step-by-Step Solution\n\n### Step 1: Use sliding window approach\n- Use two pointers (left and right) to maintain a window\n- Expand right pointer to include new characters\n- When we encounter a forbidden substring, move left pointer past the start of that forbidden substring\n\n### Step 2: Efficient forbidden checking\n- Since max forbidden length is 10, when we add a new character at position `right`, we only need to check substrings ending at `right` with length 1 to min(10, window_length)\n- Convert forbidden list to a set for O(1) lookup\n\n### Step 3: Handle edge cases\n- Update maximum length when window is valid\n- When forbidden substring is found, we need to contract the window\n\n```python\ndef longestValidSubstring(word, forbidden):\n    forbidden_set = set(forbidden)\n    n = len(word)\n    max_length = 0\n    left = 0\n    \n    for right in range(n):\n        # Check all possible substrings ending at 'right' that could be forbidden\n        # We only need to check substrings of length 1 to min(10, right-left+1)\n        found_forbidden = False\n        \n        # Check substrings ending at right, starting from max(left, right-9) to right\n        start = max(left, right - 9)  # Since max forbidden length is 10\n        \n        for i in range(start, right + 1):\n            substring = word[i:right + 1]\n            if substring in forbidden_set:\n                # Found a forbidden substring, move left pointer to i+1\n                # This ensures the forbidden substring is excluded\n                left = i + 1\n                found_forbidden = True\n                break\n        \n        # Update max length with current valid window size\n        max_length = max(max_length, right - left + 1)\n    \n    return max_length\n```",
