    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_507",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nGiven a string s, you need to partition it into one or more balanced substrings. For example, if s == \"ababcc\" then (\"abab\", \"c\", \"c\"), (\"ab\", \"abc\", \"c\"), and (\"ababcc\") are all valid partitions, but (\"a\", \"bab\", \"cc\"), (\"aba\", \"bc\", \"c\"), and (\"ab\", \"abcc\") are not. The unbalanced substrings are bolded.\nReturn the minimum number of substrings that you can partition s into.\nNote: A balanced string is a string where each character in the string occurs the same number of times.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: s = \"fabccddg\"\nOutput: 3\nExplanation:\nWe can partition the string s into 3 substrings in one of the following ways: (\"fab, \"ccdd\", \"g\"), or (\"fabc\", \"cd\", \"dg\").\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: s = \"abababaccddb\"\nOutput: 2\nExplanation:\nWe can partition the string s into 2 substrings like so: (\"abab\", \"abaccddb\").\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= s.length <= 1000\ns consists only of English lowercase letters.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3.5-9b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve(s: str) -> int:\n    n = len(s)\n    dp = [float('inf')] * (n + 1)\n    dp[0] = 0\n    \n    # Convert characters to integers 0-25 for faster access\n    nums = [ord(c) - 97 for c in s]\n    \n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        # Initialize frequency tracking for substring s[j:i]\n        # We reset these for each i\n        freq = [0] * 26\n        cnt = [0] * (n + 1)  # cnt[f] stores how many characters have frequency f\n        unique_freqs = 0\n        \n        # Iterate backwards to form substrings ending at i: s[j:i]\n        # Starting length 1 (j = i-1) up to length i (j = 0)\n        for j in range(i - 1, -1, -1):\n            idx = nums[j]\n            old_freq = freq[idx]\n            new_freq = old_freq + 1\n            freq[idx] = new_freq\n            \n            # Update counts of frequencies and the number of unique frequencies\n            if old_freq > 0:\n                cnt[old_freq] -= 1\n                if cnt[old_freq] == 0:\n                    unique_freqs -= 1\n            \n            if cnt[new_freq] == 0:\n                unique_freqs += 1\n            cnt[new_freq] += 1\n            \n            # A balanced string has all characters present appearing the same number of times.\n            # This corresponds to having exactly one unique frequency value among active characters.\n            if unique_freqs == 1:\n                # If s[j:i] is balanced, we can transition from state j to i\n                # The number of partitions increases by 1\n                if dp[j] + 1 < dp[i]:\n                    dp[i] = dp[j] + 1\n                    \n    return dp[n]\n```",
