    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_358",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nTakahashi has created a game where the player controls a dragon on a coordinate plane.\nThe dragon consists of N parts numbered 1 to N, with part 1 being called the head.\nInitially, part i is located at the coordinates (i,0). Process Q queries as follows.\n\n- 1 C: Move the head by 1 in direction C. Here, C is one of R, L, U, and D, which represent the positive x-direction, negative x-direction, positive y-direction, and negative y-direction, respectively. Each part other than the head moves to follow the part in front of it. That is, part i (2\\leq i \\leq N) moves to the coordinates where part i-1 was before the move.\n- 2 p: Find the coordinates of part p.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN Q\n\\mathrm{{query}}_1\n\\vdots\n\\mathrm{{query}}_Q\n\nEach query is in one of the following two formats:\n1 C\n\n2 p\n\nOutput\n\nPrint q lines, where q is the number of queries of the second type.\nThe i-th line should contain x and y separated by a space, where (x,y) are the answer to the i-th such query.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2 \\leq N \\leq 10^6\n- 1 \\leq Q \\leq 2\\times 10^5\n- For the first type of query, C is one of R, L, U, and D.\n- For the second type of query, 1\\leq p \\leq N.\n- All numerical input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n5 9\n2 3\n1 U\n2 3\n1 R\n1 D\n2 3\n1 L\n2 1\n2 5\n\nSample Output 1\n\n3 0\n2 0\n1 1\n1 0\n1 0\n\nAt each time when processing the second type of query, the parts are at the following positions:\n\nNote that multiple parts may exist at the same coordinates.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We can solve this by storing the positions of each part in a list and updating only the head for each movement, then having the following parts take the previous position of the part ahead of them.  \n\nHowever, since \\( N \\) can be as large as \\( 10^6 \\) and \\( Q \\) up to \\( 2\\times 10^5 \\), we need an \\( O(1) \\) update per move.  \nA common trick for snake-like movement problems is to store only the *head’s history of positions* and realize that part \\( i \\) at time \\( t \\) will be where the head was at time \\( t - (i-1) \\), provided we haven’t run out of history.  \n\nHere’s the implementation:  \n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    input = sys.stdin.readline\n    N, Q = map(int, input().split())\n\n    # Initial positions: part i at (i, 0)\n    # We store them as (x, y) in an array for quick lookup.\n    # But for large N and Q, we only track head movement and use a deque for history.\n    # However, we can also simulate directly for moderate sizes.\n    # Since constraints are large, we use the \"history of head\" approach.\n\n    # We'll keep an array of positions for all parts at current time.\n    # But updating all N parts for each move is O(NQ) → too slow.\n    # Instead, note: part i's position = head's position at time (current_time - (i-1))\n    # So we keep a history of head positions.\n\n    # At time 0, head at (1,0), part i at (i,0).\n    # We can store initial positions in an array for quick querying.\n    # But moves only affect the front and propagate.\n\n    # Let's implement with direct array simulation but optimized:\n    # We'll keep positions in a list of tuples.\n    # For move: new head position = head moved in direction.\n    # Then shift positions: part i <- old position of part i-1.\n    # This is O(N) per move → too slow for large N.\n    # We need O(1) per move.\n\n    # Better: store positions in a deque, but we need random access for queries.\n    # Actually, we only need to track head and keep a history.\n    # We can store history in a list: history[t] = head position at time t.\n    # Then at current time T, part i's position = history[T - (i-1)] if T >= i-1,\n    # else initial position (i - T, 0)? Wait, careful.\n\n    # Let's think simpler: parts follow the head with delay of i-1 steps.\n    # So at any time, part i = where head was (i-1) steps ago.\n    # So we just need to remember head positions for last N-1 steps.\n    # We can use a deque of maxlen N-1 for head history.\n\n    from collections import deque\n\n    # head_x, head_y: current head position\n    head_x, head_y = 1, 0\n\n    # history stores past head positions, most recent last.\n    # Initially, at time 0, head at (1,0). But we need to know head positions from time 0 up to now.\n    # Actually, we start with head at (1,0), and before any move, all parts are at (i,0).\n    # This means part i's position = (i,0) = head at time 0 moved right i-1 steps? Not exactly.\n    # Better: we initialize history with (1,0) repeated? Let's instead simulate explicitly:\n\n    # We'll store positions of all parts in an array for querying.\n    # But updating all parts each move is slow. Wait, we can update in O(1) by:\n    # Keep a deque of length N: positions of parts part1..partN.\n    # Move head: append new head to left, pop from right -> rest follows.\n    # Example: parts: [p1, p2, p3, p4, p5]\n    # Move head to new p1': new deque: [p1', p1, p2, p3, p4], discard p5.\n    # Then part i in new deque = new_p1 (head) at index 0, part2 = old head at index 1, etc.\n    # This matches: part i goes to where part i-1 was before.\n\n    # Let's implement that.\n\n    from collections import deque\n\n    # Initialize parts positions: part i at (i, 0)\n    parts = deque((i, 0) for i in range(1, N + 1))\n\n    out_lines = []\n    for _ in range(Q):\n        query = input().split()\n        if query[0] == '1':\n            # Move head in direction\n            C = query[1]\n            hx, hy = parts[0]  # current head\n            if C == 'R':\n                hx += 1\n            elif C == 'L':\n                hx -= 1\n            elif C == 'U':\n                hy += 1\n            elif C == 'D':\n                hy -= 1\n            # New head position\n            new_head = (hx, hy)\n            # Update: insert new head at front, last part is removed\n            parts.appendleft(new_head)\n            parts.pop()\n        else:\n            # Query part p\n            p = int(query[1])\n            # parts are 0-indexed in deque, part p is at index p-1\n            px, py = parts[p - 1]\n            out_lines.append(f\"{px} {py}\")\n\n    sys.stdout.write(\"\\n\".join(out_lines))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```",
