    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_493",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a string word of size n, and an integer k such that k divides n.\nIn one operation, you can pick any two indices i and j, that are divisible by k, then replace the substring of length k starting at i with the substring of length k starting at j. That is, replace the substring word[i..i + k - 1] with the substring word[j..j + k - 1].\nReturn the minimum number of operations required to make word k-periodic.\nWe say that word is k-periodic if there is some string s of length k such that word can be obtained by concatenating s an arbitrary number of times. For example, if word == “ababab”, then word is 2-periodic for s = \"ab\".\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: word = \"leetcodeleet\", k = 4\nOutput: 1\nExplanation:\nWe can obtain a 4-periodic string by picking i = 4 and j = 0. After this operation, word becomes equal to \"leetleetleet\".\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: word = \"leetcoleet\", k = 2\nOutput: 3\nExplanation:\nWe can obtain a 2-periodic string by applying the operations in the table below.\n\n\n\ni\nj\nword\n\n\n0\n2\netetcoleet\n\n\n4\n0\netetetleet\n\n\n6\n0\netetetetet\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= n == word.length <= 10^5\n1 <= k <= word.length\nk divides word.length.\nword consists only of lowercase English letters.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We can reason this out step by step.\n\nWe have a string of length `n`, split into `n/k` blocks of size `k`.  \nIn one operation, we can copy any block to any other block (both indices divisible by `k`).  \nWe want all blocks to be the same, with minimal operations.\n\nIf we think of blocks as “positions” 0 to `m-1` where `m = n/k`,  \nthen each block has a string of length `k`.  \nWe want to make all blocks equal by replacing entire blocks.\n\nThis is essentially:  \nWe have `m` items (blocks), each is a string of length `k`.  \nWe can copy any item to any other item in one operation.  \nMinimal operations to make all items the same.\n\n---\n\nIf we pick a target final block (the one we want all others to match),  \nthe cost is: number of blocks different from it.  \nBut we can also choose the final block to be one that doesn’t yet exist —  \nbut then we’d need to create it first.\n\nActually, copying works by picking `i` and `j` divisible by `k`,  \nso if we pick `j` as the source block index and `i` as destination,  \nwe replace block `i` with block `j`.\n\nThis is like:  \n- We can replace any block with any other existing block in one operation.  \n- If all blocks are the same, done.  \n- If we want all to be `X`, and there are `count[X]` blocks already equal to `X`,  \n  then we need `m - count[X]` operations (replace all others with one of the `X` blocks).\n\nSo the minimal operations = `m - max frequency of a block`.\n\nBut careful: We can also create a new block not present originally,  \nby first copying some block to it? Wait, every block already exists in original.  \nWe can’t “create” a totally new string from scratch — we can only copy from existing blocks.  \nSo the final repeating block must be one of the existing blocks.\n\nThus:  \nSplit into `m` blocks.  \nCount frequency of each distinct block.  \nAnswer = `m - max_freq`.\n\n---\n\nLet’s test with examples:\n\nExample 1:  \n`word = \"leetcodeleet\", k = 4`  \nBlocks: `\"leet\", \"code\", \"leet\"`  \nFrequencies: `\"leet\"`: 2, `\"code\"`: 1  \nMax freq = 2, m = 3  \nAnswer = 3 - 2 = 1 ✅\n\nExample 2:  \n`word = \"leetcoleet\", k = 2`  \nBlocks: `\"le\", \"et\", \"co\", \"le\", \"et\"`  \nFrequencies: `\"le\"`: 2, `\"et\"`: 2, `\"co\"`: 1  \nMax freq = 2, m = 5  \nAnswer = 5 - 2 = 3 ✅\n\n---\n\nSo the solution is straightforward.\n\nHere’s the Python function:\n\n```python\ndef min_operations_to_make_periodic(word: str, k: int) -> int:\n    n = len(word)\n    m = n // k\n    from collections import Counter\n    \n    # Split into blocks of length k\n    blocks = [word[i * k:(i + 1) * k] for i in range(m)]\n    \n    # Count frequencies\n    freq = Counter(blocks)\n    \n    # Minimum operations = total blocks - max frequency\n    return m - max(freq.values())\n```",
