    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a string moves of length n consisting only of characters 'L', 'R', and '_'. The string represents your movement on a number line starting from the origin 0.\nIn the i^th move, you can choose one of the following directions:\n\nmove to the left if moves[i] = 'L' or moves[i] = '_'\nmove to the right if moves[i] = 'R' or moves[i] = '_'\n\nReturn the distance from the origin of the furthest point you can get to after n moves.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: moves = \"L_RL__R\"\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: The furthest point we can reach from the origin 0 is point -3 through the following sequence of moves \"LLRLLLR\".\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: moves = \"_R__LL_\"\nOutput: 5\nExplanation: The furthest point we can reach from the origin 0 is point -5 through the following sequence of moves \"LRLLLLL\".\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: moves = \"_______\"\nOutput: 7\nExplanation: The furthest point we can reach from the origin 0 is point 7 through the following sequence of moves \"RRRRRRR\".\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= moves.length == n <= 50\nmoves consists only of characters 'L', 'R' and '_'.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We can solve this by realizing that the maximum distance is achieved by maximizing the imbalance between left and right moves.  \n\nIf we interpret `'L'` as `-1`, `'R'` as `+1`, and `'_'` as a wildcard that we can choose to be either `-1` or `+1`, then the final position is the sum of these choices.  \nThe furthest distance from origin is simply the absolute value of this sum when we choose wildcards to maximize imbalance.  \n\nSo:  \n- Count fixed lefts (`L`) as `-1`.  \n- Count fixed rights (`R`) as `+1`.  \n- Count blanks (`_`) as flexible: we can assign them to whichever side will increase the absolute sum.  \n\nThus:  \nLet `fixed = count('R') - count('L')`.  \nLet `wild = count('_')`.  \n\nWe want `|fixed + wild_sign_sum|` to be maximized, where `wild_sign_sum` is ±wild.  \nWe choose the sign of `wild_sign_sum` to match the sign of `fixed` if possible (to increase magnitude), otherwise if `fixed` is zero, we choose either sign to get `|wild|`.  \n\nActually simpler:  \n`max_distance = |fixed| + wild`.  \nBecause we can always assign all blanks to move further in the direction of `fixed` if `fixed ≠ 0`, or all to one side if `fixed = 0`.  \n\nHere’s the Python function:\n\n```python\ndef furthest_distance_from_origin(moves: str) -> int:\n    left_count = 0\n    right_count = 0\n    blank_count = 0\n    \n    for ch in moves:\n        if ch == 'L':\n            left_count += 1\n        elif ch == 'R':\n            right_count += 1\n        elif ch == '_':\n            blank_count += 1\n    \n    fixed = right_count - left_count\n    return abs(fixed) + blank_count\n```",
