    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_481",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a string s. s[i] is either a lowercase English letter or '?'.\nFor a string t having length m containing only lowercase English letters, we define the function cost(i) for an index i as the number of characters equal to t[i] that appeared before it, i.e. in the range [0, i - 1].\nThe value of t is the sum of cost(i) for all indices i.\nFor example, for the string t = \"aab\":\n\ncost(0) = 0\ncost(1) = 1\ncost(2) = 0\nHence, the value of \"aab\" is 0 + 1 + 0 = 1.\n\nYour task is to replace all occurrences of '?' in s with any lowercase English letter so that the value of s is minimized.\nReturn a string denoting the modified string with replaced occurrences of '?'. If there are multiple strings resulting in the minimum value, return the lexicographically smallest one.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput:   s = \"???\" \nOutput:   \"abc\" \nExplanation:  In this example, we can replace the occurrences of '?' to make s equal to \"abc\".\nFor \"abc\", cost(0) = 0, cost(1) = 0, and cost(2) = 0.\nThe value of \"abc\" is 0.\nSome other modifications of s that have a value of 0 are \"cba\", \"abz\", and, \"hey\".\nAmong all of them, we choose the lexicographically smallest.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:  s = \"a?a?\"\nOutput:  \"abac\"\nExplanation:  In this example, the occurrences of '?' can be replaced to make s equal to \"abac\".\nFor \"abac\", cost(0) = 0, cost(1) = 0, cost(2) = 1, and cost(3) = 0.\nThe value of \"abac\" is 1.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= s.length <= 10^5\ns[i] is either a lowercase English letter or '?'.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minimizeStringValue(s):\n    # Count the fixed characters and number of question marks\n    fixed_count = {}\n    q_indices = []\n    \n    for i, char in enumerate(s):\n        if char == '?':\n            q_indices.append(i)\n        else:\n            fixed_count[char] = fixed_count.get(char, 0) + 1\n    \n    num_q = len(q_indices)\n    if num_q == 0:\n        return s\n    \n    # Determine the optimal letters to use for replacements\n    # We want to minimize the cost, so we should distribute letters as evenly as possible\n    # Use a greedy approach: at each step, choose the letter that would add minimal additional cost\n    \n    # Start with 0 counts for all letters\n    total_count = {chr(ord('a') + i): 0 for i in range(26)}\n    \n    # Add the fixed counts\n    for char, count in fixed_count.items():\n        total_count[char] += count\n    \n    # For the question marks, we need to decide which letters to use\n    # The cost added by placing a letter c at any position is equal to its current count\n    # So we should always pick the letter with minimum current count\n    # In case of ties, pick the lexicographically smallest\n    \n    q_replacements = []\n    \n    for _ in range(num_q):\n        # Find letter with minimum count (lexicographically smallest in case of ties)\n        min_count = min(total_count.values())\n        for c in sorted(total_count.keys()):  # This ensures lexicographical ordering\n            if total_count[c] == min_count:\n                q_replacements.append(c)\n                total_count[c] += 1\n                break\n    \n    # Now construct the result string\n    result = list(s)\n    q_replacement_iter = iter(q_replacements)\n    \n    for i in range(len(result)):\n        if result[i] == '?':\n            result[i] = next(q_replacement_iter)\n    \n    return ''.join(result)\n```",
