      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    \n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    N, M = map(int, data[0].split())\n    grid = []\n    for i in range(1, 1 + N):\n        grid.append(data[i])\n    \n    # Define the Tak Code pattern\n    # We'll check each 9x9 subgrid\n    results = []\n    \n    # Loop through all possible top-left positions of 9x9 regions\n    for i in range(N - 8):  # i from 0 to N-9 (0-indexed)\n        for j in range(M - 8):  # j from 0 to M-9 (0-indexed)\n            valid = True\n            \n            # Check top-left 3x3 - must be all black (#)\n            for r in range(3):\n                for c in range(3):\n                    if grid[i + r][j + c] != '#':\n                        valid = False\n                        break\n                if not valid:\n                    break\n            \n            if not valid:\n                continue\n            \n            # Check bottom-right 3x3 - must be all black (#)\n            for r in range(6, 9):\n                for c in range(6, 9):\n                    if grid[i + r][j + c] != '#':\n                        valid = False\n                        break\n                if not valid:\n                    break\n            \n            if not valid:\n                continue\n            \n            # Check the cells adjacent to top-left 3x3 region - must be white (.)\n            # These are: top row (r=-1), left column (c=-1), and diagonal (-1,-1)\n            # and also the cells just below and right of the 3x3 block\n            \n            # Check cells around the top-left 3x3\n            # The adjacent cells are: \n            # - top row: (i-1, j-1) to (i-1, j+3)\n            # - bottom row of adjacent: (i+3, j-1) to (i+3, j+3)\n            # - left column: (i, j-1) to (i+2, j-1)\n            # - right column: (i, j+3) to (i+2, j+3)\n            # But we only check within the 9x9 context\n            \n            # Check the \"frame\" around top-left 3x3\n            # Row above top-left 3x3: i-1 is out of 9x9, but inside we check row i+3, col j to j+2\n            # Actually, within the 9x9, we need to check:\n            # - Bottom boundary: cells (i+3, j), (i+3, j+1), (i+3, j+2)\n            # - Right boundary: cells (i), (i+1, j+3), (i+2, j+3)\n            # - Corner: (i+3, j+3) is not adjacent diagonally to center? Let's follow spec\n            \n            # Adjacent cells are those that share edge or corner with the 3x3 block\n            # For top-left 3x3, adjacent are:\n            for dr in [-1, 0, 1]:\n                for dc in [-1, 0, 1]:\n                    # Check all positions around the 3x3 block\n                    # The 3x3 block is at [i, i+2] x [j, j+2]\n                    # So adjacent cells are:\n                    # - (i+3, j+c) for c in [-1,0,1,2,3] but within bounds\n                    # - (i+r, j+3) for r in [-1,0,1,2,3]\n                    # But we only consider within the 9x9 and specifically the 14 cells mentioned\n                    pass\n            \n            # Instead, directly check the 14 specific cells:\n            # The cells adjacent to top-left 3x3:\n            # Bottom row adjacent: (i+3, j), (i+3, j+1), (i+3, j+2)\n            # Right column adjacent: (i, j+3), (i+1, j+3), (i+2, j+3)\n            # Bottom-right corner: (i+3, j+3)\n            # But also top and left would be outside if we're at edge, but they are not part of 9x9?\n            \n            # Actually, within the 9x9 region, the adjacent cells to top-left 3x3 are:\n            # - Below: (i+3, j), (i+3, j+1), (i+3, j+2)\n            # - Right: (i, j+3), (i+1, j+3), (i+2, j+3)\n            # - Diagonally bottom-right: (i+3, j+3)\n            # Similarly, the remaining 7 are adjacent to bottom-right 3x3:\n            # - Above: (i+5, j+5), (i+5, j+6), (i+5, j+7) -- wait, bottom-right starts at (i+6, j+6)\n            # Bottom-right 3x3: rows i+6 to i+8, cols j+6 to j+8\n            # Adjacent cells:\n            # - Top: (i+5, j+6), (i+5, j+7), (i+5, j+8)\n            # - Left: (i+6, j+5), (i+7, j+5), (i+8, j+5)\n            # - Diagonally top-left: (i+5, j+5)\n            \n            # So total 7 for top-left + 7 for bottom-right = 14 cells\n            \n            # Check 7 cells adjacent to top-left 3x3\n            adjacent_cells_top_left = [\n                (i+3, j),     # below left\n                (i+3, j+1),   # below middle\n                (i+3, j+2),   # below right\n                (i, j+3),     # right top\n                (i+1, j+3),   # right middle\n                (i+2, j+3),   # right bottom\n                (i+3, j+3)    # bottom-right corner\n            ]\n            \n            # Check 7 cells adjacent to bottom-right 3x3\n            adjacent_cells_bottom_right = [\n                (i+5, j+6),   # above left\n                (i+5, j+7),   # above middle\n                (i+5, j+8),   # above right\n                (i+6, j+5),   # left top\n                (i+7, j+5),   # left middle\n                (i+8, j+5),   # left bottom\n                (i+5, j+5)    # top-left corner\n            ]\n            \n            # All 14 cells must be white (.)\n            all_adjacent = adjacent_cells_top_left + adjacent_cells_bottom_right\n            for r, c in all_adjacent:\n                if r < 0 or r >= N or c < 0 or c >= M:\n                    valid = False\n                    break\n                if grid[r][c] != '.':\n                    valid = False\n                    break\n            \n            if valid:\n                # Row and column in 1-indexed\n                results.append((i + 1, j + 1))\n    \n    # Output results in lexicographical order (they are already sorted by i then j due to loop order)\n    for i, j in results:\n        print(f\"{i} {j}\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
