    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given M relative position constraints between people on a 2D coordinate plane. Person 1 is at the origin (0, 0). Each constraint says: \"From person A_i's perspective, person B_i is X_i units in the positive x-direction and Y_i units in the positive y-direction\", which implies:\n\n```\n(x[B_i] - x[A_i], y[B_i] - y[A_i]) = (X_i, Y_i)\n=> x[B_i] = x[A_i] + X_i\n=> y[B_i] = y[A_i] + Y_i\n```\n\nWe need to determine the absolute coordinates of each person if possible. This is a **graph connectivity** problem in 2D with relative offsets.\n\nWe will:\n- Use union-find or DFS/BFS over a graph where edges represent relative position constraints.\n- Start from person 1 (known to be at (0,0)) and propagate known positions using BFS/DFS.\n- Only people in the connected component of person 1 can have their coordinates uniquely determined.\n- If a node is not reachable from person 1, output \"undecidable\".\n\nWe must handle duplicate or redundant edges (same info multiple times) — but the constraints say input is consistent.\n\nApproach:\n1. Build a directed graph: for each (A, B, X, Y), add an edge from A → B with offset (X, Y), and also add reverse edge B → A with (-X, -Y).\n2. Use BFS starting from node 1 (at (0, 0)).\n3. For each neighbor reachable via an edge (from u to v with offset dx, dy), if v is not yet visited, set:\n   - x[v] = x[u] + dx\n   - y[v] = y[u] + dy\n4. Output the result for each person from 1 to N.\n\nLet’s code accordingly.\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque, defaultdict\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    n = int(data[0])\n    m = int(data[1])\n    \n    # Graph: adjacency list with (neighbor, dx, dy)\n    graph = defaultdict(list)\n    index = 2\n    for _ in range(m):\n        a = int(data[index])\n        b = int(data[index+1])\n        x = int(data[index+2])\n        y = int(data[index+3])\n        index += 4\n        # From a to b: b is at (a.x + x, a.y + y)\n        graph[a].append((b, x, y))\n        graph[b].append((a, -x, -y))\n    \n    # Arrays to store coordinates; use None to indicate unknown\n    x_coords = [None] * (n + 1)\n    y_coords = [None] * (n + 1)\n    \n    # Person 1 is at origin\n    x_coords[1] = 0\n    y_coords[1] = 0\n    \n    # BFS starting from node 1\n    q = deque([1])\n    visited = [False] * (n + 1)\n    visited[1] = True\n    \n    while q:\n        u = q.popleft()\n        for v, dx, dy in graph[u]:\n            new_x = x_coords[u] + dx\n            new_y = y_coords[u] + dy\n            if not visited[v]:\n                x_coords[v] = new_x\n                y_coords[v] = new_y\n                visited[v] = True\n                q.append(v)\n            # If already visited, we assume consistency (per problem statement)\n    \n    # Output\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        if x_coords[i] is None or y_coords[i] is None:\n            print(\"undecidable\")\n        else:\n            print(x_coords[i], y_coords[i])\n\nmain()\n```",
