      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the minimum number of changes to make a binary string \"beautiful\" - meaning it can be partitioned into substrings where each substring has even length and contains only one type of character (all 0's or all 1's).\n\nLet me analyze the pattern:\n- Since the entire string has even length, and each substring must have even length, I can think of grouping adjacent characters.\n- The key insight is that I can use dynamic programming or a greedy approach, but let me think about the optimal strategy.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: since each substring must have even length, I can think of processing the string in pairs of positions. For any two consecutive characters that are in the same \"block\" of same characters, they should be the same.\n\nBut looking at this more carefully, the optimal approach is to use the fact that we want to minimize changes. I can think of this as: we want to create blocks of even length of the same character.\n\nA key insight: we can use dynamic programming where we track the state of whether we're in the middle of a block or need to start a new one.\n\nHowever, there's a simpler approach: since we need even-length blocks, we can consider that adjacent pairs should ideally be the same character. But we need to be more systematic.\n\nLet me think about this: at each position, I have two choices - continue the current block or start a new block. But since blocks must be even length, if I'm at an odd position within a block, I must continue.\n\nActually, the optimal solution involves considering that we can break the string into segments of even length, each containing only 0's or only 1's.\n\nI can use DP where dp[i][c] represents the minimum changes needed to make s[0:i] beautiful, where the last block ends at position i-1 and consists of character c ('0' or '1').\n\n```python\ndef minChanges(s):\n    n = len(s)\n    # dp[i][c] = minimum changes to make s[0:i] beautiful, ending with character c\n    # c = 0 for '0', c = 1 for '1'\n    \n    # Initialize with large values\n    INF = float('inf')\n    dp = [[INF, INF] for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    \n    # Base case: empty string\n    dp[0][0] = 0\n    dp[0][1] = 0\n    \n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        for last_char in [0, 1]:\n            # Try to extend current block or start new block\n            char_to_match = '0' if last_char == 0 else '1'\n            \n            # We can end a block at position i-1 only if the block has even length\n            # So we look back at even distances\n            for block_start in range(i - 2, -1, -2):  # block_start to i-1 has even length\n                if dp[block_start][last_char] != INF:\n                    # Check if we can make s[block_start:i] all the same character\n                    changes_in_block = 0\n                    for j in range(block_start, i):\n                        if s[j] != char_to_match:\n                            changes_in_block += 1\n                    \n                    dp[i][last_char] = min(dp[i][last_char], dp[block_start][last_char] + changes_in_block)\n            \n            # Also consider ending a block of different character\n            other_char = 1 - last_char\n            other_char_to_match = '0' if other_char == 0 else '1'\n            \n            for block_start in range(i - 2, -1, -2):\n                if dp[block_start][other_char] != INF:\n                    changes_in_block = 0\n                    for j in range(block_start, i):\n                        if s[j] != other_char_to_match:\n                            changes_in_block += 1\n                    \n                    dp[i][last_char] = min(dp[i][last_char], dp[block_start][other_char] + changes_in_block)\n    \n    return min(dp[n][0], dp[n][1])\n```\n\nWait, this approach is too complex and inefficient. Let me reconsider with a simpler insight.\n\nActually, here's a better approach: since blocks must have even length, I can process the string and greedily form blocks. But the optimal solution is to use the fact that adjacent identical characters should be grouped.\n\nLooking at the examples and thinking more carefully, I can use a simpler DP:\n\n```python\ndef minChanges(s):\n    n = len(s)\n    INF = float('inf')\n    \n    # dp[i][c] = min changes for s[:i] ending with character c (0='0', 1='1')\n    dp = [[INF, INF] for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    dp[0][0] = 0\n    dp[0][1] = 0\n    \n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        current_char = s[i-1]\n        \n        # For each possible ending character\n        for end_char in [0, 1]:\n            target = '0' if end_char == 0 else '1'\n            cost = 1 if current_char != target else 0\n            \n            # We can extend a block from any previous even position\n            for prev in range(i - 1, -1, -1):\n                prev_len = i - prev\n                if prev_len % 2 == 0:  # even length block\n                    for prev_end in [0, 1]:\n                        if dp[prev][prev_end] != INF:\n                            # Can transition if prev_end == end_char (extending same char)\n                            # or any prev_end (starting new block with different char)\n                            if dp[prev][prev_end] + cost < dp[i][end_char]:\n                                dp[i][end_char] = dp[prev][prev_end] + cost\n    \n    return min(dp[n][0], dp[n][1])\n```\n\nThis is still not efficient. Let me think of the optimal solution.\n\nAfter reconsidering, here's the correct approach - I can use the fact that I want to minimize changes, and the constraint that blocks have even length means I can use a different DP state.\n\nActually, let me implement a cleaner solution:\n\n```python\ndef minChanges(s):\n    n = len(s)\n    INF = float('inf')\n    \n    # dp[i][c] represents minimum changes to make s[0:i] beautiful, \n    # where the last character of the last block is c\n    dp = [[INF, INF] for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    dp[0][0] = 0\n    dp[0][1] = 0\n    \n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        # cost to make s[i-1] equal to '0' or '1'\n        cost0 = 1 if s[i-1] != '0' else 0\n        cost1 = 1 if s[i-1] != '1' else 0\n        \n        # For each possible ending character at position i\n        for end_char in [0, 1]:\n            current_cost = cost0 if end_char == 0 else cost1\n            \n            # Try all possible start positions for the current block\n            # The block length must be even, so start at i - 2, i - 4, etc.\n            for block_len in range(2, i + 1, 2):\n                start = i - block_len\n                prev_cost = dp[start][1 - end_char]  # different character before\n                \n                if prev_cost != INF:\n                    total_cost = prev_cost + sum(1 if s[j] != ('0' if end_char == 0 else '1') else 0 \n                                               for j in range(start, i))\n                    if total_cost < dp[i][end_char]:\n                        dp[i][end_char] = total_cost\n            \n            # Also consider the current block alone (if i is even)\n            if i % 2 == 0:\n                block_cost = sum(1 if s[j] != ('0' if end_char == 0 else '1') else 0 \n                               for j in range(0, i))\n                if block_cost < dp[i][end_char]:\n                    dp[i][end_char] = block_cost\n    \n    return min(dp[n][0], dp[n][1])\n```",
