      "generated_answer": "We need to count pairs \\((i, j)\\) with \\(i < j\\) such that:  \n\n\\[\n(x_i \\oplus x_j) + (y_i \\oplus y_j) = k\n\\]\n\nWhere \\(0 \\le k \\le 100\\) and coordinates length up to 50,000.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Understanding constraints\n\nSince \\(k \\le 100\\), and XOR results are non-negative integers,  \n\\((x_i \\oplus x_j)\\) and \\((y_i \\oplus y_j)\\) are also non-negative.\n\nLet:\n\\[\na = x_i \\oplus x_j\n\\]\n\\[\nb = y_i \\oplus y_j\n\\]\nThen:\n\\[\na + b = k\n\\]\nwhere \\(a \\ge 0\\), \\(b \\ge 0\\), and both are integers.\n\nSo \\(a\\) can be \\(0, 1, \\dots, k\\), and \\(b = k - a\\).\n\n---\n\n## Step 2: Translating XOR equations\n\nIf \\(a = x_i \\oplus x_j\\), then:\n\\[\nx_j = x_i \\oplus a\n\\]\nSimilarly, if \\(b = y_i \\oplus y_j\\), then:\n\\[\ny_j = y_i \\oplus b\n\\]\n\nThus, for a given point \\((x_i, y_i)\\) and a given \\(a\\) (where \\(0 \\le a \\le k\\)),  \n\\(b = k - a\\),  \nwe can compute the *target* point:\n\\[\n(x_{\\text{target}}, y_{\\text{target}}) = (x_i \\oplus a, \\ y_i \\oplus b)\n\\]\n\nWe just need to count how many earlier points match that target.\n\n---\n\n## Step 3: Efficient counting\n\nWe can iterate through points and for each point, try all \\(a\\) from \\(0\\) to \\(k\\),  \nbut only if \\(b = k - a \\ge 0\\) (automatically true since \\(a \\le k\\)).\n\nWe need to avoid double counting \\(i < j\\), so while iterating \\(i\\) from \\(0\\) to \\(n-1\\),  \nwe check for previous points that match the target.  \nThat means storing already seen points in a hash map:  \n`count[(x, y)]` = how many times we’ve seen \\((x, y)\\) before current index.\n\nFor each \\(i\\):\n- For each \\(a\\) from \\(0\\) to \\(k\\):\n  - \\(b = k - a\\)\n  - Target = \\((x_i \\oplus a,\\ y_i \\oplus b)\\)\n  - Add to answer the count of this target in the hash map (these are points before \\(i\\))\n- Then add \\((x_i, y_i)\\) to hash map for future points.\n\nThis ensures \\(i < j\\) condition is satisfied automatically.\n\n---\n\n## Step 4: Complexity\n\nWe have \\(n\\) points, and for each point we try \\(k+1\\) possibilities of \\(a\\).  \nSo \\(O(n \\cdot k)\\) time, which is fine for \\(n=50000, k \\le 100\\) (~5M ops).  \nMemory: \\(O(n)\\) for the hash map.\n\n---\n\n## Step 5: Implementation\n\nWe can implement this directly.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef count_pairs(coordinates, k):\n    point_count = defaultdict(int)\n    result = 0\n    \n    for x, y in coordinates:\n        for a in range(k + 1):\n            b = k - a\n            target_x = x ^ a\n            target_y = y ^ b\n            result += point_count[(target_x, target_y)]\n        point_count[(x, y)] += 1\n    \n    return result\n```\n\nThis is the executable Python function for the problem.",
