      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all tokens from stdin to handle potential whitespace variations\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    \n    if not input_data:\n        return\n\n    # Parse W and B from the input\n    W = int(input_data[0])\n    B = int(input_data[1])\n    \n    # The length of the substring we are looking for\n    L = W + B\n    \n    # The base repeating pattern string S\n    P = \"wbwbwwbwbwbw\"\n    \n    # Construct a sufficiently long string S to simulate the infinite repetition.\n    # The pattern P has length 12. The constraints on W and B are <= 100, so L <= 200.\n    # To ensure we check all possible starting offsets modulo 12, we need S to be long enough\n    # such that we can form a substring of length L starting from every offset in 0..11.\n    # P repeated 50 times gives length 600, which covers max L = 200 comfortably.\n    S = P * 50\n    \n    found = False\n    \n    # Iterate through all possible substrings of length L in S.\n    # If any substring has exactly W 'w's (and thus B 'b's since length is L),\n    # then the answer is Yes.\n    # Since the pattern is periodic with length 12, checking a window of sufficient size\n    # ensures we cover all distinct substring structures relative to the pattern.\n    for i in range(len(S) - L + 1):\n        if S[i : i+L].count('w') == W:\n            found = True\n            break\n    \n    if found:\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    solve()\n```",
