      "generated_answer": "Here’s a Python function that computes the minimum number of adjacent swaps to group all black balls (`'1'`) to the right, based on the logic of moving each `'1'` to its final grouped position:\n\n```python\ndef min_swaps_to_group_ones_to_right(s: str) -> int:\n    total_ones = s.count('1')\n    if total_ones == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    n = len(s)\n    current_ones_in_window = 0\n    max_ones_in_window = 0\n\n    # First window of size total_ones\n    for i in range(total_ones):\n        if s[i] == '1':\n            current_ones_in_window += 1\n    max_ones_in_window = current_ones_in_window\n\n    # Sliding the window across the string\n    for i in range(total_ones, n):\n        if s[i] == '1':\n            current_ones_in_window += 1\n        if s[i - total_ones] == '1':\n            current_ones_in_window -= 1\n        max_ones_in_window = max(max_ones_in_window, current_ones_in_window)\n\n    return total_ones - max_ones_in_window\n```\n\nThis function works by finding the maximum number of `'1'`s that are already in a contiguous block of size `total_ones` (the number of black balls) anywhere in the string.  \nThe minimum swaps needed is the number of `'1'`s *outside* that best block, which equals `total_ones - max_ones_in_window`.",
