      "generated_answer": "We need to maximize the product of a subset of numbers.  \nSince we can choose any subset (non-empty), the problem is about picking numbers to maximize the product.\n\nGiven the small constraints (length ≤ 13), we could brute-force all subsets, but there’s a more efficient approach using dynamic reasoning about positives and negatives.\n\n---\n\nHere’s the function that solves it efficiently:\n\n```python\ndef maxStrength(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    # Separate positives, negatives, zeros\n    positives = []\n    negatives = []\n    zeros = 0\n    \n    for num in nums:\n        if num > 0:\n            positives.append(num)\n        elif num < 0:\n            negatives.append(num)\n        else:\n            zeros += 1\n    \n    # If only zeros and one negative, result might be 0 or negative\n    if not positives and not negatives:\n        return 0\n    if not positives and len(negatives) == 1:\n        if zeros > 0:\n            return 0\n        else:\n            return negatives[0]\n    \n    # Multiply all positives\n    product = 1\n    for p in positives:\n        product *= p\n    \n    # Multiply negatives if there are at least 2\n    negatives.sort()\n    if len(negatives) % 2 == 1:\n        # Exclude smallest negative (largest by abs value) if odd count\n        for i in range(len(negatives) - 1):\n            product *= negatives[i]\n    else:\n        # Multiply all negatives if even count\n        for neg in negatives:\n            product *= neg\n    \n    return product\n```",
