      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to understand what the operation does and how to maximize the sum of squares.\n\nThe key insight is that the operation preserves the total \"bit mass\". When we do:\n- nums[i] = nums[i] AND nums[j]\n- nums[j] = nums[i] OR nums[j]\n\nThe sum of all values remains the same because:\n- (a AND b) + (a OR b) = a + b\n\nHowever, we can redistribute the bits to create larger values, which when squared gives us a bigger sum.\n\nThe optimal strategy is to concentrate as many bits as possible into k values. For each bit position, we want to assign all the 1-bits from that position to at most k numbers.\n\nHere's my approach:\n1. Count how many 1-bits we have at each bit position across all numbers\n2. For each of the k positions we need to fill, greedily assign the highest possible value by setting bits where we have available 1-bits\n3. The maximum value we can create at each position is determined by which bit positions still have remaining 1-bits\n\n```python\ndef maxSum(self, nums, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    # Count bits at each position\n    bit_count = [0] * 32  # 32 bits enough for numbers up to 10^9\n    \n    for num in nums:\n        for i in range(32):\n            if num & (1 << i):\n                bit_count[i] += 1\n    \n    result = 0\n    # For each of the k elements we need to choose\n    for _ in range(k):\n        # Construct the maximum possible value using available bits\n        max_val = 0\n        for i in range(31, -1, -1):  # From highest bit to lowest\n            if bit_count[i] > 0:\n                max_val |= (1 << i)\n                bit_count[i] -= 1\n        \n        result = (result + (max_val * max_val) % MOD) % MOD\n    \n    return result\n```",
