ms.length <= 10^4\n1 <= nums[i] <= 10^9",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the maximum element-sum of a \"complete\" subset where the product of every pair of indices is a perfect square.\n\nLet me analyze the pattern:\n- For a subset to be complete, every pair of indices (i,j) must have i*j be a perfect square\n- If i*j is a perfect square, then both i and j must have the same \"square-free part\"\n- The square-free part of a number is what remains after removing all perfect square factors\n- For example: 12 = 4*3, so its square-free part is 3; 27 = 9*3, so its square-free part is 3\n- Two numbers with the same square-free part will have a product that is a perfect square\n\nSo the algorithm:\n1. For each number, find its square-free part by dividing out all perfect squares\n2. Group indices by their square-free parts\n3. For each group, the sum of nums values gives a valid complete subset\n4. Return the maximum sum across all groups\n\n```python\ndef maximumSum(nums):\n    def get_square_free(n):\n        i = 2\n        while i * i <= n:\n            while n % (i * i) == 0:\n                n //= (i * i)\n            i += 1\n        return n\n    \n    n = len(nums)\n    groups = {}\n    \n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        square_free = get_square_free(i)\n        if square_free not in groups:\n            groups[square_free] = 0\n        groups[square_free] += nums[i - 1]  # Convert to 0-indexed for array access\n    \n    return max(groups.values())\n```",
