      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef maxPalindromes(words):\n    # Count frequency of each character across all words\n    char_count = {}\n    for word in words:\n        for char in word:\n            char_count[char] = char_count.get(char, 0) + 1\n    \n    # Sort words by length in ascending order to maximize palindromes\n    # (greedy approach: make shorter words palindrome first)\n    word_lengths = sorted([len(word) for word in words])\n    \n    palindromes = 0\n    odd_count = 0  # count of characters with odd frequency\n    \n    # Count how many characters have odd frequencies\n    for count in char_count.values():\n        if count % 2 == 1:\n            odd_count += 1\n    \n    # For each word (from shortest to longest), check if we can make it a palindrome\n    for length in word_lengths:\n        # A string can be a palindrome if at most one character has odd frequency\n        # For a word of given length, we need at most 1 odd frequency character\n        if odd_count <= 1:\n            # We can make this word a palindrome\n            palindromes += 1\n        else:\n            # We cannot make a palindrome unless we reduce odd_count\n            # For current word of this length, we need to check if we have enough characters\n            # to potentially reorganize to have at most one odd frequency\n            \n            # Actually, since we can redistribute characters freely,\n            # a word of length L can be a palindrome if we have enough pairs\n            # For palindrome: at most 1 character can appear odd number of times\n            # So we need: odd_count <= 1 for the characters allocated to this word\n            \n            # But since we're allocating optimally, we can always form a palindrome\n            # as long as we have appropriate character counts\n            # The key insight is: we can form palindromes greedily from shortest to longest\n            \n            # For the overall character pool, we can form a palindrome for a word of length L if\n            # we can assign characters such that at most one character has odd count\n            \n            # Actually, we don't need to simulate assignment - we can use a greedy approach\n            # We can make a palindrome from the current character distribution\n            # as long as we have enough characters and can manage the odd counts\n            \n            # Since we can redistribute freely, we can make palindromes for shorter words first\n            # For each word, we need at most 1 odd count character in its composition\n            \n            # The limiting factor is the total odd_count across all characters\n            # Each palindrome can \"absorb\" at most one odd-count character\n            \n            # If we have more than one odd-count character globally, we can't make\n            # all words palindromes, but we can make at most (number of words) palindromes\n            # constrained by the odd_count\n            \n            # Actually, we can always convert pairs of odd-count characters to even\n            # by \"transferring\" one instance, but this is complex\n            \n            # Simpler approach: since we can redistribute arbitrarily, we're limited\n            # by the number of odd-frequency characters - we can have at most one\n            # odd-frequency character per palindrome\n            \n            # So if odd_count <= number of remaining words, we can distribute the\n            # odd-count characters (one per word) and make all remaining words palindromes\n            break\n    \n    # The key insight: we can rearrange all characters freely\n    # So the problem reduces to: how many palindromes can we form given the character counts?\n    \n    # Since we can redistribute characters arbitrarily, we can make a word a palindrome\n    # if and only if we can assign characters to it with at most one character having odd count\n    \n    # Strategy: sort words by length (ascending), and for each word try to form a palindrome\n    # Use a greedy approach: use up character pairs, and save odd-count characters for centers\n    \n    # Reset approach\n    total_chars = sum(len(word) for word in words)\n    char_freq = list(char_count.values())\n    \n    # Count available pairs - each pair can be used symmetrically in palindromes\n    total_pairs = sum(freq // 2 for freq in char_freq)\n    \n    # Sort words by length to prioritize shorter words (greedy)\n    sorted_words = sorted(words, key=len)\n    \n    palindromes = 0\n    remaining_pairs = total_pairs\n    \n    for word in sorted_words:\n        word_len = len(word)\n        # For a palindrome of length word_len, we need word_len//2 pairs\n        pairs_needed = word_len // 2\n        \n        if remaining_pairs >= pairs_needed:\n            remaining_pairs -= pairs_needed\n            palindromes += 1\n        else:\n            # Not enough pairs to form this palindrome\n            break\n    \n    return palindromes\n```",
