      "generated_answer": "We are given `N` cards, each with a strength `A_i` and cost `C_i`.  \nTakahashi repeatedly discards a card `y` if there exists another card `x` such that:\n\n- `A_x > A_y` (x is stronger)\n- `C_x < C_y` (x is cheaper)\n\nThis means: if a stronger card is cheaper, then the weaker and more expensive card is considered \"weak\" and discarded.\n\nThe key insight:\n> A card `y` is discarded if there exists any card `x` with `A_x > A_y` and `C_x < C_y`.\n\nOur goal: Find all cards that **cannot** be discarded — i.e., no other card is both **stronger and cheaper**.\n\n---\n\n### Strategy:\n\n1. Sort the cards by strength (`A_i`) in ascending order.\n2. As we process from weakest to strongest, we want to know: **is this card dominated by any stronger (i.e., previously seen in reverse order) card that is cheaper?**\n\nBut note: since stronger cards have higher `A_i`, if we process cards in **decreasing order of strength**, then for each card, we can check whether any already processed (stronger) card has a lower cost.\n\nSo:\n- Sort cards by `A_i` descending (strongest first).\n- Maintain the **minimum cost** seen so far among the processed (stronger) cards.\n- A card is **not dominated** (and thus survives) only if its cost is **less than or equal to the minimum cost seen so far among stronger cards**? Wait — no.\n\nActually:\n- When processing in descending order of `A_i` (strongest first), we keep track of the **minimum cost** we’ve seen.\n- When we get to a card `i`, if `C_i < min_cost_so_far`, then **no stronger card has lower cost**, so this card is **not dominated by any stronger card**, so it survives.\n- But wait: a stronger card dominates this card only if it has **lower cost**.\n\nSo:\n- If there exists a stronger card (already processed) with cost < `C_i`, then card `i` is dominated → discard.\n- So card `i` survives only if `C_i < min_cost_so_far` (because that would mean this card is cheaper than any stronger card).\n\nWait: if `C_i` is **less than** the current minimum cost, then this card is **cheaper** than any stronger card, so no stronger card can dominate it. So this card survives.\n\nBut also, this card now sets a new lower cost boundary.\n\nSo:\n- Traverse cards in **decreasing order of A**:\n  - Keep `min_cost` = minimum cost among cards processed (i.e., stronger cards).\n  - For current card (with cost `c`):\n    - If `c < min_cost`, then **no stronger card is cheaper**, so this card is **not dominated**, so it **survives**.\n    - Then update `min_cost = min(min_cost, c)`.\n\nSo we collect every card where `c < min_cost` at the time of processing (and initialize `min_cost` to infinity).\n\nFinally, output the **original indices** of these remaining cards, sorted in ascending order.\n\n---\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. Read input.\n2. Store (A_i, C_i, i) for each card.\n3. Sort by `A_i` descending.\n4. Traverse:\n   - Initialize `min_cost = float('inf')`\n   - For each card:\n     - if `C_i < min_cost`: it survives → add original index to result\n     - update `min_cost = min(min_cost, C_i)`\n5. Sort the result (list of indices) in ascending order.\n6. Print count and indices.\n\n---\n\n### Why this works?\n\nBecause we're checking: is there any stronger card that is cheaper?  \nBy going from strongest to weakest, we keep the minimum cost of any stronger card seen so far.  \nIf current card's cost is **less than that**, then **no stronger card is cheaper**, so there's no card that both has higher `A` and lower `C` → it's safe.\n\nConversely, if `C_i >= min_cost`, then some stronger card has cost <= `C_i`, and since it's stronger and cheaper (or equal cost), but note: costs are distinct → so `C_i > min_cost`, so there is a stronger and cheaper card → discard.\n\nSo condition `C_i < min_cost` is correct.\n\n---\n\n### Code:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    cards = []\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        a, c = map(int, data[i].split())\n        cards.append((a, c, i))  # (strength, cost, original index)\n    \n    # Sort by strength descending\n    cards.sort(key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)\n    \n    min_cost = float('inf')\n    surviving_indices = []\n    \n    for a, c, idx in cards:\n        if c < min_cost:\n            surviving_indices.append(idx)\n            min_cost = c\n    \n    # Sort indices in ascending order\n    surviving_indices.sort()\n    \n    print(len(surviving_indices))\n    print(' '.join(map(str, surviving_indices)))\n\nsolve()\n```",
