      "generated_answer": "We need to count how many integers \\( k \\) satisfy:\n\n\\[\nL \\leq A + kM \\leq R\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( k \\) is an integer.\n\n---\n\n### Step-by-step reasoning\n\nLet’s rearrange the inequality:\n\n\\[\nL \\leq A + kM \\leq R\n\\]\n\nSubtract \\( A \\):\n\n\\[\nL - A \\leq kM \\leq R - A\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to handle the sign of \\( M \\) (but \\( M > 0 \\) from constraints, so division by \\( M \\) is safe).\n\nDivide by \\( M \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{L - A}{M} \\leq k \\leq \\frac{R - A}{M}\n\\]\n\nWe want integer \\( k \\), so:\n\n- Smallest \\( k \\) = \\(\\lceil \\frac{L - A}{M} \\rceil\\)\n- Largest \\( k \\) = \\(\\lfloor \\frac{R - A}{M} \\rfloor\\)\n\nThen the count is:\n\n\\[\n\\max\\left(0, \\ \\lfloor \\frac{R - A}{M} \\rfloor - \\lceil \\frac{L - A}{M} \\rceil + 1 \\right)\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Implementation notes\n\n- Use integer arithmetic to avoid floating-point errors.\n- Ceil of \\( \\frac{a}{b} \\) for integers \\( a, b \\) with \\( b > 0 \\) can be computed as:\n\n\\[\n\\lceil \\frac{a}{b} \\rceil = \\lfloor \\frac{a + b - 1}{b} \\rfloor \\quad \\text{if } a \\geq 0\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lceil \\frac{a}{b} \\rceil = -\\lfloor \\frac{-a}{b} \\rfloor \\quad \\text{if } a < 0\n\\]\n\nBut Python’s `math.ceil` with integers is fine if we convert to float carefully?  \nBetter to avoid floats for huge numbers. Let’s do pure integer computation.\n\nWe can compute:\n\\[\n\\text{ceil}(a/b) = (a + b - 1) // b \\quad \\text{if } b > 0 \\text{ and } a \\geq 0\n\\]\nWait, careful:  \nFor integer division rounding up when \\(a\\) can be negative:  \nWe can use:  \n`ceil(a/b) = -((-a) // b)` for positive b.\n\nCheck:  \nIf `a = -3, b = 2`,  \n`-((-a)//b) = -((3)//2) = -(1) = -1`, correct.\n\nSo:\n```\nceil_div(a, b) = -((-a) // b) if b > 0\n```\nBut `//` in Python floors toward negative infinity, so this works.\n\nSimilarly, floor division: `(R - A) // M`.\n\n---\n\n### Final formula\n\nLet:\n```\nlow = ceil_div(L - A, M)\nhigh = floor_div(R - A, M)\nanswer = max(0, high - low + 1)\n```\n\nWhere:\n- `floor_div(x, m) = x // m` (Python's `//` is floor division)\n- `ceil_div(x, m) = -((-x) // m)` for `m > 0`\n\n---\n\n### Python code\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef ceil_div(a, b):\n    # ceil(a / b) for integers, b > 0\n    return -((-a) // b)\n\ndef main():\n    A, M, L, R = map(int, sys.stdin.read().split())\n    low = ceil_div(L - A, M)\n    high = (R - A) // M\n    ans = max(0, high - low + 1)\n    print(ans)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```",
