i to S[i].\n    # However, placements overlap, so we need to be more careful.\n    \n    # Actually, here's a correct approach:\n    # Use dynamic programming where dp[i] = True if we can match S[0:i]\n    # To extend dp[i], we consider placing T starting at position i\n    # But also, positions can be set by T placements that started earlier\n    \n    # Let me re-read the problem. We start with X = \"####...#\" (N times)\n    # We can replace any M consecutive characters with T.\n    # We want to know if we can make X equal S.\n    \n    # Key insight: since we can perform operations any number of times, and later operations\n    # can overwrite earlier ones, we only care about the final state.\n    \n    # A character at position i in the final string must be set by some operation that covers position i.\n    # Specifically, if we place T starting at position j, it affects positions j to j+M-1.\n    # For position i to be correct, there must be some j in [i-M+1, i] such that:\n    # - 0 <= j <= N-M (valid starting position)\n    # - T[i-j] == S[i]\n    \n    # But this is necessary but not sufficient, because we need consistent placements.\n    \n    # Let's use a greedy algorithm from left to right:\n    # We'll keep track of what positions have been definitively set.\n    # However, due to overwrites, we need to be strategic.\n    \n    # Actually, let's use this approach:\n    # We can solve this with DP where the state is our progress through the string.\n    # dp[i] = True if we can match S[0:i] with some sequence of operations.\n    # To compute dp[i], we consider:\n    # - We could have reached i without placing a T ending at i\n    # - Or we could place a T that ends at i (starts at i-M)\n    \n    dp = [False] * (N + 1)\n    dp[0] = True\n    \n    for i in range(N):\n        if not dp[i]:\n            continue\n            \n        # We can always extend by not placing T starting at i\n        dp[i + 1] = True\n        \n        # Try placing T starting at position i\n        if i + M <= N:\n            # Check if placing T at position i is compatible with S[i:i+M]\n            compatible = True\n            for j in range(M):\n                if S[i + j] != T[j]:\n                    compatible = False\n                    break\n            \n            if compatible:\n                # If we place T at position i, it will set positions i to i+M-1\n                # This doesn't necessarily mean dp[i+M] becomes true immediately,\n                # but we'll let the DP propagate\n                pass  # We'll handle this differently\n    \n    # Let me try a different perspective inspired by string covering:\n    # We want to cover the string with intervals [i, i+M-1] where T matches S[i:i+M]\n    # And we want to know if we can cover all positions 0 to N-1.\n    \n    # Use DP: dp[i] = True if we can cover positions 0 to i-1\n    dp = [False] * (N + 1)\n    dp[0] = True\n    \n    for i in range(N):\n        if not dp[i]:\n            continue\n            \n        # From position i, we can choose to place T starting at i\n        if i + M <= N:\n            # Check if T matches S[i:i+M]\n            valid = True\n            for j in range(M):\n                if S[i + j] != T[j]:\n                    valid = False\n                    break\n            \n            if valid:\n                dp[i + M] = True\n        \n        # We can also skip position i (move forward by 1)\n        dp[i + 1] = dp[i + 1] or dp[i]\n    \n    if dp[N]:\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
