    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_485",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given an integer array nums of length n, and a positive integer k.\nThe power of a subsequence is defined as the minimum absolute difference between any two elements in the subsequence.\nReturn the sum of powers of all subsequences of nums which have length equal to k.\nSince the answer may be large, return it modulo 10^9 + 7.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,2,3,4], k = 3\nOutput: 4\nExplanation:\nThere are 4 subsequences in nums which have length 3: [1,2,3], [1,3,4], [1,2,4], and [2,3,4]. The sum of powers is |2 - 3| + |3 - 4| + |2 - 1| + |3 - 4| = 4.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [2,2], k = 2\nOutput: 0\nExplanation:\nThe only subsequence in nums which has length 2 is [2,2]. The sum of powers is |2 - 2| = 0.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [4,3,-1], k = 2\nOutput: 10\nExplanation:\nThere are 3 subsequences in nums which have length 2: [4,3], [4,-1], and [3,-1]. The sum of powers is |4 - 3| + |4 - (-1)| + |3 - (-1)| = 10.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n2 <= n == nums.length <= 50\n-10^8 <= nums[i] <= 10^8 \n2 <= k <= n",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We need to compute the sum of powers (minimum absolute difference between any two elements) over all subsequences of length `k`.  \n\nA direct enumeration of all \\(\\binom{n}{k}\\) subsequences is impossible for \\(n=50\\) if we check each one individually, but here \\(n \\leq 50\\) and \\(k \\leq n\\) means \\(\\binom{50}{25}\\) is huge, so we cannot brute force all subsequences directly.  \n\nWe need a combinatorial counting approach.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 — Understanding the definition\n\nFor a subsequence of length \\(k\\), its *power* is:\n\n\\[\n\\min_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq k} |x_i - x_j|\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_k\\) are the chosen elements in sorted order.  \nSo the power is the smallest gap between consecutive chosen elements (in sorted order).\n\nIf we sort the entire array `nums` first, then any subsequence (when sorted) will correspond to picking `k` positions in the sorted array (not necessarily consecutive). The *power* will be the minimum difference between consecutive picked positions in this sorted list.\n\n---\n\n## Step 2 — Reformulating the problem\n\nLet’s sort `nums` in ascending order:  \n\\(a_1 \\leq a_2 \\leq \\dots \\leq a_n\\).\n\nWe want:\n\n\\[\n\\text{answer} = \\sum_{S \\subset \\{1,\\dots,n\\}, |S|=k} \\min_{i,j \\in S, i \\neq j} |a_i - a_j|\n\\]\n\nBut note: in sorted order, the minimum absolute difference between any two elements in \\(S\\) is actually the minimum difference between **consecutive elements** in \\(S\\) when \\(S\\) is sorted. So if \\(S = \\{p_1, p_2, \\dots, p_k\\}\\) with \\(p_1 < p_2 < \\dots < p_k\\), then:\n\n\\[\n\\text{power}(S) = \\min_{t=1,\\dots,k-1} (a_{p_{t+1}} - a_{p_t})\n\\]\n\nSo the power is the smallest gap between consecutive picked indices in sorted `nums`.\n\n---\n\n## Step 3 — Counting approach\n\nWe can sum over all subsequences by considering:  \nFor each possible *minimum gap* \\(d\\), count how many subsequences have their minimum gap **exactly** \\(d\\), then add \\(d \\times \\text{count}\\).\n\nBut computing \"exactly \\(d\\)\" is hard. Instead, we can think:  \nFor a fixed \\(d\\), count how many subsequences have minimum gap **at least** \\(d\\). Then:\n\nLet \\(F(d)\\) = number of subsequences of length \\(k\\) with min gap \\(\\geq d\\).  \nThen the number with min gap **exactly** \\(d\\) is \\(F(d) - F(d+1)\\).  \nThen the sum of powers = \\(\\sum_{d} d \\cdot [F(d) - F(d+1)]\\).\n\nThis telescopes to:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d=1}^\\infty F(d)\n\\]\n\nWhy? Let’s check:  \n\\(\\sum_{d=1}^M d \\cdot [F(d) - F(d+1)]\\)  \n= \\(F(1) - F(2) + 2F(2) - 2F(3) + 3F(3) - 3F(4) + \\dots\\)  \n= \\(F(1) + F(2) + F(3) + \\dots + F(M) - M \\cdot F(M+1)\\).  \nIf \\(F(M+1) = 0\\) for large \\(M\\), then sum = \\(\\sum_{d=1}^\\infty F(d)\\).\n\nYes, because if \\(d > \\max(a) - \\min(a)\\), no subsequence of length ≥2 can have min gap ≥ d. So finite sum.\n\nThus:\n\n\\[\n\\text{answer} = \\sum_{d=1}^{\\infty} F(d)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(F(d)\\) = number of subsequences of length \\(k\\) with all gaps between chosen elements (in sorted order) ≥ \\(d\\).\n\n---\n\n## Step 4 — Computing \\(F(d)\\)\n\nGiven sorted array `a`, we want to count number of subsequences of length \\(k\\) where \\(a_{p_{t+1}} - a_{p_t} \\geq d\\) for all \\(t\\).\n\nThis is a classic DP:  \nLet `dp[i][j]` = number of ways to choose a subsequence of length `j` ending at index `i` (i is last chosen) with all gaps ≥ d.\n\nTransition:  \n`dp[i][1] = 1` for all i.  \nFor `j > 1`:  \n`dp[i][j] = sum_{prev < i, a[i] - a[prev] >= d} dp[prev][j-1]`.\n\nWe can compute this in \\(O(n^2 k)\\) time.\n\nThen \\(F(d) = \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} dp[i][k]\\).\n\n---\n\n## Step 5 — Range of \\(d\\)\n\nWe only need \\(d\\) from 1 up to `max(nums) - min(nums)`, but that could be up to \\(2\\times 10^8\\) — too large to loop.  \nBut note: gaps are differences between sorted elements, so possible **distinct** gap values are at most \\(n^2\\) (since only differences between two array elements matter). We can collect all possible gaps from sorted array:  \nall \\(a[j] - a[i]\\) for \\(i < j\\) are possible minimum gaps.  \nAlso, \\(F(d)\\) changes only when \\(d\\) crosses one of these gaps. So we can consider only these distinct differences as thresholds.\n\nBut we need \\(\\sum_{d=1}^\\infty F(d)\\), not just at these points. However, \\(F(d)\\) is constant between consecutive possible gap values. So if sorted unique gaps are \\(g_1 < g_2 < \\dots < g_m\\), then for \\(d \\in [g_t, g_{t+1})\\), \\(F(d) = F(g_t)\\). Then:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d=1}^\\infty F(d) = \\sum_{t=1}^{m} (g_t - g_{t-1}) \\cdot F(g_t)\n\\]\nwhere \\(g_0 = 1\\), and we consider up to \\(g_m = \\max gap\\), and \\(F(d) = 0\\) for \\(d > g_m\\).\n\nBut careful: for \\(d\\) between gaps, \\(F(d) = F(\\text{previous gap value})\\). Wait, actually \\(F(d)\\) is monotone decreasing in \\(d\\), and it steps down at each possible gap. So if we have gaps sorted: \\(g_1 < g_2 < \\dots < g_M\\), then:\n\nFor \\(d < g_1\\), \\(F(d) = F(1)\\).  \nFor \\(g_1 \\leq d < g_2\\), \\(F(d) = F(g_1)\\), etc.\n\nSo:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d=1}^\\infty F(d) = (g_1 - 1)\\cdot F(1) + (g_2 - g_1)\\cdot F(g_1) + \\dots + (g_M - g_{M-1})\\cdot F(g_{M-1}) + (\\infty - g_M)\\cdot 0\n\\]\nBut we only go up to \\(d = \\max gap\\) because beyond that \\(F=0\\). Actually, \\(F(d) = 0\\) for \\(d > \\max(a) - \\min(a)\\), but here \\(\\max gap\\) is that difference. So last term is 0.\n\nBetter: Let \\(D = \\max(a) - \\min(a)\\). Then:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d=1}^D F(d) = \\sum_{t=1}^{M} (g_t - g_{t-1}) \\cdot F(g_{t-1})\n\\]\nwhere \\(g_0 = 1\\), \\(g_{M+1} = D+1\\), and \\(F(g_0) = F(1)\\), \\(F(g_M)\\) for last interval.\n\nBut easier: just iterate \\(d\\) over all distinct gaps plus 1, compute \\(F(d)\\), and sum over intervals.\n\n---\n\nGiven \\(n \\leq 50\\), we can afford to iterate \\(d\\) over all distinct gaps (O(n^2) of them) and compute DP for each \\(d\\) in O(n^2 k). Total O(n^4 k) with n=50, k≤50 is too big (50^4 * 50 huge). But n^2 k * n^2 = 50^4 * 50 ≈ 3e9 operations — too large.\n\nWe need optimization:  \nWe can note that DP for given \\(d\\) is like counting increasing subsequences with step ≥ d. We can preprocess for each i the list of prev with diff ≥ d using binary search to make it O(n k log n) per d. Then total O(n^3 k log n) maybe acceptable? 50^3 * 50 * log ≈ 3e6 * 6 = 2e7, fine.\n\n---\n\n## Step 6 — Modular arithmetic\n\nAll counts modulo \\(10^9+7\\).\n\n---\n\n## Step 7 — Implementation plan\n\n1. Sort `nums`.\n2. Get all distinct gaps from sorted array (differences a[j] - a[i] for i < j).\n3. Add `1` to list of gaps if not present (since we start from d=1).\n4. Sort gaps, include also `max_gap+1` as sentinel.\n5. For each gap value \\(d\\) in sorted gaps, compute \\(F(d)\\) via DP:\n   - `dp[i][j]` = ways to choose subsequence length j ending at i with all steps ≥ d.\n   - Use prefix sums to compute quickly.\n6. Sum over intervals:  \n   For consecutive gap values `prev_d` and `curr_d`, interval length = `curr_d - prev_d`, contribution = `interval_length * F(prev_d)`.\n7. Output total mod \\(10^9+7\\).\n\n---\n\nHere’s the Python implementation:\n\n```python\ndef sumOfPowers(nums, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    nums.sort()\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    # collect all possible gaps\n    gaps = set()\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i+1, n):\n            gaps.add(nums[j] - nums[i])\n    gaps = sorted(gaps)\n    # we need from d=1 to max gap\n    if 1 not in gaps:\n        gaps.append(1)\n    gaps.sort()\n    \n    # We also need max_gap+1 to mark end\n    max_gap = nums[-1] - nums[0]\n    if max_gap + 1 not in gaps:\n        gaps.append(max_gap + 1)\n    \n    gaps.sort()\n    \n    # function to compute F(d) = number of subsequences length k with min gap >= d\n    def count_min_gap_at_least(d):\n        # dp[i][j] = number of subsequences length j ending at i\n        dp = [[0]*(k+1) for _ in range(n)]\n        for i in range(n):\n            dp[i][1] = 1\n        \n        # prefix sums for optimization: pref[j-1][idx] = sum dp[0..idx][j-1]\n        # but we need previous indices with nums[i] - nums[prev] >= d\n        # for each i, find smallest prev such that nums[i] - nums[prev] >= d\n        # then sum dp[prev..i-1][j-1]\n        # we can maintain prefix sums for each j\n        pref = [[0]*n for _ in range(k+1)]\n        for i in range(n):\n            pref[1][i] = (pref[1][i-1] + dp[i][1]) % MOD\n        \n        for j in range(2, k+1):\n            pref[j-1][0] = dp[0][j-1]\n            for i in range(1, n):\n                pref[j-1][i] = (pref[j-1][i-1] + dp[i][j-1]) % MOD\n            \n            for i in range(n):\n                # find largest prev index with nums[i] - nums[prev] >= d\n                # binary search\n                lo, hi = 0, i\n                while lo < hi:\n                    mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n                    if nums[i] - nums[mid] >= d:\n                        lo = mid + 1\n                    else:\n                        hi = mid\n                # lo is first index where diff < d, so valid prev are 0..lo-1\n                first_invalid = lo\n                if first_invalid > 0:\n                    dp[i][j] = pref[j-1][first_invalid - 1]\n        \n        total = 0\n        for i in range(n):\n            total = (total + dp[i][k]) % MOD\n        return total\n    \n    total_sum = 0\n    for idx in range(len(gaps)-1):\n        d = gaps[idx]\n        next_d = gaps[idx+1]\n        if d > max_gap:\n            break\n        Fd = count_min_gap_at_least(d)\n        interval_len = next_d - d\n        total_sum = (total_sum + Fd * interval_len) % MOD\n    \n    return total_sum\n```",
