           u = u // 2\n            d += 1\n        # Wait. In the loop update:\n        # `curr_child = u`. Here `u` is old u.\n        # `u` is updated to `u // 2`.\n        # Next iteration, `curr_child` is the old `u`.\n        # This matches logic (since old u was child of new u).\n        # However, `curr_child` is needed to identify S.\n        # In iteration 1: `curr_child` is X. `u` is X//2. Correct.\n        # In iteration 2: `curr_child` becomes X//2. `u` becomes X//4. Correct.\n        # So this logic works.\n        \n        # Wait. One subtle point. `count_descendants(u)` logic for `d=K`.\n        # If `d=K`, `u` is added.\n        # Is `u` counted in `count_descendants(S, K-d-1)`?\n        # `K-d-1` would be -1. No.\n        # Is `u` counted in `count_descendants(X, K)`?\n        # `u` is ancestor, not descendant of X. No.\n        # So manual `ans += 1` is correct and sufficient.\n\n        results.append(str(ans))\n    \n    print('\\n'.join(results))\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    solve()\n```",
