    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_25",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given two 0-indexed integer arrays, cost and time, of size n representing the costs and the time taken to paint n different walls respectively. There are two painters available:\n\nA paid painter that paints the i^th wall in time[i] units of time and takes cost[i] units of money.\nA free painter that paints any wall in 1 unit of time at a cost of 0. But the free painter can only be used if the paid painter is already occupied.\n\nReturn the minimum amount of money required to paint the n walls.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: cost = [1,2,3,2], time = [1,2,3,2]\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: The walls at index 0 and 1 will be painted by the paid painter, and it will take 3 units of time; meanwhile, the free painter will paint the walls at index 2 and 3, free of cost in 2 units of time. Thus, the total cost is 1 + 2 = 3.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: cost = [2,3,4,2], time = [1,1,1,1]\nOutput: 4\nExplanation: The walls at index 0 and 3 will be painted by the paid painter, and it will take 2 units of time; meanwhile, the free painter will paint the walls at index 1 and 2, free of cost in 2 units of time. Thus, the total cost is 2 + 2 = 4.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= cost.length <= 500\ncost.length == time.length\n1 <= cost[i] <= 10^6\n1 <= time[i] <= 500",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the minimum cost to paint all walls using two painters:\n\n1. Paid painter: costs `cost[i]` and takes `time[i]` units of time for wall i\n2. Free painter: costs 0 but can only work when the paid painter is occupied, and takes 1 unit of time per wall\n\nThe key insight is that the free painter can paint at most as many walls as the total time the paid painter works. If the paid painter works for T total units of time, the free painter can paint up to T walls.\n\nThis suggests a dynamic programming approach where:\n- I decide which walls to assign to the paid painter\n- The remaining walls can be painted by the free painter, but only if the paid painter's total time >= number of free walls\n\nLet me use DP where `dp[i][j]` represents the minimum cost to paint the first i walls, with the paid painter having a net \"time credit\" of j (paid time - free walls painted).\n\n```python\ndef paintWalls(cost, time):\n    n = len(cost)\n    \n    # dp[i][j] = minimum cost to paint first i walls with net time credit j\n    # Net time credit = total paid painter time - total free painter walls\n    # We need net time credit >= 0 at the end\n    # Maximum possible time credit is sum of all times (at most 500*500 = 250000)\n    # But we can limit it to n since we can't paint more than n free walls\n    \n    max_credit = n  # At most n free walls possible\n    min_credit = -n  # Could potentially have negative credit temporarily\n    \n    # Initialize DP table with infinity\n    # Use offset to handle negative indices\n    offset = n\n    dp = [[float('inf')] * (2 * max_credit + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    \n    # Base case: 0 walls painted, 0 cost, 0 credit\n    dp[0][offset] = 0\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        for credit in range(-max_credit, max_credit + 1):\n            credit_idx = credit + offset\n            if dp[i][credit_idx] == float('inf'):\n                continue\n                \n            current_cost = dp[i][credit_idx]\n            \n            # Option 1: Use paid painter for wall i\n            # Cost increases by cost[i], credit increases by time[i]\n            new_credit_paid = credit + time[i]\n            if -max_credit <= new_credit_paid <= max_credit:\n                new_credit_idx = new_credit_paid + offset\n                dp[i + 1][new_credit_idx] = min(dp[i + 1][new_credit_idx], current_cost + cost[i])\n            \n            # Option 2: Use free painter for wall i\n            # Cost remains same, credit decreases by 1 (since we're using 1 unit of time credit)\n            new_credit_free = credit - 1\n            if -max_credit <= new_credit_free <= max_credit:\n                new_credit_idx = new_credit_free + offset\n                dp[i + 1][new_credit_idx] = min(dp[i + 1][new_credit_idx], current_cost)\n    \n    # Find minimum cost among all states with non-negative credit (credit >= 0)\n    result = float('inf')\n    for credit in range(0, max_credit + 1):\n        result = min(result, dp[n][credit + offset])\n    \n    return result\n```",
