    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The problem asks us to find the number of ways to divide $N$ players into $T$ teams such that:\n1. Each player belongs to exactly one team.\n2. Each team has at least one player.\n3. If two players are an incompatible pair, they cannot be in the same team.\n4. Two divisions are considered different if any player's team assignment is different. Based on the sample cases (specifically Sample 3 where output matches the Stirling number of the second kind and Sample 1 output logic), the teams are effectively **unlabeled**. That is, we are counting the number of partitions of the set of players into $T$ non-empty independent sets (where an independent set means no two sets contain incompatible pairs). However, Sample 1 output is 4, and manual calculation shows 8 labeled valid partitions (since teams are distinguishable $1..T$), but 4 unlabeled (since we divide by 2). Sample 3 output 65 is exactly $S(6, 4)$. This confirms we need the number of unlabeled partitions into $T$ independent sets.\n\nWait, actually, looking closely at Sample 1:\nSample 1 Output 4 is correct for unlabeled teams.\nIf we have 2 valid partitions into independent sets, say $\\{S_1, S_2\\}$.\nSince labels are swapped, it might be the same partition.\nWait, my derivation was: \"There are 8 labeled colorings -> 4 unlabeled partitions\".\nLet's verify the sample 4 again. $N=10, T=6$. Output 8001.\nIf it was labeled, the number would likely be much larger. With unlabeled, 8001 fits well with the scale of Bell numbers restricted by constraints.\nAlso, the phrasing \"divide the players into T teams\" often implies unlabeled in combinatorics unless \"labeled\" or \"ordered\" is specified, especially given the context of graph partitions where usually we look for number of cliques or independent sets partitions.\nSo I will calculate the number of ways to partition $V$ into $T$ non-empty independent sets.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Use Dynamic Programming with bitmask to count the number of partitions into independent sets.\n2. Precompute which submasks are independent sets (no edges inside).\n3. Let $dp[mask][k]$ be the number of ways to partition the subset of vertices `mask` into `k` independent sets.\n   To avoid overcounting (since sets are unordered), we enforce that each set in the partition must contain the vertex with the lowest index present in the current mask.\n   This means when considering a mask, we iterate over submasks `sub` that contain the lowest bit of `mask` and are independent. Then we add $dp[mask \\setminus sub][k-1]$ to $dp[mask][k]$.\n4. The answer is $dp[(1 \\ll N) - 1][T]$.\n\nConstraints: $N \\le 10$, $T \\le N$. The complexity will be roughly $O(3^N \\cdot T)$, which is well within limits for $N=10$.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n\n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n        T = int(next(iterator))\n        M = int(next(iterator))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    adj = [0] * N\n    for _ in range(M):\n        u = int(next(iterator)) - 1\n        v = int(next(iterator)) - 1\n        adj[u] |= (1 << v)\n        adj[v] |= (1 << u)\n\n    # Precompute independent masks\n    # A mask is independent if for every bit set, no other bit set is its neighbor.\n    # Since edges are symmetric, if u and v are both in mask, then u is neighbor to v.\n    # We can check by verifying for every u in mask, (adj[u] & mask) == 0 is NOT sufficient\n    # because we only check neighbors of u. If u has neighbor v, then adj[u] has v.\n    # So (adj[u] & mask) will be non-zero.\n    # But we need to ensure NO pair has an edge.\n    # If we check every u in mask, and adj[u] & mask is empty? No.\n    # If (adj[u] & mask) is not empty, it implies u has a neighbor in mask.\n    # So yes, if for ALL u in mask, (adj[u] & mask) has common bits, we need to ensure\n    # that for ALL pairs (u, v) in mask, u is NOT neighbor of v.\n    # If there is an edge (u, v) with both in mask.\n    # Then adj[u] has bit v, adj[v] has bit u.\n    # So (adj[u] & mask) will have bit v set (non-zero).\n    # So if for ANY u in mask, (adj[u] & mask) is non-zero?\n    # No. Because if we have edge u-v. Then adj[u] & mask has v. Non-zero.\n    # Wait, is it possible for one endpoint to fail and other not?\n    # adj[u] has v. adj[v] has u. Both non-zero.\n    # So if ANY u in mask has (adj[u] & mask) != 0, then edge exists?\n    # Yes, assuming no self loops.\n    # Let's verify:\n    # u in mask. adj[u] contains neighbors. If v in mask is neighbor, then v is in adj[u].\n    # So adj[u] & mask includes v. So it is != 0.\n    # So yes, (adj[u] & mask) != 0 implies edge exists for u.\n    # So we just need to find if ANY u in mask satisfies (adj[u] & mask) != 0?\n    # Wait, if adj[u] contains v (u's neighbor).\n    # If u is NOT in adj[u].\n    # If mask contains u and v.\n    # u has neighbor v. adj[u] has v.\n    # Then (adj[u] & mask) contains v. Non-zero.\n    # So yes, if (adj[u] & mask) != 0 for ANY u in mask, it means u has a neighbor in mask.\n    # But we must be careful. (adj[u] & mask) != 0 means u has neighbor in mask.\n    # Does this mean we have an edge? Yes.\n    # Is it possible for an independent set to have (adj[u] & mask) != 0?\n    # If set is independent, no edges. So u has no neighbors in mask.\n    # So (adj[u] & mask) must be 0.\n    # Wait.\n    # Check: u=0, v=1. Edge 0-1.\n    # Mask {0, 1}.\n    # adj[0] = 1. adj[1] = 1.\n    # adj[0] & mask = 0 & 2+1 = 1. Nonzero.\n    # adj[1] & mask = 2 & 2+1 = 2. Nonzero.\n    # So checking (adj[u] & mask) != 0 for any u finds edge.\n    # Correct check: `indep = True`, iterate u where `(mask >> u) & 1`. if `(adj[u] & mask) == 0` is NOT the condition.\n    # The condition is `(adj[u] & mask) == 0`.\n    # If `adj[u] & mask` has any bit set, it means u has neighbor in mask.\n    # So if `(adj[u