nitely not contain that lowest bit.\n    # So mask ^ s < mask is guaranteed numerically?\n    # Actually, if mask has bit 0 set. mask ^ s removes bit 0.\n    # Since bit 0 is value 1. Any number with bit 0 removed is strictly smaller?\n    # Yes, because bit 0 contributes 1 to value. Removing it decreases value.\n    # So iterating mask from 0 to full_mask works.\n    \n    for mask in range(1, 1 << N):\n        # Find lowest bit\n        # mask & -mask gives lowest bit as integer\n        first = mask & -mask\n        \n        # Iterate submasks s of (mask ^ first)\n        # These will be combined with first to form the set covering `first`.\n        # Let the set be `sub`. `sub` will contain `first`.\n        # We must check if `sub` is independent.\n        \n        # Submask iteration loop\n        others = mask ^ first\n        submask = others\n        while True:\n            # submask is current 's' inside others\n            curr_sub = first | submask\n            \n            if independent[curr_sub]:\n                remaining = mask ^ curr_sub\n                \n                # Propagate from dp[remaining]\n                # dp[mask][k] += dp[remaining][k-1]\n                # We iterate k from 1 to T\n                # We can iterate downwards to avoid overwriting?\n                # Wait, we are reading from dp[remaining], not dp[mask].\n                # dp[remaining] is already computed since remaining < mask.\n                # So we can iterate k.\n                \n                # Optimization: iterate only valid k in remaining\n                # range of k in remaining is up to popcount(remaining).\n                # Also we need k-1 < T.\n                \n                # Since T is small, simple loop is fine.\n                # But we must access dp[remaining] validly.\n                \n                # We need to copy values.\n                prev_dp = dp[remaining]\n                curr_vals = dp[mask]\n                \n         