    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the minimum cost to make all elements equal using two operations:\n1. Increase one element by 1 at cost `cost1`\n2. Increase two different elements by 1 at cost `cost2`\n\nKey insights:\n1. All elements must become equal to some target value, which should be at least the maximum element in the array.\n2. For any target value ≥ max(nums), I need to calculate the total \"deficit\" (how much each element needs to increase).\n3. The optimal strategy depends on the relationship between `cost1` and `cost2`:\n   - If `2 * cost1 ≤ cost2`, it's always better to use single increments\n   - If `2 * cost1 > cost2`, it's better to use double increments when possible, but I need to handle odd deficits\n\nThe minimum cost will occur when all elements are raised to the maximum value in the array (or possibly higher if there are special cases with odd counts).\n\n```python\ndef minCostToEqualize(nums, cost1, cost2):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    if n == 1:\n        return 0\n    \n    max_val = max(nums)\n    \n    # Calculate total deficit (sum of how much each element needs to increase)\n    total_deficit = sum(max_val - num for num in nums)\n    \n    # If cost2 >= 2 * cost1, always use single increments\n    if cost2 >= 2 * cost1:\n        return (total_deficit * cost1) % MOD\n    \n    # If cost2 < 2 * cost1, try to use as many double increments as possible\n    # But we need to check if we can achieve the target optimally\n    \n    # The key insight: we want to minimize cost when using the cheaper per-element operation\n    # Double increment costs cost2 for 2 elements, so per-element cost is cost2/2\n    # Single increment costs cost1 per element\n    \n    # Since cost2 < 2 * cost1, double increments are cheaper per element\n    # But we can only use double increments on pairs\n    \n    # We should use as many double increments as possible\n    # The number of double increments we can use is limited by total_deficit // 2\n    # But we need to ensure we can actually pair up the increments\n    \n    # Actually, the optimal target might be higher than max_val if we have odd total deficit\n    # Let's consider making all elements equal to max_val\n    \n    # With target = max_val:\n    # We need to distribute total_deficit increments across n positions\n    # Maximum number of double operations = total_deficit // 2\n    # Remaining increments = total_deficit % 2\n    \n    double_ops = total_deficit // 2\n    single_ops = total_deficit % 2\n    \n    cost = (double_ops * cost2 + single_ops * cost1) % MOD\n    \n    return cost\n```",
