      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef count_subarrays(nums):\n    \"\"\"\n    Count subarrays where the first and last elements equal the largest element in the subarray.\n    \n    A subarray is valid if:\n    - nums[start] == nums[end]\n    - All elements between start and end (inclusive) are <= nums[start]\n    \n    Time Complexity: O(n)\n    Space Complexity: O(n)\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    if n <= 1:\n        return n\n    \n    # Array to store the position of the last element greater than current element\n    last_greater = [-1] * n\n    \n    # Monotonic stack to process elements and find previous greater elements\n    stack = []  # Stack stores indices\n    for i in range(n):\n        # Pop elements smaller or equal to current\n        while stack and nums[stack[-1]] <= nums[i]:\n            stack.pop()\n        if stack:\n            # The closest greater element to the left\n            last_greater[i] = stack[-1]\n        # Push current index to stack\n        stack.append(i)\n    \n    result = 0\n    \n    # For each element, count valid subarrays starting at this position\n    for i in range(n):\n        value = nums[i]\n        \n        # Find how many times this value appears between\n        # last_greater[i] + 1 and i - 1 (inclusive of i for current occurrence)\n        start_of_range = last_greater[i] + 1\n        \n        # Count consecutive occurrences of value from i downwards in the valid range\n        occurrences = 1  # Start with current position\n        j = i - 1\n        while j >= start_of_range and nums[j] == value:\n            occurrences += 1\n            j -= 1\n        # Each earlier occurrence forms a valid subarray with current position\n        result += (occurrences - 1)\n    \n    return result\n```",
