    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to count the number of alternating subarrays in a binary array.\n\nAn alternating subarray has no two adjacent elements with the same value. This means elements must alternate between 0 and 1.\n\nMy approach:\n1. For each position, I'll extend the alternating subarray as far as possible\n2. If I have a contiguous alternating segment of length `k`, it contributes `k * (k + 1) / 2` subarrays (since every subarray within this segment is alternating)\n3. When I encounter two adjacent same elements, I need to start a new segment\n\nLet me trace through the examples:\n- Example 1: `[0,1,1,1]` - alternating segments are `[0,1]` (length 2) and then three single `[1]` elements\n- Example 2: `[1,0,1,0]` - one alternating segment of length 4, giving 4*5/2 = 10 subarrays\n\n```python\ndef countAlternatingSubarrays(nums):\n    if not nums:\n        return 0\n    \n    total = 0\n    i = 0\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    while i < n:\n        # Start a new alternating segment at position i\n        j = i\n        # Extend the segment as long as it remains alternating\n        while j + 1 < n and nums[j] != nums[j + 1]:\n            j += 1\n        \n        # Segment from i to j has length (j - i + 1)\n        length = j - i + 1\n        # Number of subarrays in this segment is length * (length + 1) / 2\n        total += length * (length + 1) // 2\n        \n        # Move to the next position (note: we don't skip because single elements count)\n        i = j + 1\n    \n    return total\n```",
