      "generated_answer": "We need to count permutations where adjacent elements are divisible by each other.  \nSince `n` is ≤ 14, we can use dynamic programming with bitmasking.\n\n**Key idea:**  \nLet `dp[mask][last]` = number of ways to arrange the chosen elements (represented by `mask`) with the last element being `nums[last]`.\n\nWe’ll iterate over all possible last elements and try to append a new element that satisfies the divisibility condition.\n\nHere’s the function:\n\n```python\ndef specialPerm(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    MOD = 10**9 + р7\n    \n    # Precompute divisibility matrix\n    divisible = [[False] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(n):\n            if i != j and (nums[i] % nums[j] == 0 or nums[j] % nums[i] == 0):\n                divisible[i][j] = True\n    \n    # dp[mask][last]\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(1 << n)]\n    \n    # Initialize: starting with one element\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[1 << i][i] = 1\n    \n    for mask in range(1 << n):\n        for last in range(n):\n            if not (mask & (1 << last)):\n                continue\n            # Try to add a new element\n            for next in range(n):\n                if mask & (1 << next):\n                    continue  # already used\n                if not divisible[last][next]:\n                    continue\n                new_mask = mask | (1 << next)\n                dp[new_mask][next] += dp[mask][last]\n                dp[new_mask][next] %= MOD\n    \n    full_mask = (1 << n) - 1\n    total = 0\n    for last in range(n):\n        total += dp[full_mask][last]\n    return total % MOD\n```",
