ive in mismatch list. Then answer = `dp[m]`.\n\nWait, but what if x is small, maybe better to pair non-consecutive mismatches in list? Example: mismatches at [1,3,4,6], x=2. Pair (1,3) cost min(2,2)=2, pair (4,6) cost min(2,2)=2, total 4. If pair consecutively: (1,3)=2, (4,6)=2, same. Actually same. Try [1,10,11,20], x=5: consecutive pairing: (1,10)=5, (11,20)=5 total 10. Non-consecutive: (1,11)=5, (10,20)=5 total 10 same. So maybe consecutive in mismatch list is fine. But is it always optimal? Possibly yes because distance in original array is larger for non-consecutive, so min(x, dist) will be x more often. But maybe x=1, then cost =1 for any pairing using operation A, so any pairing fine. Then consecutive is fine too. So maybe we can just pair consecutively in mismatch list.\n\nBut careful: Example: positions [1,2,100,101], x=50. Consecutive pairing: (1,2)=1, (100,101)=1 total 2. Non-consecutive: (1,100)=50, (2,101)=50 total 100 worse. So consecutive better. So pairing consecutively in mismatch list seems good. But is it always optimal? Actually, maybe not: Example: [1,50,51,100], x=30. Consecutive: (1,50)=30, (51,100)=30 total 60. Non-consecutive: (1,51)=30, (50,100)=30 total 60 same. So maybe it doesn’t matter. So we can just pair in order.\n\nThus, DP is straightforward.\n\n---\n\n## Step 6 — Handling odd mismatches\n\nIf m odd, impossible → -1.\n\n---\n\n## Step 7 — Implementation plan\n\n1. Get mismatches list.\n2. If odd length, return -1.\n3. DP: `dp[0]=0`, `dp[i]` = min cost for first i mismatches.\n4. `dp[i] = dp[i-2] + min(x, p[i-1] - p[i-2])`.\n5. Answer = `dp[m]`.\n\n---\n\n## Step 8 — Writing the function\n\n```python\ndef min_cost_to_make_equal(s1, s2, x):\n    n = len(s1)\n    mismatches = []\n    for i in range(n):\n        if s1[i] != s2[i]:\n            mismatches.append(i)\n    m = len(mismatches)\n    if m % 2 == 1:\n        return -1\n    \n    if m == 0:\n        return 0\n    \n    # dp[i] = min cost for first i mismatches, i even\n    dp =