      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we need to generate all possible non-empty substrings of a given string and count the unique ones. We can achieve this by using a set to store the substrings, ensuring that each substring is counted only once. Here's the Python function that accomplishes this:\n\n```python\ndef count_unique_substrings():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    S = input().strip()\n    \n    unique_substrings = set()\n    \n    # Iterate over all possible starting points of substrings\n    for start in range(len(S)):\n        # Generate and store each possible substring\n        for end in range(start + 1, len(S) + 1):\n            substring = S[start:end]\n            unique_substrings.add(substring)\n    \n    # The number of unique substrings is the size of the set\n    print(len(unique_substrings))\n\n# Call the function\ncount_unique_substrings()\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- We first read the input string `S`.\n- We use a set `unique_substrings` to store all unique substrings. This automatically handles duplicates as sets only store unique items.\n- We use a nested loop where:\n  - The outer loop `for start in range(len(S))` iterates over all possible starting positions of substrings.\n  - The inner loop `for end in range(start + 1, len(S) + 1)` iterates over possible ending positions, ensuring that each substring from `start` to `end` is considered.\n- For each pair of `start` and `end`, we extract the substring `S[start:end]` and add it to the set `unique_substrings`.\n- Finally, we print the size of the set, which represents the number of unique non-empty substrings.",
