      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque\n\n# Increase recursion depth just in case, though we use iterative BFS\nsys.setrecursionlimit(2000000)\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input at once\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n        M = int(next(iterator))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    sets = []\n    # sets_containing_element[v] stores the list of set indices that contain integer v.\n    # v ranges from 1 to M.\n    sets_containing_element = [[] for _ in range(M + 1)]\n    \n    start_sets = []\n    target_sets = []\n    \n    # Parse sets\n    for i in range(N):\n        try:\n            A = int(next(iterator))\n            s_members = []\n            contains_1 = False\n            contains_M = False\n            \n            for _ in range(A):\n                val = int(next(iterator))\n                s_members.append(val)\n                if val == 1:\n                    contains_1 = True\n                if val == M:\n                    contains_M = True\n            \n            sets.append(s_members)\n            \n            if contains_1:\n                start_sets.append(i)\n            if contains_M:\n                target_sets.append(i)\n            \n            for val in s_members:\n                sets_containing_element[val].append(i)\n        except StopIteration:\n            break\n    \n    # Check for intersection (if any set contains both 1 and M)\n    # We use sets for O(1) lookup\n    set_start_indices = set(start_sets)\n    set_target_indices = set(target_sets)\n    intersection = set_start_indices & set_target_indices\n    \n    if intersection:\n        print(0)\n        return\n        \n    if not target_sets:\n        print(-1)\n        return\n        \n    # BFS initialization\n    # Nodes 0..N-1 represent sets\n    # Nodes N..N+M-1 represent elements 1..M\n    # Element x (1-based) maps to index N + x - 1\n    \n    dist = [-1] * (N + M)\n    q = deque()\n    \n    for s_idx in start_sets:\n        dist[s_idx] = 0\n        q.append(s_idx)\n        \n    # BFS\n    while q:\n        u = q.popleft()\n        d_u = dist[u]\n        \n        # Check if we reach a target set\n        # This check is done when we add Set neighbors of an Element node.\n        # We also need to check if Start sets are targets, but handled above.\n        # Since we checked intersection, if we find a target set, its distance will be >= 2 (or even).\n        \n        if u < N:\n            # u is a Set node\n            # Expand to Element neighbors\n            s_list = sets[u]\n            for element_val in s_list:\n                v = N + element_val - 1\n                if dist[v] == -1:\n                    dist[v] = d_u + 1\n                    q.append(v)\n        else:\n            # u is an Element node\n            # Expand to Set neighbors\n            # element_val = u - N + 1\n            element_val = u - N + 1\n            for s_idx in sets_containing_element[element_val]:\n                if dist[s_idx] == -1:\n                    dist[s_idx] = d_u + 1\n                    q.append(s_idx)\n                    # Check if this set is a target\n                    if s_idx in set_target_indices:\n                        print(dist[s_idx] // 2)\n                        return\n                        \n    print(-1)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    solve()\n```\nOne minor optimization:\n`sets` can be stored as list of lists.\nThe BFS part:\nInside the loop, I use `sets_containing_element` which is list of lists.\nWait. `in set_target_indices` check is O(1).\nIs it possible to optimize?\nIf `start_sets` or `target_sets` is empty, handle.\nThe code handles it.\nBut `sets` list might be large.\nWait. I am storing `sets` as a list of lists.\nThe BFS loop processes nodes.\nThe memory usage of `sets` and `sets_c