mably non-negative.\nIf $A_i=0$, loop `for _ in range(A)` doesn't run.\n`s_members` empty.\n`sets[u]` empty.\nBFS works fine.\nIf $A_i=0$, then element loop in BFS does nothing.\nSo logic holds.\n\nOne minor point: `dist` initialization.\n`-1 * (N+M)`.\nWait. `dist` is list.\n`size * [value]`?\n`[-1] * size` works in Python.\n\nLooks good.\n\nIs it possible to optimize `set_target_indices` lookup?\nIt's a set. Lookup is O(1).\nIt's correct.\n\nThe solution is ready.\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque\n\n# Increase recursion depth just in case\nsys.setrecursionlimit(2000000)\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n        M = int(next(iterator))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    sets = []\n    # sets_containing_element[v] stores the list of set indices that contain integer v.\n    # v ranges from 1 to M.\n    sets_containing_element = [[] for _ in range(M + 1)]\n    \n    start_sets = []\n    target_sets = []\n    \n    # Parse sets\n    for i in range(N):\n        try:\n            A = int(next(iterator))\n            s_members = []\n            contains_1 = False\n            contains_M = False\n            \n            for _ in range(A):\n                val = int(next(iterator))\n                s_members.append(val)\n                if val == 1:\n                    contains_1 = True\n                if val == M:\n                    contains_M = True\n            \n            sets.append(s_members)\n            \n            if contains_1:\n                start_sets.append(i)\n            if contains_M:\n                target_sets.append(i)\n            \n            for val in s_members:\n                sets_containing_element[val].append(i)\n        except StopIteration:\n            break\n    \n    # Check for intersection (if any set contains 