le index, so 4 intervals, but we got 8 partitions, not 1.\n\nSo my earlier logic is wrong — we’re allowed to **merge** adjacent intervals into a larger subarray if they don’t share numbers.  \nBut wait, in example 1, intervals don’t overlap, so each is length 1. We can group them freely as long as we don’t separate same numbers (here no repeats).  \nSo the count = number of ways to split into subarrays with no restriction except order.\n\nBut with repeats, intervals overlap, forcing some indices to be in same subarray.\n\n---\n\nBetter approach:  \nWe find for each index `i` the last occurrence of `nums[i]`.  \nWe extend our current partition end to cover all last occurrences of all elements in current subarray.  \nWhen `i` reaches the current partition end, we can end a subarray.\n\nLet’s define:\n- `last[pos]` = last index where `nums[pos]` occurs.\n- We iterate `i = 0..n-1`, keeping `max_last = max(max_last, last[nums[i]])`.\n- If `i == max_last`, then we can end a subarray here.\n\nBut number of partitions:  \nWe have `m` such end points (where `i == max_last`),  \nwhich means we have `m` subarrays in that partition.  \nBut different partitions come from choosing to end a subarray earlier or later?  \nActually, if `i == max_last` is forced, we must end there. So no choice.  \nSo only one partition? Again mismatch with example 1.\n\n---\n\nI realize: In example 1, `last` for each number is same as index (no repeats).  \nThen `max_last` updates to `i` each time, so `i == max_last` at each `i`.  \nSo we can end a subarray at each index.  \nThat means: at each index i (from 0 to n-2), we can choose to cut or not.  \nThat’s `2^(n-1)` partitions. For n=4, that’s 8 — matches example 1.\n\nSo formula:  \nWe find segments where we must keep together (due to overlaps of intervals),  \nbetween these segments we can cut or not cut.  \nIf we have `k` segments, then between them there are `k-1` gaps, each gap we can cut or not → `2^(k-1)` partitions.\n\n---\n\n**Algorithm:**\n1. Find last occurrence index for each value.\n2. Traverse array, maintain current segment end `end`.\n3. Start new segment at `start`, extend `end` to max last occurrence of elements in current segment.\n4. When `i == end`, segment ends. Count segments.\n5. If we have `seg` segments, answer = `2^(seg-1) mod (1e9+7)`.\n\nWait, check example 3:  \nnums = [1,2,1,3]  \nlast[1]=2, last[2]=1, last[3]=3.  \ni=0, end=max(end,2)=2  \ni=1, end=max(2,1)=2  \ni=2, end=2 → segment 0..2, i=2 ends.  \ni=3, end=max(2,3)=3 → i=3 ends.  \nSegments = [0..2], [3..3] → 2 segments.  \n`2^(2-1) = 2` → matches example output.\n\nExample 2:  \nnums = [1,1,1,1]  \nlast[1]=3.  \ni=0, end=3  \ni=1, end=3  \ni=2, end=3  \ni=3, end=3 → one segment [0..3].  \n`2^(1-1)=1` → matches.\n\n---\n\nSo formula is consistent.\n\n---\n\n**Complexity:** O(n) time, O(n) space for last index map.\n\n---\n\n**Implementation:**\n\n```python\ndef numberOfGoodPartitions(nums):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    last_occurrence = {}\n    \n    # Store last index of each value\n    for i, num in enumerate(nums):\n        last_occurrence[num] = i\n    \n    segments = 0\n    end = 0\n    i = 0\n    \n    while i < len(nums):\n        end = max(end, last_occurrence[nums[i]])\n        if i == end:\n            segments += 1\n        i += 1\n    \n    # Number of ways to combine segments = 2^(segments-1) mod MOD\n    result = pow(2, segments - 1, MOD)\n    return result\n```",
