      "generated_answer": "We can think of this as a prefix sum problem where we track the count of elements satisfying `nums[i] % modulo == k` as we iterate through the array.  \n\nLet’s define `prefix_count` as the number of indices `j` in `[0..i]` where `nums[j] % modulo == k`.  \nFor a subarray `[l..r]`,  \n```\ncnt = prefix_count[r+1] - prefix_count[l]\n```\nWe want:\n```\n(prefix_count[r+1] - prefix_count[l]) % modulo == k\n```\nThat means:\n```\nprefix_count[r+1] % modulo - prefix_count[l] % modulo ≡ k (mod modulo)\n```\nSo:\n```\nprefix_count[r+1] % modulo - k ≡ prefix_count[l] % modulo (mod modulo)\n```\nOr equivalently:\n```\n(prefix_count[r+1] - k) % modulo ≡ prefix_count[l] % modulo\n```\nThus, as we iterate, we can track how many previous prefix counts (mod modulo) match `(current_prefix_count - k) % modulo`.\n\nWe must be careful with modulo arithmetic when dealing with negative numbers.\n\nHere’s the implementation:\n\n```python\ndef countInterestingSubarrays(nums, modulo, k):\n    n = len(nums)\n    # Transform nums into 1 if nums[i] % modulo == k else 0\n    arr = [1 if x % modulo == k else 0 for x in nums]\n    \n    prefix_count = 0\n    # freq[mod_value] = number of prefix counts with that mod_value\n    freq = {0: 1}  # prefix_count 0 appears once at start\n    result = 0\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        prefix_count += arr[i]\n        # We want (prefix_count - prefix_prev) % modulo == k\n        # => prefix_prev % modulo == (prefix_count - k) % modulo\n        target_mod = (prefix_count - k) % modulo\n        result += freq.get(target_mod, 0)\n        \n        current_mod = prefix_count % modulo\n        freq[current_mod] = freq.get(current_mod, 0) + 1\n    \n    return result\n```",
