    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9076",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Two expressions E and F are said to be unifiable if there are substitutions for the variables of E and F that make the expressions lexically identical. In the following three expressions, only w, x, y, and z are variables.\nI. f(w,w)\nII. f(x,1)\nIII. f(y,g(z))\nWhich pairs of these expressions is (are) pairs of unifiable expressions?\n\nOptions: \nA. (I, III) and (II, III) only\nB. (II, III) only\nC. (I, II) and (I, III) only\nD. (I, II) only\nE. Only single expressions are unifiable, not pairs\nF. None of the pairs are unifiable\nG. (I, II), (I, III), and (II, III)\nH. All three expressions are unifiable\nI. (I, II) and (II, III) only\nJ. (I, III) only\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Expression I is f(w,w), so both arguments must be the same after substitution. Expression II is f(x,1), where one argument is a constant 1. For I and II to unify, w would have to be both x and 1, but then both arguments in I become 1, while in II, x can be 1 — this works only if w=x=1, so (I, II) is unifiable. Expression III is f(y,g(z)), and (I, III) requires w = y and w = g(z), so y = g(z), which is possible — so (I, III) unifies. (II, III): x = y and 1 = g(z) — but 1 cannot equal complex term g(z), so not unifiable. Thus only (I, II) and (I, III) are unifiable.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
