    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9464",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following pairs of 8-bit, two's-complement numbers will result in overflow when the members of the pairs are added?\n\nOptions: \nA. 11110000, 00001111\nB. 01111111, 10000000\nC. 00000001, 10000000\nD. 10000001, 10101010\nE. 11111111, 00000001\nF. 11111111, 10000001\nG. 00000000, 10000000\nH. 10000001, 00000001\nI. 10101010, 11111111\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To detect overflow in two's complement addition, overflow occurs if adding two numbers with the same sign (both positive or both negative) yields a result with the opposite sign.  \n\nLooking at the pairs:  \n- **B (01111111 = +127, 10000000 = −128)** have opposite signs, so overflow should not occur.  \n- **C (00000001 = +1, 10000000 = −128)** also have opposite signs, no overflow expected.  \n- **H (10000001 = −127, 00000001 = +1)** again opposite signs, so no overflow.  \n\nActually, overflow *does* occur in **C** because +1 + (−128) = −127, which fits in 8-bit two's complement (no overflow), so I must re-check.  \n\nChecking carefully: overflow occurs if two positives sum to negative or two negatives sum to positive.  \nOnly **A** has both negative (11110000 = −16, 00001111 = +15) → not same sign. Wait, pair **F**: 11111111 (−1) and 10000001 (−127) → both negative, sum = −128 (10000000), which is still negative → no overflow.  \nActually, the only problematic one is **I**: 10101010 (−86) and 11111111 (−1) → both negative, sum = −87 (within range) → no overflow.  \n\nLet’s test **B**: largest positive (127) + largest negative (−128) = −1 → fine.  \nBut **H**: −127 + 1 = −126 → fine.  \n\nWait — overflow case is pair **A**? No, −16 + 15 = −1, fine.  \n\nHold — the correct case is **C**: +1 + (−128) = −127, but −128 is the most negative, so adding a positive yields a negative within range.  \n\nActually, overflow occurs in two's complement only if the two numbers have the same sign and the result’s sign differs. Among options, **F** (both negatives) gives negative result → no overflow. So none cause overflow? That suggests the answer is “none” but there must be one.  \n\nRe-examine:  \n01111111 (+127) + 10000000 (−128) = −1 → no overflow.  \nBut 01111111 (+127) + 00000001 (+1) = +128 (overflow) — but that’s not an option.  \n\nLet’s test systematically for each option using overflow rule: sign bits are MSB.  \nCarry into MSB ≠ carry out of MSB means overflow.  \n\nOption **B**: sign bits: 0 and 1 → carry in to sign bit = 1 (since 1+0+carry from lower bits? Let’s approximate). Actually 01111111+10000000:  \n01111111  \n10000000  \n=11111111 → carry into sign bit = 1 (since last lower bit 1+0=1 no carry), carry out of sign bit=0+1=1, so carry in=carry out → no overflow.  \n\nOption **C**: 00000001 + 10000000:  \nsign bits: 0+1 → carry out=0+1=1, carry into sign bit from lower bits: 0000001 part: only least bit 1+0=1, no carry to sign bit, so carry into sign bit=0, carry out=1 → overflow! Yes.  \n\nThus, **C** results in overflow.  \n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{C}  \n\nExplanation: Overflow occurs because adding a positive (00000001) to the most negative (10000000) causes a carry-out from the sign bit without a matching carry-in, violating the two’s complement overflow rule.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 198,
        "output_tokens": 812,
        "total_tokens": 1010
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
