    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let’s break this down:  \n\n1. **Statement 1** says: For joint entropy, \\( H[x, y] \\ge H[x] + H[y] \\).  \n   By the property of joint entropy, \\( H[x, y] = H[x] + H[y|x] \\), and since \\( H[y|x] \\ge 0 \\), we actually have \\( H[x, y] \\ge \\max(H[x], H[y]) \\) but not necessarily \\( H[x, y] \\ge H[x] + H[y] \\). In fact, \\( H[x] + H[y] \\) can be larger than \\( H[x, y] \\) when variables are not independent, so the statement is false.  \n\n2. **Statement 2** says: For *some* directed graphs, moralization decreases the number of edges. Moralization connects parents of each node, so it usually *adds* edges, but it’s possible moralization removes none (and never reduces edges unless you count after possible simplification?). Actually, standard moralization only adds edges, never decreases, so for “some graphs” it decreases edges? No, it never decreases; the statement is **false**.  \n\nThus: Statement 1 → **False**, Statement 2 → **False**.  \n\nThat matches option **F**.  \n\n\\boxed{F}",
