    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The grammar $S \\rightarrow (S)$, $S \\rightarrow x$ generates strings of balanced parentheses surrounding the terminal $x$ (e.g., $x$, $(x)$, $((x))$). Since the nesting depth is uniquely determined by the position of the outermost parentheses, there is only one distinct parse tree for any valid string, making the grammar **unambiguous** (Statement I is false).\n\nThis grammar is **LL(1)** because the FIRST sets of the right-hand sides of the productions—$\\{ ( \\}$ for $S \\rightarrow (S)$ and $\\{ x \\}$ for $S \\rightarrow x$—are disjoint, ensuring no conflict for top-down parsing (Statement II is true). Additionally, the grammar is **LR(1)** and free of shift-reduce conflicts, making it suitable for bottom-up parsing as well (Statement III is true).\n\nTherefore, Statements II and III are true.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
