      "generated_answer": "We test validity by assuming the premises are true and the conclusion is false. Assume I ≡ J is false, so I and J have opposite truth values. Suppose I is true and J is false: then from H ≡ (I · ~J), H ≡ (true · true) → H is true. Then K ≡ ~H → K ≡ false. Now check ~(K ⊃ J): K is false, J is false → K ⊃ J is true → ~(K ⊃ J) is false, which contradicts the premise. Try I false, J true: then I ≡ J is false. H ≡ (false · ~true) = false. Then K ≡ ~H → K ≡ true. K ⊃ J = true ⊃ true = true → ~(K ⊃ J) is false again — contradiction. But in option B: I, H, K true, J false → H ≡ (I · ~J) = true ≡ (true · true) = true → ok. K ≡ ~H → true ≡ false → false. So K cannot be true if H is true. So B fails. Try C: I, H true, J, K false. H ≡ (I · ~J) = true ≡ (true · true) = true → ok. K ≡ ~H → false ≡ ~true → false ≡ false → ok. ~(K ⊃ J) = ~(false ⊃ false) = ~(true) = false → not true, so premise false — not a counterexample. Try D: I, H, J false, K true. H ≡ (I · ~J) = false ≡ (false · true) = false ≡ false → ok. K ≡ ~H → true ≡ ~false → true ≡ true → ok. ~(K ⊃ J) = ~(true ⊃ false) = ~(false) = true → premise true. Conclusion I ≡ J: false ≡ false → true. But we need the conclusion to be false for invalidity. So no counterexample works → argument is valid.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
