t \\(N \\supset ∼O\\) in one step is if we had \\(P\\) and premise 2. But we don’t have \\(P\\) from the given unless we assume \\(N\\), but that’s not allowed.  \nHowever, one can get \\(N \\supset ∼O\\) from \\(N \\supset P\\) and \\(P \\supset (N \\supset ∼O)\\) by hypothetical syllogism — which is *one* step of hypothetical syllogism — and that **is** option B. So yes, B is the correct choice because hypothetical syllogism is a single rule in PL.\n\n**Answer:**  \n\\boxed{B}",
