    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_13",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use indirect truth tables to determine whether the following argument is valid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\nE ⊃ (F ∨ ~G)\nF ⊃ (~E ∨ ~G) / ~(E ≡ F)\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when E, F, and G are true\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when E and F are true and G is false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when E, F, and G are false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To evaluate the argument using an indirect truth table, assume the premises are true and the conclusion is false. The conclusion ~(E ≡ F) is false when E and F have the same truth value (both true or both false). Suppose E and F are both true; then from the first premise, F ∨ ~G must be true, which it is regardless of G, and the second premise becomes true as well if G is true (since ~E ∨ ~G would be false, making F ⊃ (~E ∨ ~G) false only if F is true and the consequent false — but if G is true, ~G is false, and ~E is false, so ~E ∨ ~G is false, making the implication false). But if G is false, ~G is true, so ~E ∨ ~G is true, making the second premise true. So if E and F are true and G is false, both premises are true, and the conclusion ~(E ≡ F) is false (since E and F are both true, E ≡ F is true, so its negation is false). Thus, this is a counterexample.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
