      "generated_answer": "First, the argument is:  \n1. \\( P \\supset (Q \\lor R) \\)  \n2. \\( (Q \\cdot S) \\supset {\\sim}P \\)  \n3. \\( {\\sim}({\\sim}Q \\lor R) \\) (equivalent to \\( Q \\cdot {\\sim}R \\) by De Morgan)  \nConclusion: \\( {\\sim}P \\).\n\nFrom premise 3: \\( Q = \\text{T}, R = \\text{F} \\).  \nTry \\( P = \\text{T} \\) to test for possible invalidity.  \nIf \\( P = \\text{T} \\), from premise 1: \\( Q \\lor R \\) must be true, and \\( Q \\lor R \\) is true since \\( Q = \\text{T} \\) (premise 3). So premise 1 is satisfied.  \nFor premise 2: \\( Q \\cdot S \\) means \\( \\text{T} \\cdot S \\) = \\( S \\). So premise 2 says \\( S \\supset {\\sim}P \\), i.e., \\( S \\supset \\text{F} \\), which means \\( S \\) must be false to avoid making premise 2 false. Set \\( S = \\text{F} \\). Then all premises are true with \\( P = \\text{T} \\), \\( Q = \\text{T} \\), \\( R = \\text{F} \\), \\( S = \\text{F} \\), but conclusion \\( {\\sim}P \\) is false.  \n\nThis invalidates the argument, matching option C’s truth values.\n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{C}\\)",
