    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_99",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Construct a complete truth table for the following pairs of propositions. Then, using the truth tables, determine whether the statements are logically equivalent or contradictory. If neither, determine whether they are consistent or inconsistent. Justify your answers.\n(G ∨ ~H) ⊃ G and ~G ≡ (~H · G)\n\nOptions: \nA. Logically equivalent\nB. Contradictory\nC. Neither logically equivalent nor contradictory, but consistent\nD. Inconsistent\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "First, we can evaluate the truth tables for both propositions:\n\nFor \\((G \\lor \\lnot H) \\supset G\\):\n\nIf \\(G\\) is true, this is true; if \\(G\\) is false, \\((G \\lor \\lnot H)\\) is \\(\\lnot H\\), so antecedent is \\(\\lnot H\\) and \\(G\\) false ⇒ \\(F \\supset F\\) is true.  \nWait — careful: actually if \\(G = F\\), \\((G \\lor \\lnot H) = \\lnot H\\). If \\(\\lnot H = T\\) then \\(T \\supset F\\) is false; if \\(\\lnot H = F\\) then \\(F \\supset F\\) is true. So the truth value is false when \\(G=F, H=F\\) (since \\(H=F ⇒ \\lnot H = T\\)), true otherwise.\n\nFor \\(\\lnot G \\equiv (\\lnot H \\cdot G)\\):\n\nIf \\(G=F\\), then \\(\\lnot G = T\\); right side: \\(\\lnot H \\cdot G = \\lnot H \\cdot F = F\\); so \\(T \\equiv F\\) ⇒ false.  \nIf \\(G=T\\), then \\(\\lnot G = F\\); right side: \\(\\lnot H \\cdot T = \\lnot H\\); so \\(F \\equiv \\lnot H\\) ⇒ true when \\(\\lnot H = F\\) (H=T) ⇒ false when H=F ⇒ wait check: \\(F \\equiv T\\) is false, \\(F \\equiv F\\) is true. So only true if \\(G=T, H=T\\) ⇒ truth: T only for (T,T), false otherwise.\n\nCompare: First proposition is false for (F,F), true for (F,T), (T,F), (T,T).  \nSecond is true only for (T,T), false for all others.  \n\nThey are not logically equivalent (different truth values at (F,T) and (T,F)), not contradictory (both true at (T,T)), but they are consistent (both true at (T,T)), so the answer is C.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 154,
        "output_tokens": 443,
        "total_tokens": 597
