    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5811",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the main difference between the Dickey Fuller (DF) and Phillips-Perron (PP) approaches to unit root testing?\n\nOptions: \nA. The DF test incorporates an automatic correction for non-stationary data in the test regression\nB. PP test requires a priori knowledge of the lag length whereas DF test does not\nC. PP tests have good power in small samples whereas DF tests do not.\nD. ADF is a single equation approach to unit root testing while PP is a systems approach\nE. DF test is generally used for larger datasets, while the PP test is used for smaller ones\nF. PP tests assume the presence of unit root under the null hypothesis whereas DF tests do not\nG. PP tests reverse the DF null and alternative hypotheses so that there is stationarity under the null hypothesis of the PP test\nH. DF test requires larger sample size than the PP test for accurate results\nI. The PP test incorporates an automatic correction for autocorrelated residuals in the test regression\nJ. DF test is non-parametric whereas PP test is parametric\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The main difference is that the Phillips-Perron (PP) test adjusts for autocorrelation and heteroskedasticity in the error terms non-parametrically, while the standard Dickey-Fuller (DF) test does not automatically correct for such issues. The PP test uses a correction in the test statistic to account for serial correlation, whereas DF relies on adding lagged differences (as in ADF) for such adjustments. Therefore, the most accurate statement is that the PP test incorporates an automatic correction for autocorrelated residuals.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
