  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6154",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The international value of the dollar will appreciate if\n\nOptions: \nA. American income falls relative to the rest of the world.\nB. American interest rates fall relative to interest rates in other countries.\nC. American prices rise.\nD. foreigners boycott American products.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The international value of the dollar appreciates when demand for dollars increases in foreign exchange markets. Higher U.S. interest rates relative to other countries attract foreign investors, increasing demand for dollars to invest in U.S. assets. Therefore, the dollar appreciates when American interest rates rise relative to others, making option B (which states the opposite) incorrect; however, none of the other options increase dollar demand. Careful analysis shows that if American interest rates *fall*, capital flows out, decreasing demand for dollars—so the dollar depreciates. Thus, the correct answer is the one that, if reversed, would cause appreciation—so the correct choice here must be the *absence* of that depreciation; but among the given, only A could indirectly lead to appreciation if lower income reduces imports and boosts net exports, but that's weak. Actually, the best macroeconomic driver is interest rates: higher rates appreciate currency. So if B says \"fall,\" that would depreciate, so appreciation happens when they *rise*—thus B is the wrong direction. But the question asks when appreciation occurs, so the correct answer must be the one that supports appreciation. Wait—no option says interest rates rise. Let's reevaluate.\n\nActually, if American income falls relative to the rest of the world, U.S. consumers buy fewer imports, reducing supply of dollars in forex markets, which causes the dollar to appreciate. This makes option A correct.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
