      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is \\boxed{I}.\n\nThe restrictive covenants become unenforceable when the neighborhood character undergoes a significant change that renders the restriction inequitable. In this scenario, the rezoning of the area for commercial and industrial uses, combined with the construction of a large shopping center, fundamentally altered the neighborhood from the originally intended single-family residential character. Consequently, enforcing the single-family restriction against a medical practice in a now commercial zone is no longer supported by equity or the remaining intent of the covenantors.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
