      "generated_answer": "The ordinance requires all waste within city limits to be processed locally before leaving the state, which effectively benefits local processors by mandating that all waste—regardless of origin—must be handled at the local facility. This creates an economic advantage for local businesses over out-of-state competitors, constituting discrimination against interstate commerce even if the rule appears neutral on its face. Courts have previously struck down such flow control ordinances under the Dormant Commerce Clause when they favor in-state economic interests.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
