      "generated_answer": "In Proto-Greek, the labiovelar consonant *kʷ (k^w) underwent a sound change before front vowels, where it lost its labialization and became a regular velar stop, eventually palatalizing in some contexts. This sound evolved into the voiceless palatal stop or affricate, which is represented by the Greek letter ζ (zeta) in certain environments, though more commonly *kʷ before front vowels developed into /t/ or /ts/. However, the most direct reflex in standard accounts is that *kʷ before *e or *i led to /p/ > /t/ via Grassmann’s Law and other changes, but the letter most associated with the outcome of palatalized *kʷ is ζ.\n\n\\boxed{ζ}",
