      "generated_answer": "We are given a three-digit positive integer $\\underline{a}\\underline{b}\\underline{c}$ in base 10, which equals the base-nine number $\\underline{b}\\underline{c}\\underline{a}_\\text{nine}$.  \nThis means:  \n$$\n100a + 10b + c = 81b + 9c + a\n$$\nRearranging:\n$$\n99a - 71b - 8c = 0\n$$\nTrying small digit values, we find $a = 2$, $b = 3$, $c = 9$ works:  \n$$\n99(2) = 198,\\quad 71(3) + 8(9) = 213 + 72 = 285?\\quad \\text{No.}\n$$\nWait — let's solve more carefully. Rearranged:\n$$\n99a = 71b + 8c\n$$\nTry $a = 4$: $99 \\times 4 = 396$, so $71b + 8c = 396$. Try $b = 5$: $71×5=355$, $396-355=41$, $8c=41$ → no.  \n$b=4$: $71×4=284$, $396-284=112$, $c=14$ → invalid.  \n$a=3$: $99×3=297$. Try $b=3$: $71×3=213$, $297-213=84$, $8c=84$ → $c=10.5$.  \n$b=4$: $71×4=284$, $297-284=13$, $8c=13$ → no.  \n$b=2$: $142$, $297-142=155$, too big for $8c$.  \nTry $a = 5$: $99×5 = 495$. Try $b=5$: $355$, $495-355=140 > 72$.  \n$b=6$: $71×6=426$, $495-426=69$, $8c=69$ → no.  \n$b=7$: $497 > 495$.  \nTry $a = 1$: $99$. Try $b=1$: $71$, $99-71=28$, $8c=28$ → $c=3.5$.  \n$b=0$: $0$, $99 = 8c$ → $c=12.375$.  \nTry $a = 6$: $99×6 = 594$.  \nTry $b=7$: $71×7=497$, $594-497=97$, $8c=97$ → $c=12.125$.  \n$b=8$: $568$, $594-568=26$, $8c=26$ → $c=3.25$.  \n$b=6$: $426$, $594-426=168 > 72$ → too big.  \nWait — maybe try $a=4$: $99×4=396$.  \nTry $b=4$: $284$, $396-284=112$, $c=14$ — no.  \n$b=5$: $355$, $396-355=41$, $8c=41$ → no.  \n$b=2$: $142$, $396-142=254$ — too big.  \nWait — perhaps $a = 2$: $198$.  \n$b=2$: $142$, $198-142=56$, $8c=56$ → $c=7$.  \nSo $a=2$, $b=2$, $c=7$.  \nCheck: base-10 number is $100×2 + 10×2 + 7 = 227$.  \nBase-nine number $bc a_9 = 2\\,7\\,2_9 = 2×81 + 7×9 + 2 = 162 + 63 + 2 = 227$. Yes!  \nThus, the number is $\\boxed{227}$.",
