    "global index": "AIME_15",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Adults made up $\\frac5{{12}}$ of the crowd of people at a concert. After a bus carrying $50$ more people arrived, adults made up $\\frac{{11}}{{25}}$ of the people at the concert. Find the minimum number of adults who could have been at the concert after the bus arrived.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let the initial number of people at the concert be $ x $. Then the number of adults initially is $ \\frac{5}{12}x $. After a bus with 50 more people arrives, the total number of people becomes $ x + 50 $, and adults make up $ \\frac{11}{25}(x + 50) $. Assuming no adults got off or on the bus, the number of adults remains $ \\frac{5}{12}x $, so we set:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{12}x = \\frac{11}{25}(x + 50)\n$$\n\nMultiply both sides by $ 300 $ (LCM of 12 and 25):\n\n$$\n125x = 132(x + 50) \\Rightarrow 125x = 132x + 6600 \\Rightarrow -7x = 6600 \\Rightarrow x = -\\frac{6600}{7}\n$$\n\nThis is negative, which is impossible, so our assumption that only adults were on the bus is invalid. Instead, let the 50 new people include $ a $ adults and $ 50 - a $ children. Then total adults becomes $ \\frac{5}{12}x + a $, and total people is $ x + 50 $, so:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\frac{5}{12}x + a}{x + 50} = \\frac{11}{25}\n$$\n\nMultiply both sides by $ 25(x + 50) $:\n\n$$\n25\\left(\\frac{5}{12}x + a\\right) = 11(x + 50) \\Rightarrow \\frac{125}{12}x + 25a = 11x + 550\n$$\n\nMultiply through by 12:\n\n$$\n125x + 300a = 132x + 6600 \\Rightarrow 300a = 7x + 6600\n\\Rightarrow 7x = 300a - 6600\n\\Rightarrow x = \\frac{300a - 6600}{7}\n$$\n\nWe need $ x $ to be a positive integer, so $ 300a - 6600 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7} $. Compute modulo 7:\n\n$ 300 \\equiv 300 - 294 = 6 \\pmod{7} $, $ 6600 \\div 7 = 942 \\times 7 = 6594 $, so $ 6600 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} $\n\nSo $ 6a - 6 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7} \\Rightarrow 6a \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\Rightarrow a \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} $\n\nSo $ a = 7k + 1 $, and $ a $ must be between 0 and 50. Try smallest $ a = 1 $:\n\n$ x = \\frac{300(1) - 6600}{7} = \\frac{-6300}{7} = -900 $: invalid\n\nTry $ a = 8 $: $ x = \\frac{300(8) - 6600}{7} = \\frac{2400 - 6600}{7} = \\frac{-4200}{7} = -600 $: invalid\n\nTry $ a = 15 $: $ x = \\frac{4500 - 6600}{7} = \\frac{-2100}{7} = -300 $\n\nTry $ a = 22 $: $ x = \\frac{6600 - 6600}{7} = 0 $: invalid (no initial people)\n\nTry $ a = 29 $: $ x = \\frac{300(29) - 6600}{7} = \\frac{8700 - 6600}{7} = \\frac{2100}{7} = 300 $\n\nValid! $ x = 300 $. Initial adults: $ \\frac{5}{12} \\times 300 = 125 $. Additional adults: $ a = 29 $. Total adults after: $ 125 + 29 = 154 $\n\nCheck: total people now $ 300 + 50 = 350 $. $ \\frac{154}{350} = \\frac{77}{175} = \\frac{11}{25} $ ✓\n\nIs this minimal? Try next $ a = 29 - 7 = 22 $: gave $ x = 0 $, invalid. So $ a = 29 $ gives smallest valid number.\n\nSo minimum number of adults after arrival is $ \\boxed{154} $",
