      "generated_answer": "We simplify each term in the product using the identity $\\log_a(b^c) = c \\log_a b$ and the change of base formula $\\log_a b = \\frac{\\log b}{\\log a}$. Each term becomes:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\log_k(5^{k^2 - 1})}{\\log_{k+1}(5^{k^2 - 4})} = \\frac{(k^2 - 1) \\cdot \\log_k 5}{(k^2 - 4) \\cdot \\log_{k+1} 5} = \\frac{k^2 - 1}{k^2 - 4} \\cdot \\frac{\\log 5 / \\log k}{\\log 5 / \\log (k+1)} = \\frac{k^2 - 1}{k^2 - 4} \\cdot \\frac{\\log(k+1)}{\\log k}\n$$\n\nNote that $k^2 - 1 = (k-1)(k+1)$ and $k^2 - 4 = (k-2)(k+2)$, so:\n\n$$\n\\frac{k^2 - 1}{k^2 - 4} = \\frac{(k-1)(k+1)}{(k-2)(k+2)}\n$$\n\nThus the term becomes:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(k-1)(k+1)}{(k-2)(k+2)} \\cdot \\frac{\\log(k+1)}{\\log k}\n$$\n\nBut this approach gets messy. Let's instead use direct logarithmic simplifications:\n\n$$\n\\log_k(5^{k^2 - 1}) = \\frac{\\ln 5^{k^2 - 1}}{\\ln k} = \\frac{(k^2 - 1)\\ln 5}{\\ln k}, \\quad \\log_{k+1}(5^{k^2 - 4}) = \\frac{(k^2 - 4)\\ln 5}{\\ln(k+1)}\n$$\n\nSo the ratio is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(k^2 - 1)\\ln 5 / \\ln k}{(k^2 - 4)\\ln 5 / \\ln(k+1)} = \\frac{k^2 - 1}{k^2 - 4} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln(k+1)}{\\ln k}\n= \\frac{(k-1)(k+1)}{(k-2)(k+2)} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln(k+1)}{\\ln k}\n$$\n\nThis still looks complicated. Let's instead test telescoping via logarithmic identities.\n\nBut here's a better idea: use $\\log_a b = \\frac{1}{\\log_b a}$ and simplify numerator and denominator in terms of $\\log 5$.\n\nWait — observe the structure:\n\n$$\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{\\log_k(5^{k^2 - 1})}{\\log_{k+1}(5^{k^2 - 4})}\n= \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\left( \\frac{k^2 - 1}{\\log_k 5} \\div \\frac{k^2 - 4}{\\log_{k+1} 5} \\right)\n= \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k^2 - 1}{k^2 - 4} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_{k+1} 5}{\\log_k 5}\n$$\n\nBut $\\log_{k+1} 5 / \\log_k 5 = \\frac{\\ln 5 / \\ln(k+1)}{\\ln 5 / \\ln k} = \\frac{\\ln k}{\\ln(k+1)}$\n\nSo the term becomes:\n\n$$\n\\frac{k^2 - 1}{k^2 - 4} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln k}{\\ln(k+1)} = \\frac{(k-1)(k+1)}{(k-2)(k+2)} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln k}{\\ln(k+1)}\n$$\n\nNow the full product is:\n\n$$\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\left( \\frac{(k-1)(k+1)}{(k-2)(k+2)} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln k}{\\ln(k+1)} \\right)\n= \\left( \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k-1)(k+1)}{(k-2)(k+2)} \\right) \\left( \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{\\ln k}{\\ln(k+1)} \\right)\n$$\n\nNow split into two products:\n\nFirst: telescoping logarithmic part:\n\n$$\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{\\ln k}{\\ln(k+1)} = \\frac{\\ln 4}{\\ln 5} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln 5}{\\ln 6} \\cdots \\frac{\\ln 63}{\\ln 64} = \\frac{\\ln 4}{\\ln 64}\n$$\n\nSecond: rational function product:\n\n$$\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k-1)(k+1)}{(k-2)(k+2)} = \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k-1}{k-2} \\cdot \\frac{k+1}{k+2}\n= \\left( \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k-1}{k-2} \\right) \\left( \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k+1}{k+2} \\right)\n$$\n\nFirst piece: $\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k-1}{k-2} = \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\cdots \\frac{62}{61}$ — telescopes to $\\frac{62}{2} = 31$\n\nSecond piece: $\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k+1}{k+2} = \\frac{5}{6} \\cdot \\frac{6}{7} \\cdot \\frac{7}{8} \\cdots \\frac{64}{65}$ — telescopes to $\\frac{5}{65} = \\frac{1}{13}$\n\nSo total rational part: $31 \\cdot \\frac{1}{13} = \\frac{31}{13}$\n\nLogarithmic part: $\\frac{\\ln 4}{\\ln 64} = \\log_{64} 4$\n\nBut wait — we have:\n\nOverall product = $\\frac{31}{13} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln 4}{\\ln 64} = \\frac{31}{13} \\cdot \\log_{64} 4$\n\nNow $\\log_{64} 4 = \\frac{\\log 4}{\\log 64} = \\frac{\\log 2^2}{\\log 2^6} = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}$\n\nSo total product:\n\n$$\n\\frac{31}{13} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{31}{39}\n$$\n\nBut 31 and 39 are relatively prime (31 is prime, doesn’t divide 39), so $m = 31$, $n = 39$, $m+n = 70$\n\n\\boxed{70}",
