      "generated_answer": "We are to place white and black chips on a $5 \\times 5$ grid under three constraints: (1) each cell has at most one chip, (2) all chips in the same row or column must be the same color, and (3) the configuration is maximal (no more chips can be added without violating 1 or 2). Since each row and column must be monochromatic if it contains chips, and we cannot add any more chips, every row and every column must already contain chips (otherwise we could place a chip of any color in an empty row or column, contradicting maximality). Thus, every row and column has at least one chip, and all chips in a given row or column are the same color. The only way this is possible is if each row is filled entirely with one color, and each column is filled entirely with one color, but since entries are single chips, and rows/columns must be monochromatic, and there is no contradiction — but actually, since each row is monochromatic and every cell can hold at most one chip, maximality implies that every row must be completely filled (otherwise we could place a chip in an empty cell of that row, provided it's consistent), but wait — not necessarily completely filled, just that no further chip can be added.\n\nBut let's re-analyze: the condition says \"any additional chip would violate\", so the configuration is maximal. Each row that has chips must have all its chips the same color, and same for each column.\n\nSuppose a row has some chips. All must be the same color, say color $C$. Then, any empty cell in that row could potentially have a chip of color $C$ added, unless it would violate the column condition. But for the configuration to be maximal, no such placement must be possible. So, for every empty cell in a row with chips, the corresponding column must already contain chips of the opposite color (or same row/column constraints block it). But more simply, consider that if a row has at least one chip, then all chips in the row must be same color. Simi