ite or black (based on the color of its chips).\n- Columns are assigned colors: each column is either white or black.\n\nNow, consider a cell $(i,j)$:\n- If it is filled, it must be with a chip of color equal to both row $i$'s color and column $j$'s color, so row $i$ color = column $j$ color = chip color.\n- If it is empty, to be non-placable, we must have row $i$ color ≠ column $j$ color.\n\nTherefore, a cell $(i,j)$ can have a chip if and only if row $i$ color = column $j$ color.\n\nAnd in that case, the chip must be of that color.\n\nMoreover, since each row must have at least one chip, for each row $i$, there must be at least one column $j$ such that row $i$ color = column $j$ color, and cell $(i,j)$ is filled.\n\nBut since the chip is placed only when colors match, and we can choose to place or not, but the number of chips is determined by the positions where colors match.\n\nWait: actually, for positions where row $i$ color = column $j$ color, we *could* place a chip, but do we have to? No, we could leave it empty. But if we leave it empty, and row $i$ and column $j$ have the same color, then we *could* place a chip there, since it would match both row and column color — contradicting maximality.\n\nTherefore, maximality forces that **every cell where row $i$ and column $j$ have the same color must be filled**.\n\nAnd **no cell where colors differ can be filled**, because that would violate the row or column monochromaticity.\n\nMoreover, for each row, since it must have at least one chip, there must be at least one column $j$ such that row $i$ color = column $j$ color.\n\nSimilarly for each column.\n\nSo, summarize:\n- Assign a color to each row: $r_i \\in \\{W,B\\}$ for $i=1,\\dots,5$.\n- Assign a color to each column: $c_j \\in \\{W,B\\}$ for $j=1,\\dots,5$.\n- A cell $(i,j)$ has a chip if and only if $r_i = c_j$.\n- The chip is of that color.\n- Additionally, for every row $i$, there must exist at least one $j$ such that $r_i = c_j$ (so that the row has at