43350000=1862177, 289*6440=1861160, remainder 1017, 289*3=867 remainder 150, so not divisible. So I made a mistake—I recall the actual known smallest \\( n \\) for \\( 17^2 \\mid n^4+1 \\) is \\( n=38 \\):  \nCheck \\( 38^4 = 38^2=1444, 1444^2=2085136 \\), plus 1 = 2085137. Divide by 289: 289*7215=2085135, remainder 2. So no.  \n\nActually, it turns out the minimal \\( n \\) for \\( p=17 \\) is \\( n=110 \\):  \nCheck: \\( 110^4+1 = 146410000+1 = 146410001 \\). Divide by 289: 289*506610=146410000? Wait 289*506610=146409290, remainder 711, so no.  \n\nInstead of guesswork, recall a known problem from math contests: the answer is \\( n=240 \\). Let’s verify: \\( 240^4 = (240^2)^2 = 57600^2 = 3317760000 \\), plus 1 = 3317760001. Divide by 289: 289*11480000=3317720000, remainder 40001, no. So not matching.  \n\nGiven complexity, known solution: smallest \\( p=17 \\), smallest \\( m \\) for \\( 17^2 \\mid m^4+1 \\) is \\( 38 \\) but fails earlier, so maybe \\( 70 \\) fails, but known answer: The minimal \\( n \\) is 240. Let’s check 240 mod 289:  \nFirst, 240^2=57600, 57600 mod 289: 289*199=289*200-289=57800-289=57511, remainder 89. So 240^2 ≡ 89 mod 289. Then 89^2=7921, 7921 mod 289: 289*27=7803, remainder 118. So 240^4 ≡ 118, plus 1=119, not 0. So 240 fails.  \n\nGiven the time, well-known fact: For \\( p=17 \\), \\( 17^2 \\mid 38^4+1 \\) is false. Instead, \\( n=70 \\) fails. From known number theory, minimal \\( m \\) is \\( 82 \\). Check 82^4 mod 289:  \n82^2=6724, 6724 mod 289: 289*23=6647, remainder 77.  \n77^2=5929, mod 289: 289*20=5780, remainder 149. 149+1=150 not 0. So fails.  \n\nThus by actual calculation: the minimal \\( m \\) satisfying \\( m^4+1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{289} \\) is \\( m = 110 \\)? No.  \nLet's compute systematically in mod 289:  \nWe want \\( m^4 \\equiv -1 \\). In mod 17, solutions: m ≡ 2, 8, 9, 15 mod 17. We check each mod 289 by lifting.  \n\nKnown from Euler: minimal m is 110: Test 110:  \n110 mod 289=110. 110^2=12100, 12100 mod 289: 289*41=