instead, place the pair $(k,k+1)$, require $a_{k-1} = 0$ (if $k>1$) and $a_{k+2} = 0$ (if $k<8$), and elsewhere the 1s must be isolated.\n\nUse this method: For each $k = 1$ to $9$, define the number of subsets where the only consecutive pair is $(k,k+1)$. Then, to prevent extra consecutive pairs, we must have:\n- $k-1 \\notin S$ if $k \\geq 2$,\n- $k+2 \\notin S$ if $k \\leq 8$,\n- And in $S \\setminus \\{k,k+1\\}$, no two elements are consecutive, and they are not adjacent to the pair.\n\nSo, remove from $\\{1,\\ldots,10\\}$ the elements: $k, k+1$, and exclude $k-1$ and $k+2$ (they cannot be in the subset), and then choose any subset of the remaining integers (from the rest) such that no two are consecutive.\n\nLet $A = \\{1,2,\\ldots,10\\} \\setminus \\{k-1, k, k+1, k+2\\}$, with understanding that if $k=1$, then $k-1=0$ is ignored, and $k+2=3$, so remove $\\{1,2,3\\}$; similarly for $k=9$, remove $\\{8,9,10\\}$. So the excluded set is the pair and its adjacent values.\n\nThen the number of ways to choose additional elements is the number of subsets of the remaining set with no two consecutive integers.\n\nNow, the remaining elements are in two disconnected intervals: left of $k-1$ (i.e., $1$ to $k-2$) and right of $k+2$ (i.e., $k+3$ to $10$). Since they are separated by at least one gap, we can compute the number of non-consecutive subsets for each interval and multiply.\n\nLet $L = \\{1, 2, \\ldots, k-2\\}$, $R = \\{k+3, k+4, \\ldots, 10\\}$.\n\nLet $f(n)$ = number of subsets of $\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$ with no two consecutive elements = $F_{n+2}$, where $F_n$ is Fibonacci, with $f(0) = 1$ (empty), $f(1) = 2$ ($\\emptyset$, $\\{1\\}$), $f(2) = 3$, and $f(n) = f(n-1) + f(n-2)$. But more precisely, this is the number of independent sets in a path of $n$ nodes.\n\nWe define $f(n)$ = number of subsets of $\\{1,2,\\ldots,n\\}$ with no two consecutive = $f(n) = f(n-1) + f(n-2)$, $f(0)=1$, $f(1)=2$, so $f(2)=3$, $f(3)=5$, $f(4)=8$, $f(5)=13$, $f(6)=21$, $f(7)=34$, $f(8)=55