m=r-2\\) are the two \\(m\\)'s; we need only one \\(m\\neq 2\\) ⇒ so \\(r-2 \\neq 2 \\Rightarrow r\\neq 4\\) we already have, but also \\(r-2 = 2\\) would give \\(r=4\\), no. So fine, \\(r\\neq 4\\).  \n\nBut for uniqueness of \\(m\\neq 2\\), there must be exactly one integer \\(t\\) solving quadratic? Wait, two roots: \\(t=4\\) and \\(t=r\\). We want exactly one integer \\(m\\neq 2\\) ⇒ \\(m=r-2\\) must be integer, it is, but we have two \\(m\\)'s: \\(m=2\\) (excluded) and \\(m=r-2\\). That’s exactly one \\(m\\neq 2\\), yes! So allowed.  \n\nSo \\(r\\in[-4,6]\\) integer, \\(r\\neq 4\\). That’s \\(11\\) integers (\\(-4\\) to \\(6\\) has 11 numbers, remove 4, leaves 10?).  \n\nCount: \\(-4,-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,5,6\\) → that’s 10 possibilities. Each gives \\((a,b)\\) from formulas, \\(c\\) free in 41 possibilities.\n\nSo Case 1 count: \\(10 \\times 41 = 410\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case 2:** Quadratic has exactly one integer root \\(t\\), other root not integer (and \\(t\\neq 4\\)).\n\nFor quadratic with integer coefficients \\(t^2 + (a-2)t + (b+4) = 0\\), if it has exactly one integer root, discriminant \\(D = (a-2)^2 - 4(b+4)\\) must be a perfect square \\(k^2\\), \\(k\\ge 0\\). Then root \\(t = \\frac{-(a-2) \\pm k}{2}\\).\n\nFor exactly one integer root: one root integer, one not integer ⇒ \\(k>0\\) integer and \\(a-2\\) parity? Both roots \\(( - (a-2) \\pm k)/2\\). If \\(k\\) odd, one root integer, other not integer? Check parity: \\(a-2\\) and \\(k\\) same parity → both roots integer; different parity → exactly one integer root? Let’s test: sum \\(S = 2-a\\) integer, so \\(a\\) integer, \\(S\\) integer. Roots = \\(\\frac{S \\pm k}{2}\\). If \\(k\\) even, \\(S \\pm k\\) even, both integer roots. If \\(k\\) odd, \\(S \\pm k\\) odd/even? \\(S\\) integer: \\(S\\) and \\(k\\) different parity ⇒ one root integer, one root half-integer not integer. Exactly one integer root.\n\nSo \\(D=k^2\\) with \\(k\\) odd positive integer.\n\nAlso \\(t=\\frac{S - k}{2}\\) or \\(\\frac{S + k}{2}\\) is the integer root, must be \\(\\neq 4\\).\n\nCheck both possibilities: We just need that the integer root \\(t_0\\) not equal 4.\n\nAlso must avoid Case 1: so not two integer roots ⇒ \\(k\\) odd.\n\n---\n\nGiven \\(a,b\\) integers in \\([-20,20]\\):\n\n\\(D = (a-2)^2 - 4(b+4) = k^2 \\Rightarrow 4(b+4) = (a-2)^2 - k^2 = (a-2-k)(a-2+k)\\).\n\nSo \\(b+4 = \\frac{(a-2-k)(a-2+k)}{4}\\) must be integer ⇒ \\((a-2-k)(a-2+k)\\) divisible by 4.\n\nSince \\(k\\) odd, \\(a-2\\) integer ⇒ \\(a-2-k\\) and \\(a-2+k\\) both odd or both even? They differ by \\(2k\\) (even), so same parity. If both odd, product odd, not divisible by 4 ⇒ no. So both even, product divisible by 4, ok.\n\nSo \\(a-2\\) even ⇒ \\(a\\) even.\n\nLet \\(a=2A\\) even, \\(a-2=2A-2=2(A-1)\\) even.\n\nThen \\(a-2 \\pm k = 2(A-1) \\pm k\\) is even? \\(k\\) odd ⇒ even ± odd = odd, so not both even. Contradiction? Wait earlier said both even ⇒ impossible since k odd. So correction: if k odd, a-2 even ⇒ a-2 ± k odd ⇒ product odd ⇒ not divisible by 4 ⇒ b not integer. So k must be even for integer b?  \n\nBut k even ⇒ both roots integer if S,k same parity. S=2-a integer. If k even: S ± k both even/odd same as S. If S even, both integer; if S odd, both half-integers not integer ⇒ no integer roots.  \n\nActually if both roots non-integer, not counted. We want exactly one integer root ⇒ impossible with integer coeffs quadratic unless discriminant is perfect square k² and S,k different parity, which means k odd.\n\nBut k odd ⇒ a-2 ± k have different parity: even±odd=odd, odd±odd=even. But a-2 parity?  \n\nLet’s check parity systematically: Let p = a-2 parity. For exactly one integer root, S,k diff parity ⇒ p opposite to k parity. k odd ⇒ p even ⇒ a even. Then a-2 even, ±k odd ⇒ a-2±k odd ⇒ both odd ⇒ product odd ⇒ b+4 = odd/4 not integer. So no solutions.  \n\nThus impossible to have integer a,b,k odd>0.  \n\nSo exactly one integer root possible? Actually known fact: quadratic with integer coefficients, if discriminant perfect square, both roots integers. So can’t have exactly one integer root unless other root infinite? Nope. Conclusion: For integer coefficients, if one root integer, other root integer (since sum and one root integer ⇒ other integer).  \n\nBut our earlier factorization m-2 times quadratic: quadratic has integer m means integer t = m+2. If m integer root, t integer, so integer root of quadratic. For exactly one m≠2 integer solution, means quadratic has exactly one integer t, not two integer t’s. But integer coefficients ⇒ if one integer root, other root integer. Contradiction unless other root = 4? Wait 4 is integer. So only way exactly one integer t≠4: quadratic has double root t=4 (two equal integer roots), but then only m=2, no m≠2. Not allowed.  \n\nSo our earlier Case 1 is the only possible case: must have two integer roots, one t=4, other t≠4.  \n\nThus only possibility: discriminant D perfect square (k even) and t=4 is a root. That’s already counted in Case 1.  \n\nSo no Case 2 solutions.\n\n---\n\n**Case 3:** Quadratic has no integer roots ⇒ m integer satisfying it means t integer root, so impossible except maybe quadratic identity zero? Then all m work, not allowed. So no.\n\nSo only Case 1.\n\nThus for each r in {-4,-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,5,6} we have unique (a,b), c free in 41 possibilities: c in [-20,20] has 41 integers.\n\nAlso check if r=4? That’s double root 4 ⇒ m=2 only ⇒ no m≠2 ⇒ invalid. Correct.  \n\nAlso r integer outside [-4,6] not possible because b out of range.\n\nWe must ensure a,b within [-20,20] for r in [-4,6] r≠4.\n\nCheck a = -2 - r: smallest when r=6: a=-8, largest when r=-4: a=2, so fine.  \n\nb=4r-4: smallest r=-4: b=-20, largest r=6: b=20, within range.\n\n---\n\nThus number of triples (a,b,c) = 10×41=410.\n\n---\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{410}\n\\]",
