      "generated_answer": "We need positive integers \\(n\\) such that \\(n+2\\) divides \\(3(n+3)(n^2+9)\\).\n\nWrite \\(3(n+3)(n^2+9) \\equiv 0 \\mod (n+2)\\). Substitute \\(n \\equiv -2\\) into the expression modulo \\(n+2\\).\n\nSubstituting \\(n = -2\\) gives:  \n\\(3( (-2)+3)( (-2)^2 + 9) = 3(1)(4+9) = 3 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 13 = 39\\).  \nThus \\(n+2\\) must divide \\(39\\). Positive divisors of \\(39\\) are \\(1,3,13,39\\), so \\(n+2\\) equals one of these.\n\nThus \\(n\\) can be \\(1, 11, 37\\) (since \\(n+2=3,13,39\\) respectively). Sum: \\(1+11+37=49\\).\n\nFinal answer: \\(\\boxed{49}\\)",
