      "generated_answer": "We factor the equation as \\(12x^2 - xy - 6y^2 = 0\\).  \nTreating it as a quadratic in \\(x\\), the discriminant is \\( (-y)^2 - 4(12)(-6y^2) = y^2 + 288y^2 = 289y^2\\), a perfect square.  \nThus \\( x = \\frac{y \\pm \\sqrt{289y^2}}{24} = \\frac{y \\pm 17|y|}{24} \\).\n\nFor \\(y \\ge 0\\):  \n- \\(x = \\frac{y + 17y}{24} = \\frac{18y}{24} = \\frac{3y}{4}\\) → \\(y\\) a multiple of 4: \\(y = 0,4,8,\\dots,100\\) → \\(26\\) values.  \n- \\(x = \\frac{y - 17y}{24} = \\frac{-16y}{24} = -\\frac{2y}{3}\\) → \\(y\\) a multiple of 3: \\(y = 0,3,6,\\dots,99\\) → \\(34\\) values.  \n\nFor \\(y < 0\\), set \\(Y=-y>0\\):  \n- \\(x = \\frac{-Y + 17Y}{24} = \\frac{16Y}{24} = \\frac{2Y}{3}\\) → \\(Y\\) a multiple of 3: \\(Y = 3,6,\\dots,99\\) → \\(33\\) values (excluding \\(Y=0\\) already counted in \\(y \\ge 0\\) case).  \n- \\(x = \\frac{-Y - 17Y}{24} = \\frac{-18Y}{24} = -\\frac{3Y}{4}\\) → \\(Y\\) a multiple of 4: \\(Y = 4,8,\\dots,100\\) → \\(25\\) values (excluding \\(Y=0\\)).  \n\nAll \\(y\\) integers \\(-100 \\le y \\le 100\\):  \n\nFor \\(y = 0\\), both formulas give \\(x=0\\) → 1 ordered pair.  \nFor \\(y > 0\\): Multiples of 4 (\\(26\\) values) yield \\(x = 3y/4\\) integer; multiples of 3 (\\(34\\) values) yield \\(x = -2y/3\\) integer. The intersection \\(y\\) multiple of 12 (\\(0,12,\\dots,96\\) → 9 values) were double-counted, so total \\(y>0\\) solutions: \\(26 + 34 - 9 = 51\\) (but here, \\(y=0\\) already counted separately). Wait, need careful counting:  \n\nBetter:  \n\nCase 1: \\(y\\) multiple of 4, \\(y\\ge 0\\): \\(y=0,4,8,\\dots,100\\) → 26 values, each gives one \\(x\\) (except \\(y=0\\) counted separately in both). For \\(y=0\\), \\(x=0\\) is one solution.  \n\nWe should split \\(y=0\\) separate:  \n\nSolutions for \\(y>0\\):  \n(i) \\(y\\) multiple of 4: \\(y=4k\\), \\(1 \\le k \\le 25\\): \\(x=3k\\) → 25 pairs.  \n(ii) \\(y\\) multiple of 3: \\(y=3m\\), \\(1 \\le m \\le 33\\): \\(x=-2m\\) → 33 pairs.  \nMultiples of 12: \\(y=12n\\), \\(1 \\le n \\le 8\\) appear in both (i) and (ii) but in (i) gives \\(x=9n\\), in (ii) gives \\(x=-8n\\) — different \\(x\\), so no double-count for different \\(y\\) as each \\(y\\) is fixed. Actually we just list ordered pairs \\((x,y)\\); for each \\(y>0\\), there are 1 or 2 \\(x\\) possible. Let’s check discriminant: \\(x = \\frac{y \\pm 17y}{24}\\) → gives two rational candidates. Both integers?  \n\nCheck \\(y=12\\): \\(x = (12+204)/24=9\\), \\(x=(12-204)/24=-8\\). Both integers. Yes, for \\(y\\) multiple of 12, both formulas give integer \\(x\\) (two solutions per \\(y>0\\), \\(y\\) multiple of 12). For \\(y\\) multiple of 4 but not 3: only \\(x=3y/4\\) integer, \\(x=-2y/3\\) not integer (since \\(y\\) not multiple of 3). Similarly for \\(y\\) multiple of 3 but not 4: only \\(x=-2y/3\\) integer, other not.  \n\nSo count:  \n\nFor \\(y>0\\):  \nLet \\(A\\) = multiples of 4: \\(4,8,\\dots,100\\) → 25 values.  \nLet \\(B\\) = multiples of 3: \\(3,6,\\dots,99\\) → 33 values.  \nIntersection \\(C\\) = multiples of 12: \\(12,24,\\dots,96\\) → 8 values.  \n\nTotal \\(y>0\\) solutions: \\(|A\\setminus C| + |B\\setminus C| + 2|C| = (25-8)+(33-8)+2\\times 8 = 17 + 25 + 16 = 58\\) ordered pairs.  \n\nFor \\(y<0\\): Let \\(y = -Y\\), \\(Y>0\\) integer \\(1 \\le Y \\le 100\\).  \n\\(x = \\frac{-Y \\pm 17Y}{24}\\): \\(+\\) sign: \\(x=16Y/24=2Y/3\\) integer if \\(Y\\) multiple of 3.  \n\\(-\\) sign: \\(x=-18Y/24=-3Y/4\\) integer if \\(Y\\) multiple of 4.  \n\nLet \\(A'\\) = multiples of 4 among \\(Y=1..100\\): \\(4,8,\\dots,100\\) → 25 values.  \nLet \\(B'\\) = multiples of 3 among \\(Y=1..100\\): \\(3,6,\\dots,99\\) → 33 values.  \nIntersection \\(C'\\) = multiples of 12: \\(12,24,\\dots,96\\) → 8 values.  \n\nSo for \\(Y>0\\): \\(Y \\in A'\\) → one solution \\((x=-3Y/4, y=-Y)\\).  \n\\(Y \\in B'\\) → one solution \\((x=2Y/3, y=-Y)\\).  \n\\(Y \\in C'\\) → both formulas integer → 2 solutions.  \n\nThus total \\(y<0\\) solutions: same formula: \\(25+33-8 + 8 = 58\\) pairs.  \n\nAdd \\(y=0\\) case: \\(x=0\\) → 1 pair.  \n\nTotal = \\(58+58+1 = 117\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{117}\n\\]",
