    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We have the system:\n\n1. \\(\\log_6 (x-y) + \\log_6 (x+y) = 2\\)  \nUsing the product rule: \\(\\log_6 ((x-y)(x+y)) = 2\\)  \nSo \\((x-y)(x+y) = x^2 - y^2 = 6^2 = 36\\).\n\n2. \\(\\log_y (5x) = 2\\)  \nSo \\(5x = y^2\\), i.e., \\(y^2 = 5x\\).\n\nSubstitute \\(y^2 = 5x\\) into \\(x^2 - y^2 = 36\\):  \n\\(x^2 - 5x = 36\\)  \n\\(x^2 - 5x - 36 = 0\\)  \n\\((x - 9)(x + 4) = 0\\)  \nThus \\(x = 9\\) or \\(x = -4\\).\n\nSince \\(y^2 = 5x > 0\\) and \\(x-y>0\\) for the logs in the first equation to be defined in real numbers, \\(x\\) must be positive and \\(x > y > 0\\). Only \\(x=9\\) works (checking: \\(y^2 = 45 \\Rightarrow y = 3\\sqrt{5}\\), then \\(x > y\\) and \\(x-y>0\\), yes).\n\nFinal answer: \\(\\boxed{9}\\)",
